// Philodyssey seminars: discussion decks that double as solo lessons. Generated by build_seminars.py.
window.ATLAS_SEMINARS = [{"id": "lying", "title": "Can a Lie Ever Be Right?", "sub": "Kant, Constant and the murderer at the door", "question": "Is it ever right to lie?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can explain why Kant and Augustine forbid every lie, state the best consequentialist and virtue answers, and decide whether misleading is any better than lying.", "cast": ["kant", "constant", "augustine", "aquinas", "aristotle", "mill", "korsgaard"], "related": {"agora": "lying", "islands": ["kant", "augustine", "nicomachean", "mill"], "compass": ["e4"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "A knock at the door", "text": "It is late. Your friend bursts in, pale and shaking: a man with a knife is after him. You hide him in the back room. A minute later there is a knock. A calm, polite stranger stands on the step. “Good evening. Is your friend here? I need a word with him.” You can see the blade under his coat. He is waiting for your answer. You have one second. The obvious thing is to say: “No, he went the other way.” In 1797 one of the greatest philosophers who ever lived said that would be wrong.", "note": "Read this aloud slowly, then pause. Don’t let anyone answer yet: the opening poll comes next."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The murderer at the door", "min": 12}, {"title": "Why truth matters so much", "min": 10}, {"title": "Lies that help", "min": 10}, {"title": "Lying vs misleading", "min": 8}, {"title": "Attack the arguments", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Is it ever right to lie?", "options": ["Never: every lie is wrong", "Rarely: only to prevent serious harm", "Often: small kind lies are fine too", "It depends entirely on the consequences"], "reveal": "Many people pick the middle options. The strictest answer, “never”, has the most famous defenders in the history of philosophy: Augustine, Aquinas and Kant. Keep your answer; you will vote again at the end.", "note": "Take the vote silently first (eyes closed or on paper) so people aren’t swayed, then show hands."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The murderer at the door", "sub": "A real quarrel between two philosophers about one terrible case", "min": 12}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Would you lie to the man at the door?", "follow": "Who would tell the truth? Who would look for a third way, like slamming the door or saying nothing?", "solo": "Write one sentence: what would you say to the man, and why?", "note": "Count hands for each answer and write the numbers on the board. You will return to them in section 5."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "1797 · A REAL QUARREL", "title": "Constant throws down the challenge", "text": "The French-Swiss writer Benjamin Constant, in “On Political Reactions” (1797), argued that an unconditional duty to tell the truth would make society impossible. He said a German philosopher had gone so far as to call it a crime to lie even to a murderer asking where your friend is hiding. Kant took this as aimed at him and replied the same year.", "eg": "Constant’s point is the one most of you felt at once: surely the knife changes everything."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two answers at the door", "a": {"label": "CONSTANT", "title": "Truth is owed only to those with a right to it", "text": "A duty to tell the truth is a duty owed to someone. The murderer, by planning to kill, has no right to the truth. So lying to him wrongs nobody."}, "b": {"label": "KANT", "title": "Truthfulness is a duty to everyone", "text": "When you cannot avoid answering, you must be truthful. A lie wrongs humanity in general, because it weakens the trust that all agreements and rights depend on."}, "verdict": "Kant’s reply is the short essay “On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy” (1797)."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is Constant’s key claim?", "options": ["Lying is fine whenever it produces good results", "The duty of truthfulness is owed only to people who have a right to the truth", "Murderers should be lied to as a punishment", "Truth matters less than friendship in every case"], "answer": 1, "why": "Constant does not say “lie when it helps”. He narrows the duty: it is owed to persons, and someone planning murder has lost the right to be told the truth."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Kant’s argument, step by step", "items": [{"title": "The killing is his deed", "text": "If you answer truthfully and the murderer kills, the crime is his. You did your duty."}, {"title": "A lie makes you answerable", "text": "If you lie, you step outside duty and own what follows. Suppose your friend has slipped out, and the murderer, sent away by your lie, meets him in the street."}, {"title": "Every lie damages trust", "text": "A lie, even a kind one, weakens the reliability of statements as such, the basis of every contract and right."}], "conclusion": "So, Kant concludes, you must not lie even to the murderer. He is talking about cases where you cannot avoid answering yes or no.", "note": "Common confusion: Kant does not say you must volunteer information. Silence, refusing to answer, or shutting the door are not lies. The hard case is the forced yes-or-no."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "In Kant’s essay, why are you responsible for the bad results of a lie but not of a truthful answer?", "options": ["Because truthful answers always produce better outcomes", "Because by lying you leave your duty and answer for what follows, while the murderer’s crime is his own", "Because the murderer has a right to the truth", "Because the law punishes lying but not truth-telling"], "answer": 1, "why": "Kant’s point is about responsibility, not outcomes. Doing your duty leaves the wrong with the wrongdoer; stepping outside it makes the results yours."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A man with a knife asks whether your friend is hiding in your house.", "items": [{"title": "You can refuse", "text": "You can simply shut the door without answering. Does Kant’s view now look less harsh?"}, {"title": "Amsterdam, 1943", "text": "A family is hiding in your attic. An officer of an occupying army asks if you are hiding anyone. Many people in real history faced exactly this."}, {"title": "Small stakes", "text": "The stranger only wants to collect a debt your friend owes him. Is lying still right?"}, {"title": "True but misleading", "text": "You say, truthfully, “I saw him heading for the market an hour ago.” He has since come back."}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "Spend most time on step 2. Then ask: does step 4 feel cleaner than an outright lie? Park that question for section 4."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Why truth matters so much", "sub": "Augustine, Aquinas and Kant built the strictest view. Give it its best shot.", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "WHAT IS A LIE?", "title": "Augustine’s definition", "term": "mendacium (Latin): a lie", "text": "Augustine wrote two whole books on lying: On Lying (c. 395) and Against Lying (c. 420). Roughly, a lie is saying what you believe to be false, with the intention to deceive. So jokes, stories and fiction are not lies: nobody is meant to believe them. And a false statement you sincerely believe is a mistake, not a lie.", "eg": "An actor who says “I am the Prince of Denmark” is not lying. A friend who says “I posted your letter” when he didn’t is.", "note": "Check the definition with a quick example: is a mistaken weather forecast a lie? (No: no belief that it’s false, no intent to deceive.)"}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Augustine’s hard line", "items": [{"tag": "NO EXCEPTIONS", "title": "Not even to save a life", "text": "Augustine held that no lie is permitted, even one that would save someone’s life, because a lie corrupts the soul of the liar."}, {"tag": "DEGREES", "title": "But lies differ in weight", "text": "In On Lying he sorts lies into eight kinds, from lies that corrupt religious teaching down to lies that harm no one and protect someone. All are wrong; not all are equally wrong."}, {"tag": "THE MIDWIVES", "title": "Rewarded for mercy, not the lie", "text": "In Exodus 1 the Egyptian midwives deceive Pharaoh to save Hebrew baby boys. In Against Lying Augustine says God rewarded their kindness, not their deception."}]}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "AQUINAS · c. 1270", "title": "Every lie a sin, some lies small", "text": "Thomas Aquinas followed Augustine: every lie is a sin (Summa Theologiae II-II q.110 a.3). But he distinguished jocose lies (told for fun) and officious lies (told to help someone) from malicious lies told to harm. The first two are only venial, lesser sins. The strict view was never “all lies are equally bad”.", "eg": "Telling a child the dog “went to live on a farm” is officious: wrong on this view, but forgivable."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "For Aquinas, what is a lie told to help someone, such as hiding a friend from danger?", "options": ["Not a lie at all", "A sin, but only a venial (lesser) one", "The gravest kind of sin", "A duty"], "answer": 1, "why": "Aquinas keeps Augustine’s rule that every lie is a sin, but grades them: officious (helpful) and jocose lies are venial, while malicious lies told to harm are mortal sins."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "KANT’S DEEPER REASON", "title": "A lie uses the other person", "text": "In the Groundwork (1785) Kant tests a lying promise: borrowing money you know you will never repay. If everyone did it, promises would mean nothing, so the lie depends on others not doing what you do. Worse, the liar steers the other person’s choices with a false picture they could never agree to. Their reason becomes your tool.", "eg": "A friend lends you money only because they believe your promise. Your lie made the decision for them."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "Act so that you treat humanity, in your own person and in every other, always also as an end, never merely as a means.", "src": "Kant, Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785), 4:429, our translation", "note": "Read it twice. Stress “merely”: we use each other as means all the time (a shopkeeper, a bus driver); the wrong is using someone merely as a means."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Kant’s Formula of Humanity, why is deception specially wrong?", "options": ["Because it usually causes pain when discovered", "Because it bypasses the other person’s reason, steering their choice by a picture they could not agree to", "Because it is illegal in most countries", "Because it makes the liar unpopular"], "answer": 1, "why": "Kant’s objection is not about results. Deceiving someone treats them as a mechanism to be steered rather than a rational agent to be reasoned with."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "A KANTIAN RESCUE", "title": "Korsgaard: Kant was wrong about Kant", "text": "Christine Korsgaard, in “The Right to Lie: Kant on Dealing with Evil” (1986), argues that Kant’s own universal-law test permits lying to the murderer. He is himself deceiving you, posing as an innocent visitor. A rule of lying to concealed murderers would still work if everyone followed it, since he assumes you don’t know what he is. She grants the humanity formula is stricter, and treats this as a case of responding to evil.", "note": "Optional for younger groups. The key takeaway: even Kantians disagree about whether Kantianism forbids this lie."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Constant says the murderer has no right to the truth. Can anyone lose the right to the truth? Who?", "follow": "A scammer on the phone? A tyrant’s secret police? An opponent at poker? A nosy stranger asking where you live?", "solo": "Name one person or role you think has no right to the truth, and one you think always does."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Lies that help", "sub": "Consequences, character, and the white lie about a haircut", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE CONSEQUENTIALIST ANSWER", "title": "Mill: truth is precious, but not sacred", "text": "For a consequentialist, a lie is right when it makes things go best. J. S. Mill, in Utilitarianism ch. 2 (1861), stresses how useful trust is: even a small lie weakens the reliability of human assertion. Yet he grants exceptions, such as withholding information from a wrongdoer or bad news from someone dangerously ill.", "eg": "One white lie seems harmless. A habit of lying when it is “for the best” slowly makes your word worthless."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why might a careful consequentialist still be very strict about lying?", "options": ["Because consequentialists believe lying is wrong in itself", "Because hidden long-run costs, like lost trust and our biased judgement, are easy to underestimate", "Because God forbids it", "Because consequences never matter in ethics"], "answer": 1, "why": "Consequentialists count all effects. Lies tend to spread, get discovered, and erode trust, and people overrate how “necessary” their own lies are. That can justify a firm rule in practice."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Your friend has a haircut they can’t undo and asks: “Do you like it?” You don’t. What do you say?", "options": ["“Yes, I love it!”", "“It really suits your face” (half true)", "“Honestly, it’s not my favourite”", "“What do you think of it?”"], "reveal": "Small social lies are very common. In diary studies by Bella DePaulo and colleagues (1996), people reported telling roughly one or two lies a day, many of them small lies about feelings and opinions. Kant would call option 1 a lie; a virtue ethicist would look for a reply that is both true and kind."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three verdicts on the white lie", "items": [{"tag": "CONSEQUENCES", "title": "Yes, if it really helps", "text": "Lie if the kindness outweighs the costs, counting the risk of discovery and the slow loss of trust."}, {"tag": "DUTY", "title": "No: find a truthful kindness", "text": "A lie is a lie. Say something true and kind, or say less. Honesty does not require cruelty."}, {"tag": "VIRTUE", "term": "alētheia (Greek): truthfulness", "title": "Ask what an honest, kind person would do", "text": "In Nicomachean Ethics IV.7, Aristotle’s truthful person, speaking about himself, sits between the boaster and the falsely modest. Practical wisdom finds a reply that fits the friend and the moment."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Aristotle places the virtue of truthfulness between which two vices?", "options": ["Cruelty and flattery", "Boastfulness (claiming more than is true of yourself) and false modesty (claiming less)", "Silence and gossip", "Lying and misleading"], "answer": 1, "why": "In Nicomachean Ethics IV.7 Aristotle discusses truthfulness about oneself: the boaster (alazōn) overstates, the self-deprecator (eirōn) understates, and the truthful person tells it as it is."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "A MODERN TEST", "title": "Bok: could you defend it in public?", "text": "Sissela Bok’s book Lying (1978) defends a principle of veracity: lies are not always wrong, but they always need a justification. Her test of publicity asks whether you could justify the lie to reasonable people, including the person you deceive, if they found out.", "eg": "A doctor who hides a diagnosis “for the patient’s own good” must ask: could I explain this to the patient afterwards?", "note": "Time cue: you should be about 30 minutes in. If behind, skip the next quiz in discussion mode."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which deception would most clearly fail Bok’s test of publicity?", "options": ["Telling a murderer your friend isn’t home", "Keeping a surprise party secret", "A company hiding a known safety defect from customers", "Bluffing at poker"], "answer": 2, "why": "The company’s deception could not be defended to the people deceived. The surprise party and poker bluff are accepted by the people involved, and most reasonable people would endorse deceiving the murderer."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Lying vs misleading", "sub": "Every word true, every listener fooled", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE CAREFUL ANSWER", "title": "“I had a slice”", "text": "Someone asks who ate the cake. You ate three slices and say: “I had a slice.” Every word is true. You meant them to believe something false. Many people feel this is less bad than saying “I didn’t eat any”. US law agrees in one place: in Bronston v. United States (1973) the Supreme Court held that a literally true but misleading answer is not perjury.", "note": "Ask the room whether they have ever used this trick. Everyone has."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Is misleading really better?", "a": {"label": "YES", "title": "The listener shares the work", "text": "Defenders such as Jonathan Adler (1997) argue that when you only imply, the listener draws the false conclusion themselves, and you have kept faith with the words you actually asserted."}, "b": {"label": "NO", "title": "Same aim, same harm", "text": "Jennifer Saul, in Lying, Misleading, and What Is Said (2012), argues misleading is not in itself morally better. Choosing your words carefully can show something about your character, but the deception itself is just as bad."}, "note": "Ask for a show of hands: who finds side A convincing, who side B?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Augustine’s definition, which of these is a lie, not merely misleading?", "options": ["Saying “I had a slice” after eating three", "Saying “I didn’t eat any” after eating three", "Staying silent when asked", "Reading a novel aloud"], "answer": 1, "why": "A lie asserts what you believe false. “I had a slice” is true, so it misleads without lying. Silence asserts nothing, and fiction is not meant to be believed."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE: ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Is “I had a slice” really better than “I didn’t eat any”? If so, what makes the difference?", "follow": "Back to the door: would Kant let you say “I saw him heading for the market an hour ago”?", "solo": "Write one sentence: is careful misleading more honest, or just more cunning?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every view on lying has a weak spot. Find it.", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Kant hands over the victim", "text": "An ethics that tells the truth to a murderer to keep the speaker’s hands clean seems to care more about your own purity than about your friend’s life."}, {"title": "Calculating liars can’t be trusted", "text": "If people lie whenever they judge it best, no one can rely on anyone’s word, and our judgements about our own lies are notoriously self-serving."}, {"title": "Who forfeits the truth?", "text": "Constant says some people lose the right to the truth. But every liar thinks their target doesn’t deserve it; the exception may swallow the rule."}, {"title": "Misleading is lying with a lawyer", "text": "If “I had a slice” is as deceptive as “I didn’t eat any”, then the strict views simply reward people who are good with words."}, {"title": "Virtue gives no answer", "text": "“Do what an honest, kind person would do” sounds wise, but in the hard case the question is exactly what such a person would do."}, {"title": "The 1943 attic", "text": "People who lied to protect hidden families are widely honoured as heroes. Any theory that condemns them must explain why so much moral judgement is wrong."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Give each group 4 minutes, then 1 minute each to report. Push the group with the Kant card: a common reply is that you can usually refuse to answer. Is that enough when you can’t?"}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Is it ever right to lie?", "options": ["Never: every lie is wrong", "Rarely: only to prevent serious harm", "Often: small kind lies are fine too", "It depends entirely on the consequences"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Did anyone move? If you moved, name the slide that moved you. If you didn’t, name the objection you now think you must answer. A view you have tested against its best objection is worth more than one you merely hold."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["For three days, note every lie you tell, however small", "Next time you would tell a white lie, find a true and kind thing to say instead", "Read Kant’s short 1797 essay “On a Supposed Right to Lie” (a few pages)", "Ask a close friend: which lies, if any, would you want me to tell you?"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Augustine, Aquinas and Kant forbid all lies; Constant replies that truth is owed only to those with a right to it.", "Consequentialists allow lies that make things better, but trust is so valuable that the bar is high.", "Misleading with true words feels cleaner than lying; whether it really is better is open."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "On a Supposed Right to Lie from Philanthropy", "by": "Immanuel Kant (1797)", "note": "The hardest line in ethics, in a few pages."}, {"title": "On Lying and Against Lying", "by": "Augustine of Hippo (c. 395 and c. 420)", "note": "The first sustained philosophical study of what a lie is."}, {"title": "Lying: Moral Choice in Public and Private Life", "by": "Sissela Bok (1978)", "note": "Readable modern classic on doctors, governments and white lies."}, {"title": "The Right to Lie: Kant on Dealing with Evil", "by": "Christine Korsgaard (1986)", "note": "A Kantian argument that you may lie to the murderer."}, {"title": "Lying, Misleading, and What Is Said", "by": "Jennifer Saul (2012)", "note": "Is careful wording any better than a lie?"}], "also": ["Kant island", "Augustine island", "Nicomachean Ethics island", "Mill island"]}]}, {"id": "strangers", "title": "The Child in the Pond", "sub": "Singer, Mencius and what we owe to strangers far away", "question": "Must we help strangers far away?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can reconstruct Singer’s drowning-child argument, weigh the demandingness and special-obligation objections, and take a reasoned position on how much, and how, to help distant strangers.", "cast": ["singer", "unger", "mencius", "mozi", "kant", "williams"], "related": {"agora": "strangers", "islands": ["singer", "confucius", "mozi_xunzi", "kant"], "compass": ["e6", "e4"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The shallow pond", "text": "You are walking to school in new shoes that cost you a lot. You pass a shallow pond and see a small child in the water, thrashing, about to go under. No one else is around. You could wade in: the water only reaches your knees. You would save the child, but your shoes would be ruined and you would be late. Almost everyone says you must go in, and that walking on to save your shoes would be monstrous. In 1972 Peter Singer asked: if that is true, what follows for the rest of your life?", "note": "Let the room react. The case is meant to be easy. (Singer’s 1972 version has muddy clothes; the expensive shoes come from his later retellings.) The hard part comes when we apply it."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The child in the pond", "min": 12}, {"title": "Near and far", "min": 10}, {"title": "How much is enough?", "min": 10}, {"title": "Where should help go?", "min": 8}, {"title": "Attack the argument", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "If you can save a stranger’s life far away by giving up something you don’t need, must you?", "options": ["Yes: it is a strict duty", "It is good to do, but not required", "Only after I have looked after my own people", "No: distant strangers have no claim on me"], "reveal": "Remember your answer. Singer argues the first option follows from things you already believe about the pond. Tonight we test whether he is right."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The child in the pond", "sub": "An easy case that turns into a hard argument", "min": 12}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Would you wade in and ruin your shoes?", "follow": "Now: would it be wrong, not just unkind, to walk on?", "solo": "Write one sentence: why would walking past be wrong?", "note": "Expect every hand up. That near-unanimity is the premise Singer needs, so make it visible."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "1972 · A FAMOUS ESSAY", "title": "Famine, Affluence, and Morality", "text": "In 1971 millions of refugees from East Bengal (today Bangladesh) faced starvation. Singer, then a young Australian philosopher, wrote “Famine, Affluence, and Morality” (Philosophy and Public Affairs, 1972). He argued that the pond is not a special case. It shows a principle, and the principle has enormous consequences for how comfortable people live.", "eg": "The money for one pair of new shoes, given to an effective health charity, can do a great deal of good for someone you will never meet.", "note": "Avoid getting stuck on 1971 history; the argument is what matters. One minute here at most."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Singer’s argument", "items": [{"title": "Something bad", "text": "Suffering and death from lack of food, shelter and medical care are bad."}, {"title": "The principle", "text": "If it is in our power to prevent something bad without sacrificing anything of comparable moral importance, we ought to do it."}, {"title": "The fact", "text": "By giving to effective aid, we can prevent such suffering and death at the cost of things like new shoes, gadgets and holidays."}], "conclusion": "So we ought to give, and failing to give is wrong, not merely ungenerous. Charity, Singer says, is not an optional extra.", "note": "Ask: which premise would you reject? Most people discover they accept all three, which is why the argument bites."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Singer gives a strong and a moderate version of his principle. What is the difference?", "options": ["The strong version applies to strangers, the moderate one only to neighbours", "The strong version asks you to give until you would sacrifice something of comparable importance; the moderate one, until you would sacrifice anything morally significant", "The moderate version only applies to famines", "There is no difference; they are two names for one idea"], "answer": 1, "why": "Even the moderate version, which lets you keep anything morally significant, still rules out much spending on luxuries. Singer thought the strong version correct but that the moderate one already asks a lot."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A child is drowning in a shallow pond; saving her will ruin your shoes.", "items": [{"title": "Far away", "text": "A child is drowning in another country. Pressing a button sends a rescue drone; it costs exactly what your shoes cost."}, {"title": "Others could help", "text": "Twenty other people stand around the pond, doing nothing. Are you less obliged?"}, {"title": "Every day", "text": "A new child falls in every single morning, for the rest of your life."}, {"title": "No face", "text": "You will never see whom you saved. The money cuts the risk of death for many children you will never know."}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "Singer argues that distance and the number of bystanders make no moral difference. Step 3 is where most people start to resist; that is the demandingness objection, coming in section 3."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "UNGER’S TWIST", "title": "Bob’s Bugatti", "text": "Peter Unger, in Living High and Letting Die (1996), sharpened the point with stories. Bob has put his savings into a rare classic car. A runaway train is heading for a child; Bob can switch it onto the track where his Bugatti is parked. He doesn’t, and the child dies. Everyone condemns Bob. Unger asks why we don’t condemn ourselves for keeping money that could save a child.", "eg": "Singer retold the Bugatti case in a widely read newspaper essay in 1999."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Near and far", "sub": "Does distance matter? Do family and friends come first?", "min": 10}, {"t": "quote", "text": "Anyone who suddenly saw a child about to fall into a well would feel alarm and compassion.", "src": "Mencius (Mengzi 孟子) 2A6, c. 300 BCE, our translation", "note": "Point out the uncanny overlap with Singer’s pond, from a different tradition and era."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "AN OLDER POND", "title": "Mencius and the child at the well", "text": "Twenty-three centuries before Singer, the Confucian Mencius used almost the same case. His point was different: the alarm you feel shows that human nature has a sprout (duan 端) of goodness. For Mencius, morality grows outward from such feelings, starting with family. Compassion is real, but it is not impartial.", "term": "ren 仁: humaneness, benevolence"}, {"t": "versus", "title": "An ancient Chinese debate", "a": {"label": "MOZI", "title": "Impartial care", "text": "Mozi taught jian ai (兼愛), impartial care: treat others’ families as your own. Partiality, he argued, is the root of theft, feuds and war."}, "b": {"label": "MENCIUS", "title": "Love with distinctions", "text": "Mencius replied that care should be graded: most for parents, then kin, then others. Impartial care, he said, amounts to having no father (Mencius 3B9)."}, "verdict": "Singer’s impartialism has often been compared to Mozi’s. The debate about impartiality is more than two thousand years old."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Mencius and Singer both use a child in danger. How do their conclusions differ?", "options": ["Mencius thinks nobody would care about the child", "Mencius uses the case to show natural goodness, but keeps graded love; Singer uses it to argue for impartial duties to all", "Singer thinks family comes first, Mencius thinks strangers do", "They draw exactly the same conclusion"], "answer": 1, "why": "Mencius reads the child-at-the-well case as evidence that compassion is part of human nature, not as a proof that we owe equal concern to everyone."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SPECIAL OBLIGATIONS", "title": "One thought too many", "text": "William Godwin, in Political Justice (1793), said that if a fire traps both the great writer Fénelon and his chambermaid, you should save Fénelon, even if the chambermaid is your mother. Bernard Williams (“Persons, Character and Morality”, 1976) discussed a man who can save only one of two drowning people, one of them his wife. If he first checks that impartial morality permits saving her, he has had “one thought too many”.", "note": "In later editions Godwin changed the details (a valet, and the rescuer’s father) but kept the principle. Williams’ point is about motive: loving someone means not needing an impartial permission slip to save them."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is Williams’ “one thought too many” objection aimed at?", "options": ["People who think too slowly in emergencies", "The idea that saving your own wife needs an impartial justification first", "The claim that strangers matter at all", "People who give to charity without research"], "answer": 1, "why": "Williams thinks impartial theories distort personal relationships. Love gives its own reason to act; demanding impartial approval misunderstands what love is."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Is it wrong to spend $100 on your sister’s birthday present instead of giving it to save strangers’ lives?", "follow": "What about $1,000? $10,000? Where does it start to feel wrong, and why there?", "solo": "Write one sentence: how much extra weight should family get, and what justifies it?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "How much is enough?", "sub": "The demandingness objection and its answers", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE BIG OBJECTION", "title": "Too demanding?", "text": "Singer’s strong principle implies giving until you reach the point where giving more would cost you as much as it helps others, which he calls marginal utility. That means a far simpler life than most of us live. Susan Wolf’s “Moral Saints” (1982) argues that a life wholly devoted to doing good would lack much that makes a life admirable: humour, art, sport, deep personal projects.", "eg": "Learning the cello takes money and thousands of hours. Should it go?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What exactly does the demandingness objection claim?", "options": ["Giving to charity never helps", "A moral principle that requires near-total sacrifice is, for that reason, likely mistaken", "People are too selfish to give", "Only governments should help the poor"], "answer": 1, "why": "The objection is not that people won’t comply. It is that a correct morality would leave room for a life of one’s own, so a principle that doesn’t is suspect."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Four answers to demandingness", "items": [{"tag": "BITE THE BULLET", "title": "Yes, it is demanding", "text": "Singer and Unger: morality may simply ask more than we like. That our habits are comfortable doesn’t make them right."}, {"tag": "FAIR SHARE", "title": "Do your part", "text": "Liam Murphy (Moral Demands in Nonideal Theory, 2000): you must give what would be needed if everyone did their share, not cover for those who don’t."}, {"tag": "KANT", "title": "An imperfect duty", "text": "Kant counts beneficence as a duty (Groundwork 4:423), but one with latitude: you choose when, whom and how much (Metaphysics of Morals 6:390)."}, {"tag": "A PUBLIC STANDARD", "title": "Ask for what people will do", "text": "Singer later proposed modest, income-based giving targets. The pledge society Giving What We Can (founded 2009) asks members to give 10% of income."}], "note": "Ask each person to stand by the card they find most persuasive. It shows the room’s split quickly."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Kant calls beneficence an “imperfect duty”. What does that mean?", "options": ["It is not really a duty", "You must make others’ happiness one of your ends, but you have latitude over when, whom and how much", "You only need to help if it is convenient and you feel like it", "It applies only to people you know"], "answer": 1, "why": "Imperfect duties are real and binding: you may not adopt a policy of never helping. But unlike the duty not to lie, they do not dictate a specific act on each occasion."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "What share of income should a comfortably-off person give to help strangers in extreme poverty?", "options": ["0%: it is optional", "1–2%", "About 10%", "As much as possible short of real hardship"], "reveal": "Singer’s 1972 argument points to the last option. Murphy’s fair-share view gives a much smaller figure. Kant leaves the amount to judgement. Giving What We Can settled on 10% as a public standard. Each answer rests on a theory: which one is yours?"}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE: ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Walking past the drowning child feels monstrous. Not donating feels normal. Is there a real moral difference, or just a psychological one?", "follow": "List the differences: distance, certainty, being the only one, an emergency vs a chronic problem. Which, if any, matter morally?", "solo": "Write one sentence: the one difference between the pond and global poverty that you think matters most.", "note": "Keep a list on the board. These differences feed straight into the critique cards."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Where should help go?", "sub": "Effective giving, or changing the system?", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "EFFECTIVE ALTRUISM", "title": "Do the most good you can", "text": "From around 2009, Singer’s argument inspired the effective altruism movement: use evidence to find where help goes furthest. Charity evaluators such as GiveWell (founded 2007) estimate the cost of saving a life through their top-rated health charities, such as malaria-net programmes, at a few thousand US dollars. The estimates change year to year and are disputed, but the gaps between charities can be large.", "eg": "Two charities can both be well-meaning, yet one may do many times more good per dollar.", "note": "Avoid debating specific charities or organisations in the news. Keep the focus on the principle of comparing impact."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is the central claim of effective giving?", "options": ["All charities are equally good", "Since charities differ greatly in impact, we should use evidence to choose where help goes furthest", "Only local charities count", "Giving should always be anonymous"], "answer": 1, "why": "The key idea is comparison: if one option saves far more lives per dollar, choosing the other has a real cost in lives."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Charity or change?", "a": {"label": "EFFECTIVE GIVING", "title": "Measure, then give", "text": "We can verify that nets or medicines save lives now. Uncertain systemic projects may do nothing. Proven help today beats a hoped-for revolution."}, "b": {"label": "SYSTEMIC CHANGE", "title": "Fix the causes", "text": "Critics such as economist Angus Deaton (The Great Escape, 2013) warn that aid can weaken governments’ accountability to their own people. Poverty is shaped by institutions and trade rules; measurable charity treats symptoms."}, "verdict": "Defenders reply that systemic work is welcome when evidence backs it. Critics reply that the best work is often the hardest to measure."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "You have $500. Where does it go?", "options": ["Malaria nets through a top-rated health charity", "A food bank in your own city", "A group working to reform aid or trade rules", "Keep it: save for your own future"], "reveal": "Singer and GiveWell would point to the first for measurable impact. The systemic critics would favour the third. Special-obligation views give real weight to the second. And on Kant’s imperfect duty, keeping this $500 may be permissible if you help at other times. Your choice reveals which argument you actually find strongest.", "note": "Ask one person from each option to defend it in 30 seconds."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the argument", "sub": "The pond argument is strong. Is it strong enough?", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Too demanding", "text": "A principle that condemns nearly everyone who buys a coffee may be refuted by that result, rather than proving that we are all wrongdoers."}, {"title": "Poverty isn’t a pond", "text": "The pond is a one-off emergency with one rescuer and a sure result. Global poverty is chronic, with billions of possible helpers and uncertain chains of cause and effect."}, {"title": "Special ties matter", "text": "Impartiality cannot honour family, friendship and community. Mencius and Williams say a morality that treats your mother like a stranger has gone wrong."}, {"title": "Charity can’t fix structures", "text": "Giving can let rich countries feel good while leaving unfair institutions untouched, and may weaken the link between governments and citizens."}, {"title": "Not rescue but harm", "text": "Thomas Pogge (World Poverty and Human Rights, 2002) argues we are not bystanders at a pond: rich countries help uphold a global order that harms the poor. The duty is to stop harming, a stronger duty than to rescue."}, {"title": "Why must I cover for others?", "text": "If everyone gave a little, poverty would shrink. Why should those who give have to make up for those who don’t?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "The Pogge card is a twist: it attacks Singer’s framing but makes the conclusion stronger, not weaker. Make sure that group sees this."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "If you can save a stranger’s life far away by giving up something you don’t need, must you?", "options": ["Yes: it is a strict duty", "It is good to do, but not required", "Only after I have looked after my own people", "No: distant strangers have no claim on me"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Compare with your first vote. If you still reject the strict duty, which premise of Singer’s argument do you now reject? Saying which is the honest price of disagreeing."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["Look up how an independent charity evaluator rates a charity you or your family already support", "For one week, note what you spend on things you don’t need, just to see the total", "Read Singer’s 1972 essay “Famine, Affluence, and Morality”", "Ask your family at dinner: what do we owe to strangers far away?"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Singer’s pond argument says distance and other bystanders don’t weaken the duty to prevent serious harm at small cost.", "The strongest replies appeal to demandingness, special obligations, and the difference between a rescue and a chronic problem.", "Even if we must help, it is a further question whether to fund measurable aid or work on causes."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Famine, Affluence, and Morality", "by": "Peter Singer (1972)", "note": "The short essay that started it all."}, {"title": "The Life You Can Save", "by": "Peter Singer (2009)", "note": "The argument updated for a general reader, with practical targets."}, {"title": "Living High and Letting Die", "by": "Peter Unger (1996)", "note": "Sharper thought experiments, including Bob’s Bugatti."}, {"title": "Mencius 2A6 and 3B9; Mozi, “Impartial Caring”", "by": "Mozi (5th century BCE) and Mencius (4th century BCE)", "note": "The ancient Chinese debate on graded vs impartial love."}, {"title": "Persons, Character and Morality", "by": "Bernard Williams (1976)", "note": "The source of “one thought too many”."}, {"title": "World Poverty and Human Rights", "by": "Thomas Pogge (2002)", "note": "Why the rich may be harming, not just failing to rescue."}], "also": ["Singer island", "Confucius island", "Mozi and Xunzi island", "Kant island"]}]}, {"id": "killing", "title": "The Lever and the Bridge", "sub": "Foot, Thomson, Rachels and the trolley problem", "question": "Is killing worse than letting die?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can explain the trolley problem, the distinctions between doing and allowing and between intending and foreseeing, and Rachels’ challenge, and decide which (if any) they accept.", "cast": ["foot", "thomson", "rachels", "aquinas", "kamm", "anscombe"], "related": {"agora": "killing", "islands": ["virtue", "aquinas", "kant", "mill"], "compass": ["e1", "e2", "e2a"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The runaway trolley", "text": "A trolley is hurtling down a track, its brakes gone. Ahead, five workers are fixing the rails; they can’t hear it and can’t get out in time. You are standing by a lever. Pull it, and the trolley swerves onto a side track, where one worker is standing. He will die instead. If you do nothing, the trolley follows its course and kills the five. If you act, you kill one. There is no time to shout, no other option, no doubt about the outcome. Your hand is on the lever.", "note": "Stress that the case is deliberately artificial: certainty and no third option. That is how the philosopher isolates one question."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The trolley", "min": 12}, {"title": "Doing vs allowing", "min": 10}, {"title": "Intending vs foreseeing", "min": 10}, {"title": "Is there any difference at all?", "min": 8}, {"title": "Attack the arguments", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Other things equal, is killing someone worse than letting them die?", "options": ["Yes, always", "Usually, but it can be outweighed", "No: only outcomes and motives matter", "Not sure yet"], "reveal": "Keep your answer. Most people feel a difference, and many laws build it in. Tonight we ask whether the feeling tracks something real."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The trolley", "sub": "Same numbers, different verdicts. Why?", "min": 12}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Would you pull the lever?", "follow": "Those who wouldn’t: is it because pulling it makes you a killer?", "solo": "Write one sentence: would you pull it, and why?"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "1967 · WHERE IT STARTED", "title": "Philippa Foot’s tram", "text": "The modern debate began with Philippa Foot’s “The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect” (1967). Her version had a tram driver who can steer from five people onto one. She paired it with a judge who could stop a deadly riot, which would kill five, only by framing and executing an innocent man. Same numbers. Almost everyone says the driver may steer and the judge may not frame.", "eg": "The puzzle is not “one or five?” It is why five-for-one is fine in one case and an outrage in another.", "note": "Write “driver: steer” and “judge: don’t frame” on the board. The whole seminar is about explaining that pair."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "New case. You are on a footbridge over the track. The only way to stop the trolley is to push a large stranger off the bridge; his body will stop it, saving the five. Do you push?", "options": ["Yes, push: one death is better than five", "No, don’t push"], "reveal": "Judith Jarvis Thomson, who named “the trolley problem” in 1976, made this footbridge case famous. In a large online study by Marc Hauser and colleagues (2007), roughly 9 in 10 said switching the lever was permissible, but only about 1 in 10 said the same of pushing. Same numbers, opposite verdicts."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why is the trolley problem a philosophical problem, not just a hard choice?", "options": ["Because nobody knows whether five is more than one", "Because the outcomes are the same in the lever and footbridge cases, yet most people judge them differently, and it is hard to state the principle behind that", "Because trolleys are dangerous", "Because pulling a lever is illegal"], "answer": 1, "why": "The challenge is to find a principle that explains our considered verdicts across cases. If we can’t, maybe one verdict is wrong."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "You can save five from the trolley at the cost of one life.", "items": [{"title": "Transplant", "text": "A surgeon has five patients who will die without organs. A healthy visitor’s organs would save all five. May she kill him? (Thomson)"}, {"title": "Trapdoor", "text": "The large man stands on a trapdoor. You can drop him onto the track by pulling a lever, without touching him."}, {"title": "Loop", "text": "The side track loops back to the five. The trolley only stops because it hits the one man. Without him, it would come round and kill them anyway. (Thomson, 1985)"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "Transplant gets a near-unanimous no. Trapdoor and Loop split rooms. Write the split on the board; it matters in section 3."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Doing vs allowing", "sub": "Is causing a death worse than failing to prevent one?", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "FOOT’S ANSWER", "title": "Negative and positive duties", "text": "Foot suggested that negative duties, not to harm, are stronger than positive duties, to help. The tram driver is not choosing between killing and letting die: either way he kills, so he should kill fewer. The judge must choose between killing one (breaking a negative duty) and failing to save five (a positive duty). So he may not frame the man.", "term": "doing vs allowing"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Foot’s view, why may the tram driver steer but the judge may not frame?", "options": ["Because judges have special legal duties", "Because the driver kills either way and should kill fewer, while the judge must choose between killing one and letting five die", "Because the riot victims are guilty", "Because driving is not a deliberate act"], "answer": 1, "why": "Foot’s idea is that the strong duty not to kill is on both sides only for the driver. For the judge, the weaker duty to save is set against the stronger duty not to kill."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THOMSON’S PROBLEM", "title": "The bystander breaks the pattern", "text": "Thomson noticed a gap. Replace the driver with a bystander at a switch. Doing nothing, he lets five die; pulling, he kills one. On Foot’s view he should not pull. Yet most people think he may. So the doing/allowing line can’t be the whole story. Decades later, in “Turning the Trolley” (2008), Thomson changed her mind and argued the bystander may not turn it after all.", "note": "Many students are surprised that Thomson reversed her own famous view. Use it to show that philosophers do change their minds."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Why might doing be worse than allowing?", "items": [{"tag": "AGENCY", "title": "You become the cause", "text": "Warren Quinn (1989) argued that harmful agency is especially grave: it treats the victim’s body and life as yours to use or end."}, {"tag": "BASELINE", "title": "Taking vs not giving", "text": "When you let someone die, they lose only what you would have provided. When you kill, you take what was already theirs."}, {"tag": "RIGHTS", "title": "Side constraints", "text": "Robert Nozick (1974) treats rights as limits on what we may do to people, not goals to maximise. You may not violate one right to prevent five violations."}], "note": "Ask: which of the three reasons survives the bystander case?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which of these is most clearly a case of letting die, not killing?", "options": ["Pushing a man off a bridge to stop a trolley", "Steering a tram from five onto one", "Not paying for a stranger’s life-saving operation", "Switching the trolley onto the side track"], "answer": 2, "why": "In the other three cases you start the sequence that kills someone. Failing to pay for an operation leaves a person to a fate you didn’t cause."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "The driver and the bystander face the same lever and the same tracks. Should their answers differ?", "follow": "Does it matter that the driver is already in control of the trolley?", "solo": "Write one sentence: does it matter morally whether you are the driver or a bystander?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Intending vs foreseeing", "sub": "Is it what you aim at, not what you cause, that counts?", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "AQUINAS · c. 1270", "title": "The doctrine of double effect", "text": "In Summa Theologiae II-II q.64 a.7, Aquinas says one act can have two effects: one intended, the other beside the intention (praeter intentionem). Killing an attacker in self-defence can be lawful if you aim to save your life, not to kill. Later moralists turned this into a test for acts with bad side effects.", "term": "praeter intentionem (Latin): beside the intention"}, {"t": "flow", "title": "The doctrine’s four conditions", "items": [{"title": "The act", "text": "The act itself is not wrong in its own nature."}, {"title": "The intention", "text": "You intend only the good effect, not the bad one."}, {"title": "The means", "text": "The bad effect is not the means by which the good effect is achieved."}, {"title": "Proportion", "text": "There is a serious enough reason to allow the bad effect."}]}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two bombers, same deaths", "a": {"label": "STRATEGIC BOMBER", "title": "Deaths as a side effect", "text": "Bombs a munitions factory to shorten a war, foreseeing that nearby civilians will die. Their deaths are a terrible side effect."}, "b": {"label": "TERROR BOMBER", "title": "Deaths as the means", "text": "Bombs civilians so that their deaths will break the enemy’s morale. The deaths are exactly how his plan works."}, "verdict": "The doctrine permits the first (if proportionate) and forbids the second, even if the body count is the same.", "note": "Common confusion: the strategic bomber still knows civilians will die. The doctrine is about intention, not knowledge."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "How does double effect explain the difference between the lever and the footbridge?", "options": ["In the footbridge case, more people die", "At the lever, the one’s death is a foreseen side effect; on the footbridge, the man being hit is the means of stopping the trolley", "The lever is far from the victim, the bridge is close", "Pushing is illegal, pulling a lever isn’t"], "answer": 1, "why": "At the lever you would be glad if the one somehow escaped; your plan still works. On the bridge your plan needs his body to stop the trolley."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "TROUBLE FOR THE DOCTRINE", "title": "Loops and close calls", "text": "In Thomson’s Loop case, the one man’s body is needed to stop the trolley, so his being hit is the means. Yet many people think turning is still permissible. And there is a closeness problem: the pusher on the bridge could say he intends only that the man’s body stops the trolley, not that he dies. If such redescriptions work, the doctrine forbids almost nothing.", "note": "Foot herself was unconvinced by double effect and preferred her doing/allowing account. Make sure the room knows the two ideas are rivals."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why is the Loop case a problem for the doctrine of double effect?", "options": ["Because nobody dies in it", "Because the one man’s being hit is the means of saving the five, yet many people still think turning is permissible", "Because the trolley goes faster on a loop", "Because it involves pushing someone"], "answer": 1, "why": "If the doctrine is right, using the man as a means should be forbidden. If turning in Loop is permissible, either the doctrine is wrong or our intuition is."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Is there any difference at all?", "sub": "Rachels and the bathtub", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "1975 · SMITH AND JONES", "title": "Two cousins in two bathtubs", "text": "James Rachels, in “Active and Passive Euthanasia” (New England Journal of Medicine, 1975), told two stories. Smith will inherit if his young cousin dies; he drowns the boy in the bath. Jones has the same motive and plan, but the boy slips, hits his head and falls face down. Jones stands ready to push him back under, but need do nothing; he watches the boy drown.", "eg": "Is Jones any better than Smith? Almost no one thinks so."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Rachels’ argument", "items": [{"title": "One difference", "text": "The two cases differ only in that Smith kills and Jones lets die."}, {"title": "Equal verdicts", "text": "Smith and Jones are equally bad."}, {"title": "So", "text": "If killing were in itself worse than letting die, Smith would be worse. He isn’t."}], "conclusion": "So the bare difference between killing and letting die is morally irrelevant. Rachels concluded that medical rules allowing doctors to withdraw treatment but forbidding active euthanasia rest on a mistake.", "note": "Time cue: about 38 minutes in. Keep this section brisk to leave 10 minutes for the critique cards."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Critics say the Smith and Jones cases don’t prove Rachels’ conclusion. What is the strongest form of that reply?", "options": ["Jones isn’t really to blame because he didn’t touch the boy", "A factor can matter in some contexts but be masked in others: both men are as bad as possible, so a real difference may not show", "Inheritance law makes the cases different", "Drowning is not a real case of letting die"], "answer": 1, "why": "Shelly Kagan (“The Additive Fallacy”, 1988) and Frances Kamm argue that one case pair cannot show a factor never matters. Showing it makes no difference here doesn’t show it makes none anywhere."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE: ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "A patient asks her doctor to switch off her ventilator; she dies. Another asks her doctor for a lethal injection. Many legal systems allow the first and forbid the second. Is there a real moral difference?", "follow": "Is switching off a machine doing or allowing? Does it matter who switches it off?", "solo": "Write one sentence: is there a moral difference between the two doctors?", "note": "Keep this discussion on the philosophical distinction, not on any current law or campaign."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Which best explains why you treat the lever and the footbridge differently, if you do?", "options": ["Doing vs allowing", "Intending vs foreseeing", "Pushing someone with your own hands feels different", "Nothing: I treat them the same"], "reveal": "Psychologists such as Joshua Greene have studied this: in a 2009 study, his team found that people judged harm worse when it involved personal force, especially when the harm was also intended. Philosophers then ask whether that reaction is moral insight or a gut alarm. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, most professional philosophers favoured switching in the lever case.", "note": "If most choose option 3, ask: is “it feels different” a reason, or a fact about us that needs a reason?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every position here has a serious weak spot", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Trolleys are too artificial", "text": "Real choices are never certain and always have more options. Intuitions about fantasy cases may tell us nothing about how to act."}, {"title": "Intuitions are gut alarms", "text": "Greene and Singer argue that reluctance to push may be an emotional reaction to up-close violence, not insight into a moral truth."}, {"title": "Allowing excuses neglect", "text": "If letting die is less bad than killing, rich societies can let millions die of preventable poverty and still feel their hands are clean."}, {"title": "Intentions are too slippery", "text": "Double effect lets you redescribe what you intend. And intention seems to judge the agent’s mind, not whether the act is allowed (Thomson and Scanlon press this)."}, {"title": "Outcomes alone back the surgeon", "text": "If only outcomes matter, the surgeon should kill one patient to save five. Almost everyone finds this monstrous; can a pure outcome view explain why not?"}, {"title": "Where is the line?", "text": "Is switching off life support killing or letting die? Is it different if a stranger, not the doctor, switches it off? The distinction may be too blurry to bear weight."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Make sure the outcome view gets attacked too (the surgeon card). Every side should leave feeling some pressure."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Other things equal, is killing someone worse than letting them die?", "options": ["Yes, always", "Usually, but it can be outweighed", "No: only outcomes and motives matter", "Not sure yet"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. If you still think killing is worse, can you now say why in one sentence: agency, rights, intention? If you don’t, how do you handle the transplant surgeon?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["Try the lever, footbridge and transplant cases on family or friends and note the reasons they give", "Write one sentence stating a principle that fits all your answers tonight, then look for a case that breaks it", "Read Rachels’ short 1975 article “Active and Passive Euthanasia”", "Read Thomson’s 1985 paper “The Trolley Problem”"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Lever and footbridge have the same numbers, yet most people judge them differently; the trolley problem is finding out why.", "Two leading explanations: doing is worse than allowing (Foot), and intending harm is worse than foreseeing it (double effect).", "Rachels argues the bare difference is irrelevant; critics reply that one pair of cases cannot show it never matters."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect", "by": "Philippa Foot (1967)", "note": "Where the trolley first ran."}, {"title": "The Trolley Problem", "by": "Judith Jarvis Thomson (1985)", "note": "Bystander, footbridge, transplant and loop, in one classic paper."}, {"title": "Active and Passive Euthanasia", "by": "James Rachels (1975)", "note": "Smith and Jones in four pages."}, {"title": "Actions, Intentions, and Consequences: The Doctrine of Doing and Allowing", "by": "Warren Quinn (1989)", "note": "The best defence of the doing/allowing distinction."}, {"title": "Would You Kill the Fat Man?", "by": "David Edmonds (2013)", "note": "A lively, readable history of the trolley problem."}], "also": ["Virtue ethics island (Foot and Anscombe)", "Aquinas island", "Kant island", "Mill island"]}]}, {"id": "animals", "title": "Do Animals Count as Much as We Do?", "sub": "Bentham, Singer, Kant, Korsgaard and Regan on pain, rights and dinner", "question": "Do animals count as much as we do?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 16, "aim": "Participants can explain equal consideration and speciesism, Kant’s indirect duties and Korsgaard’s reply, Regan’s rights view and the argument from marginal cases, and can defend a reasoned view about what, if anything, follows for food.", "cast": ["bentham", "singer", "kant", "korsgaard"], "related": {"agora": null, "islands": ["singer", "kant", "descartes"], "compass": ["e3", "e4"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The Question at the Barbecue", "text": "A summer barbecue. The family dog lies on the sofa inside, wrapped in a blanket because she’s scared of the fireworks. Outside, sausages hiss on the grill. Your friend’s seven-year-old brother looks from one to the other and asks: “Why does Bella get a blanket and the pig gets eaten?” Everyone laughs. Someone says, “Because that’s different.” He asks, “Different how?” Nobody answers. Tonight we try to answer him properly. Is there a real moral difference between a dog and a pig, or between a pig and us? Or have we just never been made to explain it?", "note": "Let the laugh happen, then hold the silence a moment. The child’s question is the whole seminar."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "Can they suffer? Bentham and Singer", "min": 12}, {"title": "Duties to whom? Kant and Korsgaard", "min": 9}, {"title": "Rights, not sums: Regan", "min": 7}, {"title": "The argument from marginal cases", "min": 7}, {"title": "What follows for food?", "min": 7}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then vote again", "min": 13}]}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "id": "v1", "q": "A pig and a human feel exactly the same amount of pain. Does the pig’s pain matter as much?", "options": ["Yes, exactly as much", "It matters, but less than the human’s", "It matters only because of its effects on humans", "It doesn’t matter morally at all"], "reveal": "Each option has a famous defender. Bentham and Singer say yes, equally. Much common sense says less. Kant says the duty is really about us. Descartes is often read as denying animals feel at all, though scholars debate how far he went. Keep your answer: we vote again at the end."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "Can they suffer?", "sub": "Bentham asks one question, and Singer builds a movement on it", "min": 12}, {"t": "quote", "text": "“The question is not, Can they reason? nor, Can they talk? but, Can they suffer?”", "src": "Jeremy Bentham, An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), ch. 17, sec. 1, footnote", "note": "Mention the context: in the same footnote Bentham compares the treatment of animals to slavery, noting that skin colour had been recognised as no reason to abandon a human being to torment."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "EQUAL CONSIDERATION", "title": "Equal weight, not equal treatment", "text": "Peter Singer, in “All Animals Are Equal” (1974) and Animal Liberation (1975), argues for the equal consideration of interests. If a pig and a child feel the same pain, that pain counts the same. This is not equal treatment: a pig has no interest in voting or reading, so there’s nothing to give it. Where interests are alike, the weight is alike.", "eg": "A dog and a toddler both want not to be kicked. Neither wants a library card. Equality applies to the first, not the second."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which statement would Singer accept?", "options": ["Animals should have the same rights as humans, including the right to vote", "A pain matters the same whoever feels it, though different beings have different interests", "Animals matter only if they are intelligent enough to reason", "We must treat a pig and a child in exactly the same way"], "answer": 1, "why": "Equal consideration weighs similar interests equally. It doesn’t mean identical treatment or identical rights, since a pig has no interest in voting."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SPECIESISM", "title": "A prejudice like the others?", "text": "The psychologist Richard Ryder coined “speciesism” in 1970; Singer made it famous. It means giving less weight to someone’s interests simply because of their species. Singer’s analogy: the racist weighs the interests of his own race more heavily, the sexist those of his own sex. The speciesist does the same with species, and has no better reason.", "term": "speciesism (Ryder, 1970)"}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Singer’s argument in four steps", "items": [{"title": "Suffering is bad", "text": "Pain is bad for whoever feels it."}, {"title": "Equal interests, equal weight", "text": "Morality requires giving similar interests similar weight, impartially."}, {"title": "Species isn’t a reason", "text": "Like race or sex, species membership is not in itself a morally relevant difference."}, {"title": "Many animals suffer", "text": "Mammals, birds and probably many other animals can feel pain."}], "conclusion": "So a similar pain in a pig or a human deserves similar moral weight. To ignore it is speciesism."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which premise do most critics deny to block Singer’s conclusion, while still agreeing that pain is bad?", "options": ["That pain is bad for whoever feels it", "That species membership is not in itself morally relevant", "That humans can feel pain", "That the conclusion follows from the premises"], "answer": 1, "why": "Most critics who reject Singer accept that pain is bad. They argue that being human, or belonging to a kind of being that reasons, does matter morally in itself. That is the battleground."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Predict: do fish feel pain?", "options": ["Yes", "No", "Scientists aren’t sure"], "reveal": "Scientists still argue. Lynne Sneddon and colleagues (2003) found that trout injected with irritants changed their behaviour in ways that looked like pain. Brian Key (2016) argued fish lack the brain structures needed to feel it. The 2012 Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness held that mammals, birds and other creatures such as octopuses have the brain basis for conscious experience. Whether the circle reaches fish, crabs or insects is still open."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "By pressing a button that costs you one minute, you can stop a creature being scalded with hot water for ten seconds.", "items": [{"title": "A dog", "text": "The creature is a dog. Do you press?"}, {"title": "A pig", "text": "The creature is a pig on a farm, which will be slaughtered next week anyway."}, {"title": "A fish", "text": "The creature is a fish. Its pain is uncertain, but it thrashes."}, {"title": "A wasp", "text": "The creature is a wasp, which has just stung you."}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Was it the creature’s capacity to suffer that changed, or something else?", "note": "If most of the room presses for all four, that suggests suffering matters to them regardless of species. Then ask: why does the button feel easier to press than changing our eating habits?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Duties to whom?", "sub": "Kant says cruelty is wrong, but not because of the animal. Korsgaard disagrees, as a Kantian.", "min": 9}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "KANT · INDIRECT DUTIES", "title": "Cruel to animals, cruel to people", "text": "For Kant, only rational beings are ends in themselves, so we have no duties directly to animals. Yet cruelty to them is still wrong: it dulls our natural sympathy, which helps us do our duties to humans. In the Metaphysics of Morals (1797, Doctrine of Virtue §17) he calls cruelty to animals a violation of a duty to oneself. In his ethics lectures, a man who has his old dog shot because it can no longer earn its keep does not fail in a duty to the dog, but damages the humanity in himself.", "eg": "On Kant’s view, kicking a stray dog is wrong roughly the way practising cruelty on a dummy is wrong: because of what it does to you."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Duties to, or duties about?", "a": {"label": "DIRECT DUTY", "title": "Wrong to the animal", "text": "The animal itself is wronged. Its pain is the reason the act is bad, whether or not it changes anyone’s character."}, "b": {"label": "INDIRECT DUTY", "title": "Wrong because of us", "text": "The act is bad because of its effects on humans: hardening our hearts, upsetting onlookers, or damaging someone’s property."}, "verdict": "Test case: someone tortures an animal in total secret, feels nothing, and it changes nothing about how they treat people. Is anything wrong?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Kant’s view, why is it wrong to torment a dog?", "options": ["Because the dog has rights as an end in itself", "Because it damages the human sympathy we need to do our duties to people", "Because it causes more pain than pleasure overall", "Because dogs are someone’s property"], "answer": 1, "why": "Kant’s duty is indirect: cruelty damages humanity in us. That’s why critics say his view gets the wrong reason, even when it gets the right verdict."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "KORSGAARD · A KANTIAN REPLY", "title": "Fellow creatures", "text": "Christine Korsgaard, in Fellow Creatures (2018), uses Kant against Kant. When we value our own good, she argues, we treat ourselves as ends in themselves. But what we value then is not only our reason. It’s that things can go well or badly for us, and that’s true of animals too. Animals can’t make moral laws, but they are ends in themselves in the sense that matters: beings with a good of their own.", "note": "The key move: Kant links being an end in itself to rational agency. Korsgaard splits that into two senses and argues animals have the one we actually care about when we value ourselves."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE · ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Kant and Korsgaard would both condemn a man who tortures his dog. Where do they disagree?", "follow": "Which of them can also condemn factory farming, where no one feels cruel?", "solo": "Write one sentence on the difference between Kant’s reason and Korsgaard’s reason for condemning cruelty to animals."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is Korsgaard’s key claim against Kant?", "options": ["Animals can reason, so they qualify as rational agents after all", "Animals are ends in themselves because things can be good or bad for them", "Only the consequences of our actions matter, not duties", "Animals have no moral standing, but humans should still be kind"], "answer": 1, "why": "Korsgaard doesn’t say animals reason like us. She says the value we place on ourselves rests on our having a good of our own, which animals share."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Rights, not sums", "sub": "Regan thinks Singer’s arithmetic leaves animals unprotected", "min": 7}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "REGAN · SUBJECTS-OF-A-LIFE", "title": "Not containers for pleasure", "text": "Tom Regan, in The Case for Animal Rights (1983), argues that many animals, at least normal mammals over a year old, are “subjects-of-a-life”: they have beliefs, desires, memory, a sense of their own future and a welfare that matters to them. Such beings have inherent value and rights. They may not be used merely as means, even if using them would bring more good overall."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Singer and Regan: allies who disagree", "a": {"label": "SINGER · UTILITARIAN", "title": "Weigh the sums", "text": "What matters is the total of interests affected. In principle, a painful experiment could be justified if it prevented far greater suffering."}, "b": {"label": "REGAN · RIGHTS", "title": "Some things you can’t do", "text": "Rights work like a fence: they can’t be traded for benefits to others. Regan aims at empty cages, not bigger ones."}, "verdict": "Singer’s view can bend; Regan’s cannot. Which is a strength, and which a weakness?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which practice could Singer’s view allow in principle, but Regan’s would forbid?", "options": ["Tormenting animals for entertainment", "A painful experiment on a few animals that would save many more lives", "Factory farming chickens for cheaper meat", "Hunting purely for sport"], "answer": 1, "why": "A utilitarian weighs total suffering, so a rare experiment with huge benefits could pass. Regan’s rights cannot be overridden by benefits to others."}, {"t": "puzzle", "q": "Regan’s own test case: a lifeboat holds four adult humans and a dog. One must go overboard, or all will drown. What does Regan say, and is it consistent with equal rights?", "a": "Regan says the dog should go, because death takes away far less from a dog than from a human with richer future possibilities. He even says this would hold against a million dogs. Critics reply that this quietly brings back the comparison of values his rights view was meant to reject. Defenders say it applies only in true emergencies where someone must be harmed.", "note": "This is a good place to let disagreement run if time allows."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "The argument from marginal cases", "sub": "Whatever line you draw, some humans may fall on the other side", "min": 7}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The argument", "items": [{"title": "Pick the trait", "text": "Name the trait that gives humans full moral status: reason, language, moral agency, a sense of the future."}, {"title": "Some humans lack it", "text": "Newborn babies, and people with the most severe cognitive disabilities, have less of that trait than some animals, or none."}, {"title": "They still count fully", "text": "We rightly hold that these humans have full moral status."}, {"title": "So the trait isn’t the reason", "text": "Then the trait can’t be what gives status, or some animals qualify too."}], "conclusion": "Either species alone matters, which looks like speciesism, or many animals matter more than we treat them."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "A NAME AND ITS PROBLEM", "title": "Marginal to whom?", "text": "The name “argument from marginal cases” was used by Jan Narveson (1977). Many now prefer “argument from species overlap”, because calling any person “marginal” is offensive and misleading. The argument does not say disabled people matter less. Its premise is the opposite: they matter fully, and it asks what follows.", "note": "Handle with care in the room. Make sure no one hears the argument as devaluing anyone; its whole force depends on the premise that every human counts fully."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Which step would you reject? Carl Cohen (1986) answers that rights belong to beings of a kind that normally reasons, so every human qualifies. Is that a good answer, or speciesism in a nicer coat?", "solo": "Write one sentence: which step of the argument is weakest, and why?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What does Carl Cohen’s “kind” reply claim?", "options": ["Babies will reason someday, so only their future matters", "Moral status depends on the normal capacities of one’s kind, not one’s individual capacities", "Animals reason as well as humans, so both have rights", "Only beings who can make moral claims feel pain"], "answer": 1, "why": "Cohen grounds rights in the kind of being you are. Critics ask why the kind matters and not the individual in front of you."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "What follows for food?", "sub": "Philosophy meets the plate", "min": 7}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE SCALE", "title": "The numbers are enormous", "text": "According to the UN Food and Agriculture Organization’s statistics, tens of billions of land animals, over 70 billion, are slaughtered for food each year, most raised in intensive systems. The FAO (2013) also estimated that livestock produce about 14.5% of human-caused greenhouse gas emissions. Whatever your view, the stakes are not small.", "note": "Facts are from the FAO (FAOSTAT and its 2013 report Tackling Climate Change Through Livestock). Avoid getting into contested figures beyond these."}, {"t": "table", "title": "Five views, five plates", "cols": ["View", "What follows for food"], "rows": [["Singer", "Above all, stop supporting factory farming; he leans vegetarian or vegan, while granting hard cases at the margins"], ["Regan", "Using animals for food violates their rights: go vegan"], ["Korsgaard", "Animals are ends in themselves; she argues we should not eat them, and should end factory farming"], ["Kant", "Eating meat is not forbidden; cruelty is"], ["Common sense", "Eating meat is fine; gratuitous cruelty is not"]]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which practice is the main target of Singer’s Animal Liberation?", "options": ["Owning pets", "Industrial factory farming and painful animal experiments", "Hunting by indigenous peoples for survival", "Keeping bees for honey"], "answer": 1, "why": "Singer’s book focuses on where the most suffering occurs: intensive farming and painful experimentation."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "You’re planning your meals for next month. What do you do?", "options": ["Nothing changes", "Avoid factory-farmed products where I can", "Eat much less meat", "Go vegetarian or vegan"], "reveal": "Notice the gap between belief and action. Many people say animal suffering matters but eat as before. Philosophers call a gap like this akrasia, weakness of will, when you act against your own best judgement. That gap is itself worth discussing: is it hypocrisy, or reasonable compromise?", "note": "Stress that this is not a pledge. People may answer privately and change nothing."}, {"t": "section", "num": 6, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "The best objections, aimed at every side", "min": 13}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Morality is a pact (against Singer and Regan)", "text": "Contractualists like Peter Carruthers (1992) argue morality is an agreement among beings who can make and keep claims. Animals can’t, so they have no direct standing, though cruelty may still show bad character."}, {"title": "The human prejudice (against Singer)", "text": "Bernard Williams (2006) argued that favouring humans is not like racism. We can only ever judge from a human point of view, and there is no “point of view of the universe” for us to take."}, {"title": "The wrong kind of argument (against Singer)", "text": "Cora Diamond (1978) argued that we don’t refrain from eating people because of their capacities. It’s what being a fellow human means. Concern for animals should grow from seeing them as fellow creatures, not from capacity checklists."}, {"title": "The predator problem (against equal consideration)", "text": "If a pig’s pain counts equally, so does a gazelle’s. Must we stop lions hunting? If not, why must we stop farms?"}, {"title": "Replaceability (against Singer)", "text": "A utilitarian might allow killing a happy animal painlessly, if it is replaced by another equally happy one. Singer has wrestled with this in Practical Ethics. Does it make “humane meat” acceptable?"}, {"title": "The lifeboat (against Regan)", "text": "If Regan lets the dog go overboard because its death is a smaller harm, he is comparing values after all. Can his rights view survive its own test case?"}, {"title": "The overlap cuts both ways (against the marginal cases argument)", "text": "If status depends on capacities, the argument might lower some humans instead of raising animals. Is it safe to tie moral status to capacities at all?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Make sure the Diamond and Williams cards are taken: they are the subtlest and most often misunderstood. 4 minutes per group, then 1 minute each to report."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "A pig and a human feel exactly the same amount of pain. Does the pig’s pain matter as much?", "options": ["Yes, exactly as much", "It matters, but less than the human’s", "It matters only because of its effects on humans", "It doesn’t matter morally at all"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. If you still say “less”, can you now say why, without just appealing to species? If you say “equally”, how do you answer the predator problem?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Read Singer’s essay “All Animals Are Equal” (1974) and write down the premise you doubt most", "Find out how one food you buy regularly is produced", "Try one fully plant-based day and notice what was hard and what wasn’t", "Answer the boy at the barbecue in a paragraph you’d be happy to read aloud"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Bentham and Singer: what matters is the capacity to suffer, and species alone is no reason to discount pain.", "Kant grounds duties about animals in our own humanity; Korsgaard and Regan argue animals matter in their own right.", "The marginal cases argument presses everyone to say what really gives moral status, and the answer shapes what we eat."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Animal Liberation (1975)", "by": "Peter Singer", "note": "The book that launched the modern animal ethics debate."}, {"title": "The Case for Animal Rights (1983)", "by": "Tom Regan", "note": "The classic rights view, and the lifeboat case."}, {"title": "Fellow Creatures: Our Obligations to the Other Animals (2018)", "by": "Christine Korsgaard", "note": "A Kantian turns Kant’s ethics toward animals."}, {"title": "“Eating Meat and Eating People” (1978)", "by": "Cora Diamond", "note": "A short, profound critique of capacity-based arguments, from someone who cares about animals."}, {"title": "An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation (1789), ch. 17", "by": "Jeremy Bentham", "note": "Read the famous footnote in context."}, {"title": "Other Minds (2016)", "by": "Peter Godfrey-Smith", "note": "A philosopher-diver on octopus minds and where consciousness begins."}], "also": ["The Shallow Pond", "The Lawgiver of Königsberg", "The Last Certainty"]}]}, {"id": "morality", "title": "Is Morality Objective?", "sub": "Realists, relativists, expressivists, Mackie’s error theory and Street’s Darwinian dilemma", "question": "Is morality objective?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can say what it would mean for morality to be objective, explain the main rival views (realism, relativism, expressivism, error theory, constructivism), and judge which survives its strongest objection.", "cast": ["moore", "enoch", "parfit", "harman", "protagoras", "hume", "ayer", "blackburn", "gibbard", "mackie", "street", "korsgaard", "rawls"], "related": {"agora": "realism", "islands": ["sophists", "hume", "vienna", "kant", "nietzsche"], "compass": ["e5", "e4"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "Darius asks a question", "text": "The Greek historian Herodotus tells a story about the Persian king Darius. He called in some Greeks at his court and asked what price would make them eat the bodies of their dead fathers. Nothing in the world, they said. Then he called in the Callatiae, a people from India who did eat their dead parents, and asked, with the Greeks listening through an interpreter, what would make them burn their fathers on a pyre, as the Greeks did. They cried out in horror and begged him not to speak of it. Herodotus drew his moral from a line of the poet Pindar: custom is king of all.", "note": "Source: Herodotus, Histories 3.38. Ask the room at once: who was right, the Greeks or the Callatiae? Then: is there anything about which no culture could be right to disagree?"}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "Facts or feelings?", "min": 8}, {"title": "Is it all culture? Relativism", "min": 9}, {"title": "Boo and hurrah: expressivism", "min": 8}, {"title": "The sceptics: Mackie and Street", "min": 11}, {"title": "Building morality: constructivism", "min": 5}, {"title": "Attack, then your verdict", "min": 9}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "“Torturing a child for fun is wrong.” Which comes closest to your view of that sentence?", "options": ["It states an objective fact, true whatever anyone thinks", "It’s true, but only relative to a culture or a person", "It expresses a strong attitude, not a fact", "Strictly, it’s false: there are no moral facts at all"], "reveal": "Each option is a serious position held by serious philosophers: realism, relativism, expressivism and error theory. By the end you should be able to defend the one you chose against its best objection, or know why you switched."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "Facts or feelings?", "sub": "What would it even mean for morality to be objective?", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE QUESTION", "title": "What “objective” means", "text": "A claim is objective if its truth does not depend on what anyone believes or feels about it. “The Earth orbits the Sun” was true when almost everyone denied it. Moral realists say some moral claims are like that: cruelty for fun would be wrong even if every human approved of it. This branch of philosophy is called metaethics: not what is right, but what rightness is.", "term": "metaethics, from Greek meta (beyond, about) + ēthikos (of character)", "eg": "“Pineapple on pizza is gross” seems to depend on taste. Does “slavery is wrong” work like that, or like “the Earth is round”?", "note": "Give the Earth example and then ask for a moral claim people think is as solid. Most rooms offer cruelty to children; keep that example running all evening."}, {"t": "table", "title": "A map of the positions", "cols": ["View", "Are moral claims true or false?", "Are any true?", "True whatever anyone thinks?"], "rows": [["Realism", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"], ["Relativism", "Yes", "Yes, relative to a group or person", "No"], ["Expressivism", "Not in the plain sense: they express attitudes", "Only in a minimal sense", "No"], ["Error theory", "Yes", "No, all positive ones are false", "No"], ["Constructivism", "Yes", "Yes", "In a sense: fixed by an ideal procedure, not by mind-free facts"]], "note": "Leave this map on screen for a minute. Students often merge expressivism and relativism; the difference is that relativists still think moral sentences state facts (about a culture), expressivists think they don’t state facts at all."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "The case for realism", "items": [{"tag": "CERTAINTY", "title": "Some things are just obviously wrong", "text": "You are more sure that torturing children for fun is wrong than you are of any clever philosophical premise that says otherwise."}, {"tag": "PROGRESS", "title": "Moral progress seems real", "text": "Abolishing slavery looks like getting something right, not just changing fashion."}, {"tag": "DISAGREEMENT", "title": "Arguments seem to be about something", "text": "When people argue over animal rights, each thinks the other is mistaken. That makes sense if there is a fact to be right about."}, {"tag": "REASONS", "title": "Reasons bind you anyway", "text": "Realists such as Derek Parfit and David Enoch argue that some things matter whether or not we care: your future agony gives you a reason to avoid it, now."}]}, {"t": "predict", "q": "What share of professional philosophers do you think accept or lean towards moral realism?", "options": ["Under 25%", "About 40%", "About 60%", "Over 85%"], "reveal": "In the 2020 PhilPapers survey of professional philosophers, roughly 6 in 10 accepted or leaned towards moral realism, and about a quarter towards anti-realism. A majority, but far from a consensus. Of course, a vote of experts doesn’t settle what is true."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which of these is a metaethical question rather than a question of everyday ethics?", "options": ["Should I give a tenth of my income to charity?", "When I say “lying is wrong”, am I describing a fact or expressing a feeling?", "Is eating meat wrong?", "Should I tell my friend the truth about her essay?"], "answer": 1, "why": "Metaethics asks what moral claims are and whether they can be true. The others ask which actions are right."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Is it all culture?", "sub": "Custom may be king. Is it also judge?", "min": 9}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "CULTURAL RELATIVISM", "title": "Right means “approved here”", "text": "Cultural relativism says an action is right or wrong only relative to the norms of a culture. Burning the dead was right for the Greeks, eating them right for the Callatiae. There is no neutral standpoint from which to rank cultures. Anthropologists like Ruth Benedict in the 1930s, struck by human diversity, made such views popular.", "eg": "Driving on the left is right in Japan and wrong in France. Relativists say all of morality is like that."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The argument from disagreement", "items": [{"title": "Cultures disagree deeply about morality", "text": "This is a fact, well documented by historians and anthropologists."}, {"title": "If there were objective moral truth, they would agree", "text": "Or at least converge over time."}, {"title": "So there is no objective moral truth", "text": "Only the codes of different cultures."}], "conclusion": "Tempting, but is premise 2 true? Cultures once disagreed about whether the Earth is flat and what causes disease. Disagreement alone doesn’t show there is no fact of the matter."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is the main flaw in the argument from disagreement, as stated?", "options": ["Disagreement about something does not show there is no truth about it", "Cultures do not really disagree about morality", "It assumes that science is objective", "It proves too little: it shows only that some cultures are wrong"], "answer": 0, "why": "People have disagreed about many objective matters. Some disagreement may come from different factual beliefs too, for example about the afterlife, rather than different values."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Four problems for relativism", "items": [{"tag": "REFORMERS", "title": "Reformers are always wrong", "text": "If right means “approved by my society”, then the first abolitionists were, by definition, morally mistaken."}, {"tag": "PROGRESS", "title": "No progress, only change", "text": "Ending slavery would be no better than starting it, just different."}, {"tag": "CRITICISM", "title": "No outside criticism", "text": "You could not say a genocidal regime was wrong, only that it differs from you. Most relativists don’t want that."}, {"tag": "WHICH GROUP?", "title": "Whose culture?", "text": "You belong to a country, a religion, a school, a family. Which one sets the standard when they clash?"}], "note": "Many of these are drawn together in James Rachels, The Elements of Moral Philosophy, ch. 2. Point out that tolerance cannot be justified by relativism: if relativism is true, tolerance is only right where a culture approves of it."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A society approves of a practice you find cruel. Is it right for them?", "items": [{"title": "A brave minority objects", "text": "A tenth of that society thinks the practice is cruel and says so. Are they wrong just because they are outnumbered?"}, {"title": "It’s your own country, in 1800", "text": "Your own society once approved of practices it now condemns. Was it right for them then?"}, {"title": "A society of one", "text": "One person approves of their own cruelty. If culture can make it right, why not one person’s approval? That is subjectivism."}, {"title": "They’re mistaken about the facts", "text": "They approve because they think the victims feel no pain. Does correcting the fact change the moral verdict?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Where, if anywhere, does relativism stop feeling right?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Boo and hurrah", "sub": "Maybe moral talk isn’t describing anything at all", "min": 8}, {"t": "source", "text": "Take any action allow’d to be vicious: Wilful murder, for instance. Examine it in all lights, and see if you can find that matter of fact, or real existence, which you call vice. In which-ever way you take it, you find only certain passions, motives, volitions and thoughts. There is no other matter of fact in the case. The vice entirely escapes you, as long as you consider the object. You never can find it, till you turn your reflexion into your own breast, and find a sentiment of disapprobation, which arises in you, towards this action.", "src": "David Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40), 3.1.1", "trans": "original English, public domain", "note": "Hume does not say murder is fine. He says the wrongness is found in our feelings, not in the act seen coldly. Scholars still argue whether this makes him an early expressivist, a subjectivist or something subtler."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "EMOTIVISM", "title": "Ayer: wrong means “boo!”", "text": "A. J. Ayer, in Language, Truth and Logic (1936), argued that “stealing money is wrong” states no fact. It is like saying “stealing money!” in a tone of horror. It expresses your disapproval and may stir others to share it. So moral claims are neither true nor false. Later critics nicknamed this the boo/hurrah theory.", "term": "non-cognitivism: moral judgements are not beliefs that can be true or false", "eg": "“Go Lakers!” isn’t true or false. Emotivists think “cheating is wrong” is closer to that than to “the Lakers won”.", "note": "Ayer’s motive was his theory of meaning: only claims verifiable by experience or true by definition say anything. Moral claims are neither, so he concluded they state nothing."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Bea says “eating meat is wrong”; Carl says “eating meat is fine”. According to Ayer’s emotivism, what is going on?", "options": ["They clash in attitude, but neither says anything true or false", "One of them is stating an objective falsehood", "Both are describing their culture accurately", "Each is just reporting a fact about their own feelings"], "answer": 0, "why": "For Ayer there is no fact to be right about: they express opposed attitudes and each may try to change the other’s. He insists this is not reporting one’s feelings, which would be a true-or-false statement (subjectivism)."}, {"t": "puzzle", "q": "Peter Geach (1965) pressed a puzzle using arguments like this one: “If lying is wrong, then getting your brother to lie is wrong. Lying is wrong. So getting your brother to lie is wrong.” It is plainly valid. In the first line nobody is booing lying. Why is that a problem for Ayer?", "a": "Validity needs “lying is wrong” to mean the same in both premises. In the second premise Ayer says it expresses disapproval; in the “if” clause it expresses nothing, since the speaker isn’t condemning lying. So the argument would equivocate, yet it obviously doesn’t. This is the Frege-Geach problem: expressivists owe an account of what moral words mean inside “if”, “not” and “or”.", "note": "Read the argument aloud slowly. Let a few people guess before revealing."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "QUASI-REALISM", "title": "Earning the right to say “true”", "text": "Simon Blackburn and Allan Gibbard developed subtler expressivism. They try to show how attitudes can be combined by a logic of their own, so that we may go on calling moral claims “true” and even saying cruelty would be wrong if we all approved of it. On their view, that last claim does not report our attitudes; it voices our disapproval of cruelty itself, whatever anyone thinks of it. Whether the project fully works is still debated.", "eg": "Like learning to speak a realist’s language fluently without accepting the realist’s view of what the words are made of.", "note": "If time is short, skip this slide and mention quasi-realism in one sentence."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "The sceptics", "sub": "Mackie says moral talk is a mistake; Street says evolution makes realism a lucky guess", "min": 11}, {"t": "quote", "text": "There are no objective values.", "src": "J. L. Mackie, Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong (1977), ch. 1, its opening sentence"}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Mackie’s two arguments", "items": [{"tag": "RELATIVITY", "title": "Codes look like ways of life", "text": "Moral codes vary in ways better explained by people taking part in different ways of life than by some cultures seeing objective values more clearly than others."}, {"tag": "QUEERNESS", "title": "Moral facts would be very strange", "text": "An objective value would have to be something that by its very nature tells you to pursue it. Nothing else in the universe is like that, and we would need a special faculty to detect it."}], "note": "Mackie granted that ordinary moral talk does aim at objective truth. That is why he is an error theorist rather than an expressivist: he thinks ordinary speakers are making a claim, just a false one."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "ERROR THEORY", "title": "Everyone is mistaken, systematically", "text": "Error theory says moral claims try to state objective facts, but there are no such facts, so every positive moral claim is false. Mackie compared morality to a projection: we spread our feelings onto the world and then take them for features of it. He still thought we should keep a moral code; we just invent it, as the subtitle says.", "eg": "Like witch-talk in 1600. “She is a witch” really did claim something, and it was always false, because there are no witches."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "How does Mackie’s error theory differ from Ayer’s emotivism?", "options": ["Mackie thinks moral claims do try to state facts, but are false; Ayer thinks they don’t state facts at all", "Mackie thinks moral claims are true; Ayer thinks they are false", "They are the same view under different names", "Mackie thinks morality is relative to culture"], "answer": 0, "why": "Error theory is cognitivist (moral claims are beliefs that aim at truth) but says they all miss. Emotivism is non-cognitivist (no belief, just attitude)."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Street’s Darwinian dilemma (2006)", "items": [{"title": "Evolution shaped our moral starting points", "text": "We care for our children, return favours and punish cheats largely because ancestors who did so left more descendants."}, {"title": "Horn one: evolution tracked moral truth", "text": "Then selection favoured seeing the truth. But that is bad science: being kind to kin spread because it helped genes, not because it was true."}, {"title": "Horn two: evolution ignored moral truth", "text": "Then it would be a huge coincidence if our evolved values happened to match mind-independent moral facts."}, {"title": "Either way, realism is in trouble", "text": "Either it makes a scientific mistake or it makes our moral knowledge a lucky accident."}], "conclusion": "Sharon Street, “A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value”, Philosophical Studies (2006). Her own answer: value is constructed from our attitudes, so there is nothing to miss.", "note": "Give 2 minutes. The key is horn two: the realist’s values would be right only by luck. Ask the room for an analogy (a compass that was never magnetised but happens to point north)."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What exactly is Street’s target?", "options": ["The view that values are true independently of all our attitudes", "The view that morality evolved at all", "The view that people should be kind", "The theory of evolution"], "answer": 0, "why": "Street accepts evolution and doesn’t argue against kindness. She argues against realism: mind-independent value would be cut off from the forces that actually shaped our values."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "How realists answer back", "items": [{"tag": "COMPANIONS IN GUILT", "title": "Then logic falls too", "text": "Evolution also shaped our sense of good reasoning and of maths. If debunking works on morals, it seems to work on those, which nobody accepts."}, {"tag": "THIRD FACTOR", "title": "Survival and goodness overlap", "text": "David Enoch and others: if survival and well-being are in fact good, then evolution pushing us towards them also pushed us, roughly, towards the truth."}, {"tag": "REFLECTION", "title": "We can correct our instincts", "text": "We have revised evolved biases, like loyalty only to our own tribe, by reasoning. Evolution gave the start, not the finish."}]}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "If you discovered that your strongest moral belief exists mainly because it helped your ancestors survive, would that make you trust it less?", "follow": "Does the same go for your belief that 2 + 2 = 4, or that your eyes are reliable?", "solo": "Write one sentence: does knowing where a belief came from tell you whether it is true?", "note": "Don’t let the room dismiss this too quickly as the genetic fallacy. Street’s point is subtler: the issue is whether the origin of the belief is connected to its truth at all."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Building morality", "sub": "Not found, not just felt, but constructed", "min": 5}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "CONSTRUCTIVISM", "title": "Truth fixed by a procedure", "text": "Constructivists say moral truths are not discovered out there, but neither are they arbitrary. They are what would be agreed or endorsed from the right standpoint: in John Rawls, fair parties choosing principles; in Christine Korsgaard, what any rational agent must commit to just by valuing anything; in Sharon Street, what follows from your own deepest values on reflection.", "eg": "The rules of chess are invented, yet “a bishop moves diagonally” is plainly true and no one can just decide otherwise in the middle of a game.", "note": "Short section: keep to 5 minutes. The chess example works well; ask who invented the rules and whether a player can still get chess wrong."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two kinds of construction", "a": {"label": "KANTIAN", "title": "Binding on every rational agent", "text": "Korsgaard: anyone who acts for reasons is committed to valuing their own humanity, and so, consistently, everyone’s. Morality is the same for all."}, "b": {"label": "HUMEAN", "title": "Built from each person’s values", "text": "Street: what you have reason to do depends on what follows from your own evaluative attitudes. An ideally coherent cruel being might have no reason to be kind."}, "verdict": "The Kantian version gives more objectivity; the Humean version fits better with Darwin. Can you have both?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which statement would a constructivist most likely accept?", "options": ["Moral truths depend on a correct procedure of reasoning or agreement, not on what people happen to think now", "Moral truths are features of the world, like mass and charge", "Moral claims are all false", "Whatever a culture approves is right for that culture"], "answer": 0, "why": "Constructivism sits between realism and relativism: truth is fixed by an idealised standpoint, so actual majorities can be wrong."}, {"t": "section", "num": 6, "title": "Attack, then your verdict", "sub": "Every view has a weak point. Find it.", "min": 9}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the views", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Realism: moral facts explain nothing", "text": "Gilbert Harman (1977): seeing some youths set a cat on fire, you judge it wrong. But your psychology explains that judgement fully; the wrongness itself seems to do no explaining. Why believe in what explains nothing?"}, {"title": "Realism: queer and unknowable", "text": "Mackie and Street together: mind-independent values would be odd things, and our evolved minds would have no way to track them."}, {"title": "Relativism: the reformer’s problem", "text": "If right means approved by your society, every moral pioneer was wrong at the moment they spoke up."}, {"title": "Expressivism: the Frege-Geach problem", "text": "If moral words express attitudes, how can they keep the same meaning inside “if”, “not” and arguments, so that moral reasoning is valid?"}, {"title": "Error theory: the Moorean reply", "text": "You are more certain that torturing a child for fun is wrong than you are of the premises of any argument concluding it isn’t. So reject a premise."}, {"title": "Street: debunking spreads", "text": "Our standards of logic and evidence evolved too. If evolution undermines moral realism, does it undermine the reasoning in Street’s own argument?"}, {"title": "Constructivism: a dilemma", "text": "Why that procedure? If because it tracks real moral truth, it collapses into realism. If because we happen to like it, it collapses into relativism."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Ask each group to start by naming which view their card targets and who in the room holds it. Groups with Harman often need a hint: compare with physics, where electrons do explain our observations."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "“Torturing a child for fun is wrong.” Which comes closest to your view of that sentence?", "options": ["It states an objective fact, true whatever anyone thinks", "It’s true, but only relative to a culture or a person", "It expresses a strong attitude, not a fact", "Strictly, it’s false: there are no moral facts at all"], "reveal": "Compare with your first vote. Notice that almost nobody in the room thinks such torture is acceptable: the dispute is about what kind of claim that is. Constructivists may have found no perfect option here; if so, say which one came nearest and why."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week.", "options": ["Catch yourself saying “that’s just your opinion” about a moral question, and ask: do I really believe that?", "Read chapter 1 of Mackie’s Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong and find the premise you would reject", "Ask someone from a different background about a moral belief they hold that surprises you, and look for the shared value underneath", "Read Herodotus 3.38 and write a reply from the Callatiae’s side"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Disagreement alone doesn’t show there is no moral truth, and relativism struggles with reformers and progress.", "Expressivists say moral talk voices attitudes; error theorists say it states facts that don’t exist.", "Street’s Darwinian dilemma and Harman’s cat press realists hardest; companions-in-guilt arguments are their best defence."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Ethics: Inventing Right and Wrong, ch. 1", "by": "J. L. Mackie (1977)", "note": "The classic case for error theory, short and sharp."}, {"title": "The Elements of Moral Philosophy, ch. 2", "by": "James Rachels", "note": "The clearest beginner’s critique of cultural relativism."}, {"title": "Language, Truth and Logic, ch. 6", "by": "A. J. Ayer (1936)", "note": "Emotivism in a few bold pages."}, {"title": "“A Darwinian Dilemma for Realist Theories of Value”", "by": "Sharon Street (2006)", "note": "The most discussed debunking argument of recent decades."}, {"title": "Taking Morality Seriously", "by": "David Enoch (2011)", "note": "A robust defence of non-natural realism."}, {"title": "Ruling Passions", "by": "Simon Blackburn (1998)", "note": "Quasi-realism explained by its inventor."}], "also": ["The Persuaders (Protagoras)", "The Harbour of Habit (Hume)", "Rebuilding the Ship at Sea (Ayer)", "The Lawgiver of Königsberg (Kant)", "Six Thousand Feet Beyond (Nietzsche)"]}]}, {"id": "freewill", "title": "Do We Have Free Will?", "sub": "Determinism, Frankfurt’s secret chip, Libet’s lab and the case against blame", "question": "Do we have free will?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can explain the consequence argument, the compatibilist reply and the hard incompatibilist view of punishment, say what Libet-style experiments do and do not show, and defend their own position against its strongest objection.", "cast": ["spinoza", "hume", "locke", "vaninwagen", "kane", "chisholm", "frankfurt", "pfstrawson", "pereboom", "gstrawson", "lewis", "dennett"], "related": {"agora": "freewill", "islands": ["spinoza", "hume", "existentialists", "stoa"], "compass": ["m3", "s1"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The envelope", "text": "Imagine a café near your school running an experiment. Before you walk in, a machine scans your brain and writes a prediction in a sealed envelope: tea or coffee, croissant or nothing. You deliberate, change your mind twice, and order. The barista opens the envelope. It is right. It has been right for a thousand customers in a row. Nobody forced you. You weighed reasons, you felt the pull of both drinks, you chose. And yet the choice could be read before you made it. Was it still yours? Tonight’s question is far older than any machine: if everything you do has causes, can you ever act freely?", "note": "Make clear at once that no real machine can do this. It is a thought experiment; section 3 looks at what real brain experiments can and cannot predict."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The rewind: determinism and its rivals", "min": 12}, {"title": "Free after all? The compatibilists", "min": 12}, {"title": "The lab: did your brain decide first?", "min": 8}, {"title": "No free will: now what?", "min": 10}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then your verdict", "min": 13}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Do we have free will?", "options": ["Yes, and the future is genuinely open", "Yes, even if everything we do is caused", "No, not the kind that matters for blame and praise", "I can’t decide yet"], "reveal": "These four answers map onto tonight’s main positions: libertarianism, compatibilism, hard incompatibilism and honest doubt. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey of professional philosophers (Bourget and Chalmers), about 59% accepted or leaned towards compatibilism, about 19% libertarianism and about 11% no free will. Remember your answer: we vote again at the end.", "note": "Count hands and write the numbers on the board. You will need them for the closing vote."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The rewind", "sub": "If the universe replayed this morning exactly, could you have chosen differently?", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "DETERMINISM", "title": "Everything fixed by what came before", "text": "Determinism is the thesis that the state of the world at one moment, together with the laws of nature, fixes everything that happens afterwards. In 1814 Pierre-Simon Laplace imagined an intelligence that knew every force and the position of every particle: for it, he wrote, nothing would be uncertain, and the future would be as present to it as the past.", "term": "“Laplace’s demon” is a later nickname; Laplace himself just said “an intelligence”", "eg": "A row of dominoes: once the first one falls and the spacing is set, the last one’s fate is already settled.", "note": "Someone will raise quantum physics. Short answer: on standard interpretations some events are chancy, on others (such as Bohm’s) they are not, and physicists still disagree. Then ask: would random atomic jumps in your brain make your choices more yours? That sets up the luck problem."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The consequence argument", "items": [{"title": "Determinism", "text": "If determinism is true, everything you do follows from the laws of nature plus the state of the world long before you were born."}, {"title": "The past is not up to you", "text": "Nothing you can do now changes what happened in 1800."}, {"title": "The laws are not up to you", "text": "You cannot make gravity weaker or change how neurons fire."}, {"title": "So", "text": "If something follows from things that are not up to you, it is not up to you either."}], "conclusion": "If determinism is true, nobody could ever have done otherwise, so nobody has free will. This is incompatibilism: free will and determinism cannot both be true. (Peter van Inwagen, An Essay on Free Will, 1983.)"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which claim does every incompatibilist accept?", "options": ["If determinism is true, no one acts freely", "Physics has proven determinism true", "Every human choice is random", "Humans have free will"], "answer": 0, "why": "Incompatibilism is only the claim that free will and determinism cannot both hold. Some incompatibilists then deny free will; others deny determinism."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Two ways to be an incompatibilist", "items": [{"tag": "LIBERTARIAN", "title": "We are free, so determinism is false", "text": "At least some of our choices are not fixed in advance. (This has nothing to do with political libertarianism.)"}, {"tag": "HARD DETERMINIST", "title": "Determinism is true, so we are not free", "text": "Baron d’Holbach argued in 1770 that humans are links in nature’s chain of causes, like everything else."}, {"tag": "HARD INCOMPATIBILIST", "title": "Either way, we are not free", "text": "Derk Pereboom adds: if our choices are random rather than determined, that doesn’t make them ours either."}]}, {"t": "versus", "title": "How could an undetermined choice be yours?", "a": {"label": "EVENT-CAUSAL", "title": "Robert Kane", "text": "In hard, torn choices, which Kane calls “self-forming actions”, indeterminism in the brain leaves the outcome open. Whichever way you go, you made it happen by your own effort, and you endorse it."}, "b": {"label": "AGENT-CAUSAL", "title": "Thomas Reid, Roderick Chisholm", "text": "You, the agent, are a cause that is not itself caused by earlier events. Chisholm (1964) said that when we act, each of us is a kind of unmoved first mover."}, "verdict": "The worry for both is the luck objection: if the past and the laws are identical and the outcome still differs, what explains the difference except chance?"}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "The universe is rewound to just before you chose tea this morning, every atom and every thought exactly as it was.", "items": [{"title": "Sometimes coffee", "text": "On some replays you pick coffee, with no difference in your reasons or feelings. Is that freedom, or a coin toss inside your head?"}, {"title": "Always tea", "text": "Every replay ends the same way, because tea really is what you wanted. Does that make the choice unfree?"}, {"title": "A torn choice", "text": "Swap tea for something hard: telling the truth will cost you a close friend. You struggle for an hour. Does an open outcome look more like freedom now?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "The third step is Kane’s point: indeterminism looks least like luck when you are torn between reasons you care about. Let the room test whether that works."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "If your choice on the replay could come out either way, what would make it yours rather than luck?", "follow": "Is “I could have done otherwise” really what you care about when you feel free?", "solo": "Write one sentence: what would have to be true of you for a choice to be truly yours?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Free after all?", "sub": "Maybe freedom never meant escaping causes in the first place", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "COMPATIBILISM", "title": "The opposite of free is not caused but forced", "text": "Compatibilists say free will and determinism can both be true. Freedom is acting from your own desires and reasons, without coercion. The unfree cases are the gun to your head, the locked cell, the compulsion you cannot resist. Hobbes, Hume and Mill held versions of this, and so do most philosophers today.", "eg": "John Locke’s example: a man is carried, asleep, into a room with someone he longs to talk to, and the door is locked. He stays willingly, though he could not have left (Essay II.xxi.10). Locke calls this staying voluntary but not free; later compatibilists such as Frankfurt took the case further."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On a classic compatibilist view, which person does NOT act freely?", "options": ["Someone handing over their wallet at gunpoint", "Someone giving to charity because they care", "Someone choosing tea because they prefer it", "Someone whose generous character came from a kind upbringing"], "answer": 0, "why": "For the compatibilist, what removes freedom is coercion or compulsion, not having causes. The other three act from their own desires, whatever caused those desires."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "By liberty, then, we can only mean a power of acting or not acting, according to the determinations of the will", "src": "David Hume, An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding (1748), Section VIII, Part I"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "FRANKFURT CASES", "title": "The secret chip", "text": "Harry Frankfurt (1969) imagined cases like this. Black wants Jones to do something and secretly fits a device in his brain. If Jones shows any sign of deciding otherwise, the device will step in. But Jones decides to do it on his own, for his own reasons, and the device never acts. Jones could not have done otherwise. Is he responsible?", "note": "Most groups say yes. Ask why. The answer that matters: what explains his action is his own reasoning, and the unused device changes nothing about that."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "What the chip is meant to show", "items": [{"title": "The principle", "text": "Many assume: you are morally responsible only if you could have done otherwise. Frankfurt called this the principle of alternate possibilities."}, {"title": "The case", "text": "Jones could not have done otherwise: the chip guaranteed the outcome."}, {"title": "The verdict", "text": "Yet Jones seems fully responsible, since he acted for his own reasons."}], "conclusion": "So the principle is false. Responsibility depends on the actual causes of what you did, not on alternatives. If so, the consequence argument may not threaten responsibility at all."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What do Frankfurt cases mainly challenge?", "options": ["The idea that responsibility requires being able to do otherwise", "The idea that brains cause actions", "The idea that determinism is true", "The idea that manipulating people is wrong"], "answer": 0, "why": "The chip removes every alternative without touching the actual reasons Jones acts on, so if he is still responsible, alternatives were not what responsibility required."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Freedom as the right inner structure", "items": [{"tag": "FRANKFURT 1971", "title": "Wanting what you want", "text": "Persons have desires about their desires. The unwilling addict hates his craving; the willing addict endorses it. Only the first acts against his own will."}, {"tag": "FISCHER AND RAVIZZA 1998", "title": "Responsive to reasons", "text": "You are morally responsible when the process you act from would respond to good reasons, for example if you would change course on learning it would hurt someone."}, {"tag": "P. F. STRAWSON 1962", "title": "Resentment and gratitude", "text": "Blame lives in our reactions to each other’s good or ill will. No theory about determinism could make us give these up, or should."}]}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Both addicts will take the drug tonight. One hates the craving; the other is glad of it. Is one of them freer than the other?", "follow": "Does your answer support compatibilism or embarrass it?", "solo": "Write one sentence saying which addict is freer, if either, and why."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Ordinary people are told about a universe where every event is fully determined. A man there robs a bank. Is he morally responsible?", "options": ["Yes, he is responsible", "No, he couldn’t help it"], "reveal": "It depends on how you ask. Eddy Nahmias and colleagues (2005) found most people said yes. Shaun Nichols and Joshua Knobe (2007) found that, asked abstractly, most said nobody in such a universe is fully responsible, while given a vivid crime, most said the criminal was. Common sense seems to pull both ways."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "The lab", "sub": "Did your brain decide before you did?", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "LIBET 1983", "title": "The brain moves first", "text": "Benjamin Libet asked volunteers to flex a wrist whenever they felt like it and to note, on a fast clock, when they first felt the urge. A build-up of brain activity, the readiness potential, began about half a second before the movement. People reported the urge only about a fifth of a second before it. The brain seemed to start before the person decided.", "term": "Bereitschaftspotential (German, “readiness potential”), first measured by Kornhuber and Deecke in 1965", "note": "Libet himself did not conclude that free will is an illusion. He suggested we keep a veto: the conscious power to stop a movement already under way, sometimes called “free won’t”."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Four reasons for caution", "items": [{"tag": "TRIVIAL CHOICE", "title": "Nothing at stake", "text": "Flexing a wrist whenever you like involves no reasons. Deciding whether to lie or move abroad is very different."}, {"tag": "THE CLOCK", "title": "Timing the urge", "text": "Reporting the exact moment you felt an urge is itself unreliable, and small errors matter at this scale."}, {"tag": "NOISE", "title": "Maybe not a decision", "text": "Aaron Schurger and colleagues (2012) modelled the readiness potential as random neural fluctuation drifting towards a threshold. The build-up may not be a decision at all."}, {"tag": "WHO IS “YOU”?", "title": "Your brain is you", "text": "If unconscious processes start a choice, they are still your processes. Why assume you are only the conscious part?"}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Even if Libet’s timings were exactly right, which conclusion would still NOT follow?", "options": ["All our choices, including slow, reasoned ones, are made unconsciously", "In this task, brain activity began before the reported urge", "People’s reports of when they decide deserve scrutiny", "Spontaneous movements have measurable neural precursors"], "answer": 0, "why": "The experiment studied sudden, reason-free movements. Jumping from that to every choice, including long deliberations, is a big extrapolation."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Hands up if anything in this section moved your view. Which way did it move?", "solo": "Write one sentence: does neuroscience matter to the free will question, or is it really a philosophical question?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "No free will: now what?", "sub": "If nobody deserves blame, what happens to prisons, praise and love?", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "HARD INCOMPATIBILISM", "title": "No one deserves blame, in the deep sense", "text": "Derk Pereboom argues that if our actions are determined, they are not up to us in the way needed for basic desert; if they are random, they are not up to us either. So nobody deserves blame or praise just because of what they did. Many forward-looking practices survive: warning, protecting, reforming, reconciling.", "term": "basic desert: deserving blame or reward simply because of what you did, not because it will do any good"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Pereboom think indeterminism would not rescue free will?", "options": ["A random event in the brain is no more under the agent’s control than a determined one", "Indeterminism has been ruled out by physics", "Random events cannot happen in brains", "Indeterminism would make everyone a libertarian"], "answer": 0, "why": "Chance is not control. If a choice tips one way by luck, the agent did not settle it, so it gives no more basis for desert than determinism."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Why punish?", "a": {"label": "RETRIBUTION", "title": "Because they deserve it", "text": "Punishment is owed to the guilty in proportion to the wrong, whether or not it does any good. Kant is the classic defender."}, "b": {"label": "QUARANTINE", "title": "Pereboom’s model", "text": "We isolate people carrying dangerous diseases they did not choose. Likewise we may detain dangerous offenders, but only as much as safety requires, with no suffering for its own sake and with rehabilitation as the aim."}, "verdict": "Critics ask: without desert, what stops us detaining people who are dangerous but have done nothing yet?"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "NOT FATALISM", "title": "Your choices still make a difference", "text": "Fatalism says the outcome will happen whatever you do. Determinism says it happens because of what you do. Even in a deterministic world, your deliberation is one of the causes in the chain. Remove it and the outcome changes.", "eg": "Studying for an exam still matters. If determinism is true, it was settled that you would pass, but settled partly by the fact that you studied."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A friend says: “If determinism is true, there’s no point revising, the result is already fixed.” What is the best reply?", "options": ["Your revising is one of the causes that fixes the result", "Determinism has been disproved", "Results are random anyway", "Revising never makes a difference"], "answer": 0, "why": "This is the fatalist confusion. Determinism does not say the result comes regardless of your actions; it says your actions are part of how it comes about."}, {"t": "source", "text": "Imagine a stone that some outside cause has set moving. Now suppose the stone, while it keeps moving, could think, and knew that it was trying as hard as it could to keep moving. This stone, aware only of its own effort, would believe itself completely free, and think it kept moving for no other reason than that it wanted to. That is the human freedom everyone boasts of having. It consists only in this: people are aware of their desires and ignorant of the causes that determine them.", "src": "Baruch Spinoza, Letter 58, to G. H. Schuller (1674), abridged", "trans": "our translation", "note": "Spinoza is no gloomy determinist. For him the only real freedom is understanding the causes that move you, which frees you from hatred and blame."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "A man with no history of wrongdoing suddenly starts committing crimes. Doctors find a tumour pressing on his frontal lobe; once it is removed, the behaviour stops. What follows?", "options": ["He was not responsible: treat, don’t punish", "He was less responsible", "He was fully responsible", "Then nobody is ever responsible: all behaviour has brain causes"], "reveal": "A real case like this was reported by neurologists Jeffrey Burns and Russell Swerdlow (Archives of Neurology, 2003). Hard incompatibilists ask: if this cause excuses, why not all the causes we can’t yet see? Compatibilists reply that the tumour destroyed his capacity to respond to reasons, while ordinary causes do not."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every position here has a wound. Find the deepest one.", "min": 13}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "The luck objection (against libertarians)", "text": "If the past and the laws are fixed and the choice could still go either way, nothing about you settles which way it goes. Then the outcome is luck, not control."}, {"title": "The manipulation argument (against compatibilists)", "text": "Pereboom’s Professor Plum is programmed by scientists to kill, through reasoning that meets every compatibilist test. Step by step, replace the scientists with ordinary upbringing and determinism. Where is the difference that makes the last Plum responsible?"}, {"title": "The chip assumes too much (against Frankfurt)", "text": "If the device reacts to an early sign, Jones can still produce the sign or not. If the device simply knows in advance, the case assumes determinism, the very point in dispute. (Robert Kane, David Widerker, Carl Ginet.)"}, {"title": "Could have done otherwise, after all (against the consequence argument)", "text": "David Lewis (1981) argued you can be able to act differently even if determinism is true: had you done so, a law of nature would have been slightly different, though you would not have broken any law yourself."}, {"title": "Quarantine has no brakes (against hard incompatibilism)", "text": "Desert limits punishment: you may not punish the innocent or punish too hard. Without desert, what stops a state detaining the merely dangerous, or punishing harshly when it deters?"}, {"title": "We cannot live it (against hard incompatibilism)", "text": "P. F. Strawson: resentment, gratitude and love are not a theory we could drop on learning a fact about physics. But is that an argument, or a confession?"}, {"title": "The Basic Argument (against everyone who believes in free will)", "text": "Galen Strawson: you do what you do because of how you are. To be truly responsible for that, you would have to have made yourself how you are, which requires an earlier self, and so on for ever. So no one is ultimately responsible."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "With fewer groups, prioritise the luck objection, the manipulation argument and the Basic Argument: one each against libertarians, compatibilists and everyone. Collect verdicts on the board."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Do we have free will?", "compare": "v1", "options": ["Yes, and the future is genuinely open", "Yes, even if everything we do is caused", "No, not the kind that matters for blame and praise", "I can’t decide yet"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. If you moved, which argument moved you? If you didn’t, which objection is now the one you most need to answer?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["Next time you blame someone, list three causes behind what they did that they did not choose. Does the blame change?", "Watch a small decision, like when to get up, and try to catch the moment you decide", "Ask a friend the rewind question and work out which camp they are in", "Read Galen Strawson’s short paper “The Impossibility of Moral Responsibility” (1994) and try to refute it"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Incompatibilists say determinism rules out free will; libertarians then deny determinism, hard incompatibilists deny free will.", "Compatibilists say freedom is acting from your own reasons without coercion, and Frankfurt cases suggest responsibility needs no alternatives.", "Libet-style experiments show neural build-up before simple movements, but they cannot settle the philosophical question alone."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "A Contemporary Introduction to Free Will", "by": "Robert Kane (2005)", "note": "The clearest map of every position, by a leading libertarian."}, {"title": "Freedom of the Will and the Concept of a Person", "by": "Harry Frankfurt (1971)", "note": "Short, famous paper on willing and unwilling addicts."}, {"title": "Freedom and Resentment", "by": "P. F. Strawson (1962)", "note": "Why blame may not depend on metaphysics at all."}, {"title": "Just Deserts: Debating Free Will", "by": "Daniel Dennett and Gregg Caruso (2021)", "note": "A compatibilist and a free will sceptic argue it out in dialogue."}, {"title": "Free Will, Agency, and Meaning in Life", "by": "Derk Pereboom (2014)", "note": "Hard incompatibilism and the quarantine model in full."}, {"title": "Ethics, Part I Appendix and Parts IV–V", "by": "Baruch Spinoza (1677)", "note": "Freedom as understanding necessity."}], "also": ["God or Nature (Spinoza)", "The Harbour of Habit (Hume)", "Condemned to Be Free (the existentialists)", "The Inner Citadel (the Stoics)"]}]}, {"id": "identity", "title": "Will You Still Be You?", "sub": "Theseus’ ship, Locke’s memories, Parfit’s teleporter and the Buddha’s chariot", "question": "What makes you the same person over time?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can explain and test the memory, psychological, animalist and no-self views of personal identity, and say whether, with Parfit, they think identity is what matters in survival.", "cast": ["locke", "reid", "hume", "parfit", "williams", "shoemaker", "olson", "buddha", "hobbes"], "related": {"agora": "identity", "islands": ["locke", "parfit", "buddha", "elea", "hume"], "compass": ["m4", "m2"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The oldest photo", "text": "Find the oldest photo of yourself you can, maybe a toddler on a beach. Look at that child. Nearly everything has changed: your height, your face, your voice, what you believe and love and fear. You probably remember nothing of that day. Much of the matter in your body has been swapped out many times since. And yet you point and say, without hesitation, “That’s me.” Now look forward. Will the person who has your name in seventy years, with different beliefs and few of your memories, be you? What exactly makes it true that someone in the past or future is you?", "note": "If people have photos on their phones, ask them to find one. It makes the puzzle personal at once."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The ship and the self", "min": 8}, {"title": "Memory makes the person: Locke", "min": 12}, {"title": "Beam me up: Parfit", "min": 14}, {"title": "An animal, or no self at all?", "min": 11}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then your verdict", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "What makes you the same person as the child in that photo?", "options": ["The same living body and brain", "A continuous chain of memory and personality", "A soul or inner self that doesn’t change", "Nothing deep: “same person” is just a convenient way of talking"], "reveal": "Roughly: the animalist view, the psychological view, the soul view and the Buddhist or reductionist view. Among professional philosophers the psychological view is the most popular, but far from a consensus. Hold on to your answer: we vote again at the end.", "note": "Write the counts on the board for the closing vote."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The ship and the self", "sub": "If you replace every plank, is it the same ship?", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE SHIP OF THESEUS", "title": "One plank at a time", "text": "Plutarch reports that the Athenians kept the ship of the hero Theseus for centuries, replacing rotten timbers with new ones, so that philosophers argued over it as an example of things that grow: some said it was still the same ship, others that it was not (Life of Theseus 23.1).", "eg": "A band with none of its founding members, still touring under the same name. Or a grandfather’s axe with a new head and a new handle."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "HOBBES’S TWIST", "title": "Two ships in the harbour", "text": "Thomas Hobbes added a twist (De Corpore II.11.7). Suppose someone keeps every old plank as it is removed, then reassembles them in the original order. Now there are two ships. One has continuity: it never stopped sailing. The other has the original material and design. Which is the ship of Theseus?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Hobbes’s version make the puzzle harder?", "options": ["Two ships now have a good claim: one by continuity, one by original material", "It proves the repaired ship was never the real one", "It shows wood decays faster than people think", "It shows identity depends on who owns the ship"], "answer": 0, "why": "With one ship, continuity wins easily. With two, our two criteria for sameness, continuity and original parts, point to different ships."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Two kinds of “same”", "items": [{"tag": "QUALITATIVE", "title": "Exactly alike", "text": "Two new phones of the same model are qualitatively identical: you can’t tell them apart, but there are two."}, {"tag": "NUMERICAL", "title": "One and the same", "text": "The phone you bought last year is numerically identical to the scratched one in your pocket: one thing, much changed."}, {"tag": "THE QUESTION", "title": "Persistence", "text": "Tonight’s question is about numerical identity: what makes a later person one and the same as an earlier one, despite qualitative change?"}]}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 2 MIN", "q": "Hands up: which is the real ship of Theseus, the repaired one or the rebuilt one? Now: does the question feel more urgent if the thing being rebuilt is you?", "follow": "Why might there be no fact of the matter for ships, but surely one for people?", "solo": "Write one sentence on which ship you would call the real one, and why."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Memory makes the person", "sub": "John Locke: you reach back as far as your consciousness reaches", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "LOCKE 1694", "title": "Same man, same person: not the same thing", "text": "In the second edition of his Essay (1694), John Locke separated the man, a living human organism, from the person, a thinking being that can consider itself as itself at different times and places. What makes the same person, he argued, is the same consciousness: being able to look back on past thoughts and actions as your own.", "term": "person, in Locke, is “a forensic term”: it is about who can be held responsible (Essay II.xxvii.26)"}, {"t": "quote", "text": "as far as this consciousness can be extended backwards to any past action or thought, so far reaches the identity of that person", "src": "John Locke, An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, II.xxvii.9"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE PRINCE AND THE COBBLER", "title": "Swap the minds", "text": "Locke imagined the soul of a prince, carrying his consciousness of the prince’s past life, entering the body of a cobbler as the cobbler’s own soul departs. Everyone, he says, would see that this is the same person as the prince, though to others it would look like the same man as the cobbler (Essay II.xxvii.15).", "eg": "Body-swap films work the same way: we follow the memories, not the body."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Locke’s view, who wakes up in the cobbler’s body?", "options": ["The prince, since he has the prince’s consciousness", "The cobbler, since it is the cobbler’s body", "Nobody, since the swap destroys both people", "Both at once"], "answer": 0, "why": "For Locke the person goes with consciousness of the past, not with the body. The body tells you only which man, in his technical sense, you are looking at."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Thomas Reid’s brave officer", "items": [{"title": "The boy", "text": "A boy is flogged for stealing apples from an orchard."}, {"title": "The officer", "text": "Years later, as a young officer capturing an enemy flag, he still remembers the flogging. On Locke’s view, officer = boy."}, {"title": "The general", "text": "As an old general he remembers capturing the flag, but has forgotten the flogging completely. So general = officer, but general ≠ boy."}], "conclusion": "Identity is transitive: if A is B and B is C, then A is C. Locke’s view says the general both is and is not the boy, so it cannot be right as stated. (Reid, Essays on the Intellectual Powers of Man, 1785, Essay III, ch. 6.)"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which logical principle does the brave officer case exploit?", "options": ["Transitivity: if A is B and B is C, then A is C", "Identity of indiscernibles: things exactly alike are one and the same", "Causation: every event has a cause", "Induction: the future will resemble the past"], "answer": 0, "why": "Locke’s criterion links general to officer and officer to boy but not general to boy, which breaks the transitivity that identity must have."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Two objections, two repairs", "items": [{"tag": "REPAIR 1", "title": "Chains, not direct links", "text": "Answer to Reid: you are the same person if linked by overlapping chains of memory, as the general is to the boy through the officer. This is psychological continuity."}, {"tag": "OBJECTION", "title": "Butler’s circle", "text": "Joseph Butler (1736): a real memory, unlike a false one, is a memory of something you yourself did. So memory presupposes identity and cannot define it."}, {"tag": "REPAIR 2", "title": "Quasi-memory", "text": "Sydney Shoemaker and Derek Parfit: define a memory-like state caused in the right way by a past experience, without assuming it was yours. Then build identity from that."}]}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Someone with advanced dementia has lost nearly all their memories. On Locke’s view, are they still the same person? What do you think, and what would their family say?", "follow": "Does it matter whether we ask “is it the same person?” or “who should care for them, and how?”", "solo": "Write one sentence: is a person with severe memory loss still the same person, and what makes you say so?", "note": "Some participants may have relatives with dementia. Keep it respectful and remind the room that this is a question about the theory, not a judgement on anyone’s family."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Beam me up", "sub": "Derek Parfit’s machines: travel, death or something in between?", "min": 14}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "PARFIT 1984", "title": "The teletransporter", "text": "In Reasons and Persons (1984), Derek Parfit asks you to imagine a trip to Mars. A scanner records the exact state of every cell in your body and brain, destroying them as it does. The information goes to Mars at the speed of light, where a replicator builds an exact copy from new matter. The person on Mars has your memories, plans and character, and feels as if they just woke up."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Would you step into the teletransporter to get to Mars?", "options": ["Yes: it’s just very fast travel", "No: it kills me and makes a twin", "I genuinely can’t tell"], "reveal": "Professional philosophers are split, with no clear majority in recent PhilPapers surveys. Parfit’s own answer was that it’s about as good as ordinary survival, and that your hesitation reflects a belief in a deep further fact about identity that he thought does not exist."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "You are scanned on Earth, destroyed, and rebuilt perfectly on Mars.", "items": [{"title": "The branch line", "text": "A new scanner records you without destroying you. You are still on Earth, talking to your replica on Mars by video. But the scan damaged your heart: you will die in a few days. (Parfit’s Branch-Line Case.) Do you survive?"}, {"title": "The slow version", "text": "Instead of all at once, your cells are replaced with new ones gradually over a year, as happens in part anyway. Is this better than the teleporter?"}, {"title": "Two copies", "text": "A fault means two replicas are built on Mars, both waking up with your memories. Which one is you?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "Most people feel the branch line is death, though the Mars replica is just as like you as in the original case. Push on this: what differs, except that the original is still around?"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "FISSION", "title": "My division", "text": "Parfit borrowed a case from David Wiggins. Your brain’s two halves are transplanted into two new bodies, and each half alone can carry your whole psychology. Two people wake up, each remembering your life. If only one half had survived, that person would surely be you. But you can’t be both, since they are two people with separate futures.", "note": "Some people do live well with one brain hemisphere after surgery, but the full case is a thought experiment."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why can’t you be identical to both people who wake up after the split?", "options": ["Identity is one-to-one: if both were you, they would be each other, but they are two people", "Neither has a full brain, so neither has your memories", "The law only allows one person per name", "One of them must have the original body"], "answer": 0, "why": "If Left is you and Right is you, then Left is Right. But they are two people who could walk in different directions, so at most one, and maybe neither, is you."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Parfit’s conclusion: identity is not what matters", "items": [{"title": "Double success", "text": "Each survivor is related to you exactly as you would be if only one had survived."}, {"title": "One would be enough", "text": "If one survivor would count as you surviving, two can hardly be worse than one: it isn’t like death."}, {"title": "But not identity", "text": "Yet you are identical to neither, since identity can’t hold one-to-two."}], "conclusion": "So what matters in survival is not identity but psychological connectedness and continuity, which Parfit called Relation R. Whether the survivor is strictly “you” is an empty question once all the facts about R are known."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE GLASS TUNNEL", "title": "Liberation, not loss", "text": "Parfit wrote that his old view of himself felt like being inside a glass tunnel, moving faster each year towards darkness at the end. After he came to think that identity is not what matters, the walls of the tunnel disappeared. He cared less about his own death and more about the lives of others (Reasons and Persons, §95, paraphrased).", "eg": "Think of how you feel about a younger self you have little in common with. Parfit thought future selves deserve the same, more relaxed attitude."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "An animal, or no self at all?", "sub": "Two radical answers: you are a living organism, or there is no “you” to find", "min": 11}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Where do you go in a brain transplant?", "a": {"label": "PSYCHOLOGICAL VIEW", "title": "You go with your mind", "text": "Move your cerebrum into another body, and the person who wakes up with your memories and plans is you. Sydney Shoemaker’s 1963 case of Brown and Brownson made this vivid."}, "b": {"label": "ANIMALISM", "title": "You stay with the animal", "text": "Eric Olson (1997): you are a human organism, and organisms persist by staying alive. The animal left behind, still breathing, is you. You were once a foetus with no psychology at all, and you could survive in a coma."}, "verdict": "Animalism wins on foetuses and comas, and the psychological view wins on transplants and the teleporter. Which cases matter more to you?"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE BUDDHA", "title": "Not-self", "text": "The Buddha taught anattā: look for a permanent, unchanging self and you find only five changing aggregates, body, feeling, perception, mental formations and consciousness, arising and passing away. Clinging to a self that isn’t there, he said, is a root of suffering.", "term": "anattā (Pāli), anātman (Sanskrit): “not-self”; khandha (Pāli): “heap” or aggregate", "eg": "A river keeps its name though no drop of water stays put."}, {"t": "source", "text": "The monk Nāgasena asks King Milinda how he came. “By chariot,” says the king. “Then show me the chariot,” says Nāgasena. “Is the pole the chariot? The axle? The wheels? The frame? Or is the chariot something apart from all these?” To each the king answers no. “Then I cannot find any chariot. Yet you say you came in one.” The king replies that “chariot” is the name used for pole, axle, wheels and frame put together. “Just so,” says Nāgasena: “Nāgasena” is only a name for the body and the aggregates together. In the ultimate sense, no person is found there.", "src": "The Questions of King Milinda (Milindapañha), Book II, ch. 1, §1, abridged", "trans": "our translation", "note": "The text is not saying chariots and people don’t exist at all. It distinguishes conventional truth, where we rightly use names, from ultimate truth, where only the parts and processes are found."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is the chariot comparison claiming?", "options": ["“Self” is a useful name for a collection of parts and processes, with no extra thing behind them", "People don’t exist, so nothing anyone does matters", "The self is a soul hidden inside the body", "The self is the most important part of the body, like the axle"], "answer": 0, "why": "Nāgasena keeps the name for practical life while denying that there is an extra self over and above the aggregates, just as there is no chariot over and above its parts."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "I never can catch myself at any time without a perception, and never can observe any thing but the perception.", "src": "David Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature (1739), 1.4.6"}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Parfit noticed that his view was close to the Buddha’s. If there is no deep, further fact about you, should you fear death less? Care less about your own future and more about other people’s?", "follow": "Is anything lost if we believe this?", "solo": "Write one sentence on whether believing in no deep self would change how you live."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every view of the self has a case it cannot handle. Find it.", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Duplication (against the psychological view)", "text": "Bernard Williams (1956): if psychology could be copied into one new body, it could be copied into two. Identity can’t hold with both, so psychological continuity cannot be what identity consists in."}, {"title": "Fear the torture (against the psychological view)", "text": "Williams (1970): told that tomorrow your memories will be replaced and then your body tortured, you would still dread it. Perhaps you do go with your body after all."}, {"title": "Too many thinkers (against the psychological view)", "text": "Eric Olson: there is a thinking human animal in your chair. If you are not that animal, there are two thinkers there. How do you know which one you are?"}, {"title": "The transplant intuition (against animalism)", "text": "Almost everyone feels they would go where their cerebrum goes. Animalism says you’d stay behind with an empty-headed body. Is that believable?"}, {"title": "Identity matters to me (against Parfit)", "text": "Critics such as Mark Johnston argue that special concern for your own future does not depend on a deep further fact, so losing that fact gives no reason to care less."}, {"title": "Who suffers? (against no-self)", "text": "If there is no self, who is liberated, who remembers, who is responsible for past deeds? Even within Buddhism, the Pudgalavāda school held that the person is neither the same as nor different from the aggregates."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "If short of time, use the duplication, transplant and no-self cards: one against each of the three main views."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "What makes you the same person as the child in that photo?", "compare": "v1", "options": ["The same living body and brain", "A continuous chain of memory and personality", "A soul or inner self that doesn’t change", "Nothing deep: “same person” is just a convenient way of talking"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Then ask the harder question Parfit leaves us with: whatever identity is, is it what you really care about when you hope to survive?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["Write a short letter to yourself in ten years. What do you hope that person still shares with you?", "Ask an older relative what about them has stayed the same since childhood, and what hasn’t", "Read Chapter 10 of Parfit’s Reasons and Persons, the one with the teletransporter", "Ask three friends whether they would use the teletransporter, and why"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Locke made personal identity a matter of consciousness and memory; Reid and Butler found problems that later versions try to repair.", "Parfit’s teleporter and fission cases suggest identity can be indeterminate, and that what matters in survival may be psychological connection, not identity.", "Animalists say you are a living organism; the Buddhist tradition says the self is a name for changing processes."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "A Dialogue on Personal Identity and Immortality", "by": "John Perry (1978)", "note": "A short, lively dialogue that covers almost every view tonight."}, {"title": "An Essay Concerning Human Understanding, Book II, ch. xxvii", "by": "John Locke (1694)", "note": "Where the modern debate begins: the prince and the cobbler."}, {"title": "Reasons and Persons, Part Three", "by": "Derek Parfit (1984)", "note": "The teletransporter, fission and why identity is not what matters."}, {"title": "The Self and the Future", "by": "Bernard Williams (1970)", "note": "A short paper that turns the body-swap intuition upside down."}, {"title": "The Human Animal", "by": "Eric Olson (1997)", "note": "The best defence of animalism."}, {"title": "The Questions of King Milinda", "by": "trans. T. W. Rhys Davids (1890)", "note": "The chariot and other dialogues on not-self."}], "also": ["The White Paper Isle (Locke)", "The Glass Tunnel (Parfit)", "The Tree of Waking (the Buddha)", "Does Anything Change? (Heraclitus and Parmenides)"]}]}, {"id": "mind", "title": "Minds, Brains and Machines", "sub": "Descartes, Mary’s room, Nagel’s bat, Turing’s test and Searle’s Chinese room", "question": "Is the mind just the brain? Could a machine think?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can explain dualism, physicalism and the hard problem of consciousness, weigh the knowledge and zombie arguments, and say whether passing the Turing test would show that a machine thinks or feels.", "cast": ["descartes", "smart", "putnam", "churchland", "nagel", "jackson", "chalmers", "dennett", "turing", "searle", "block"], "related": {"agora": "mind", "islands": ["descartes", "mind", "falsafa"], "compass": ["m1", "m6"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "One neuron at a time", "text": "A surgeon offers you a strange upgrade. Each day she replaces one of your neurons with a tiny silicon chip that does exactly the same job: same inputs, same outputs, same timing. You notice nothing. Your friends notice nothing. Years later, the last neuron goes. You are now made entirely of silicon, and you still say, “What a gorgeous red sunset.” Here is the question. Is anyone still home? Did your experience of red survive, or did the lights fade out somewhere along the way, while the talking carried on? Your answer depends on what a mind is.", "note": "This is adapted from David Chalmers’s “fading qualia” argument (The Conscious Mind, 1996). Return to it in section 4."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "A ghost in the machine? Descartes", "min": 10}, {"title": "Just the brain: physicalism", "min": 10}, {"title": "What science might leave out", "min": 14}, {"title": "Could a machine think?", "min": 12}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then your verdict", "min": 9}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Is your mind just your brain at work?", "options": ["Yes: the mind is what the brain does", "No: there is a non-physical mind or soul", "The brain is physical, but experience has non-physical features", "Not sure yet"], "reveal": "These are physicalism, substance dualism and property dualism, plus honest doubt. In the 2020 PhilPapers Survey, about half of professional philosophers accepted or leaned towards physicalism about the mind and about a third rejected it. Remember your vote: we will come back to it.", "note": "Put the counts on the board for the closing vote."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "A ghost in the machine?", "sub": "René Descartes: you are a thinking thing, lodged in a body", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SUBSTANCE DUALISM", "title": "Two kinds of stuff", "text": "In his Meditations (1641), Descartes argued that the world contains two kinds of substance. Matter is extended: it takes up space and can be divided. Mind thinks: it doubts, wills, imagines and feels, and takes up no space at all. You are essentially a mind, joined to a body.", "term": "res cogitans (Latin, “thinking thing”) and res extensa (“extended thing”)", "eg": "You can cut a brain in half. Can you cut a thought, a wish or a doubt in half?"}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The argument from clear conception", "items": [{"title": "Conceiving", "text": "I can clearly and distinctly conceive of my mind existing without my body."}, {"title": "Possibility", "text": "Whatever I can clearly and distinctly conceive apart, God could make exist apart."}, {"title": "Distinctness", "text": "Things that could exist apart are really distinct."}], "conclusion": "So my mind is really distinct from my body, and could exist without it (Meditations VI, simplified). Notice the second step: it is the bridge from what we can conceive to what is possible. It comes back with the zombies in section 3."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A student argues: “I can doubt my brain exists, but I can’t doubt my mind exists. So my mind isn’t my brain.” What’s wrong with this?", "options": ["What you can doubt is a fact about your knowledge, not about the thing itself", "Nobody can doubt that their brain exists", "Minds can be doubted too", "Nothing: it proves dualism"], "answer": 0, "why": "Lois Lane can doubt that Clark Kent can fly while being sure Superman can, yet they are one man. Philosophers call this the masked man fallacy."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "I am not lodged in my body only as a pilot is in his ship, but am very closely joined to it and, as it were, mixed up with it", "src": "René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy (1641), Meditation VI, our translation"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "ELISABETH’S CHALLENGE", "title": "How can a ghost push a body?", "text": "In 1643 Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia wrote to Descartes with a sharp question. Moving a body seems to need contact and extension, but the mind, on his view, has no extension at all. So how can a thinking thing make an arm move? Unconvinced by his reply, she wrote that it would be easier to grant matter and extension to the soul.", "note": "This interaction problem is still the main objection to substance dualism. Descartes said the union of mind and body is known best through ordinary life, not through philosophy."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Is there anything about you that you think brain science could never explain? What is it?", "follow": "Is that because science hasn’t got there yet, or because it couldn’t in principle?", "solo": "Write one sentence naming something about your mind you doubt brain science could ever explain, and why."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Just the brain", "sub": "Physicalism: everything about the mind is physical", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE CASE FOR PHYSICALISM", "title": "Change the brain, change the mind", "text": "Anaesthetics switch consciousness off. Strokes can remove the ability to recognise faces or to see motion. Coffee, alcohol and sleep loss reshape your moods and thoughts. Every mental change we know of seems to come with a brain change. Physicalism says this is because the mind is something the brain does, not a second thing attached to it.", "eg": "A tiny lesion in one area of the brain can leave someone unable to recognise their own mother’s face, while still able to recognise her voice."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three ways to be a physicalist", "items": [{"tag": "IDENTITY THEORY", "title": "Pain is a brain state", "text": "U. T. Place (1956) and J. J. C. Smart (1959): mental states just are brain processes, as lightning just is electrical discharge."}, {"tag": "FUNCTIONALISM", "title": "Mind is what it does", "text": "Hilary Putnam (1967): a mental state is defined by its role, what causes it and what it causes, not by what it is made of. Pain is whatever plays the pain role."}, {"tag": "ELIMINATIVISM", "title": "Folk psychology may be false", "text": "Paul and Patricia Churchland: talk of beliefs and desires is an old theory that neuroscience may replace, as chemistry replaced alchemy."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why did many philosophers move from the identity theory to functionalism?", "options": ["An octopus, an alien or a robot might feel pain with very different hardware", "The identity theory was shown to be dualist", "Brain scans proved that pain has no location", "Functionalism says the mind is non-physical"], "answer": 0, "why": "If pain just is one kind of human brain state, creatures with different brains could not feel pain. Functionalism allows the same mental state to be realised in many physical systems."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The causal argument for physicalism", "items": [{"title": "Minds make things happen", "text": "Your pain causes you to pull your hand from the stove."}, {"title": "Physics is complete", "text": "Every physical event that has a cause has a sufficient physical cause: the hand moves because of muscles, nerves and neurons."}, {"title": "No double causes", "text": "Your actions are not routinely caused twice over, once by the mind and once by the brain."}], "conclusion": "So the pain must itself be physical. (A version of the argument defended by Jaegwon Kim and David Papineau.) Dualists must deny a premise, and each denial has a cost."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "What science might leave out", "sub": "Bats, a colour scientist and zombies", "min": 14}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "NAGEL’S BAT", "title": "The view from inside", "text": "Nagel (1974) said a creature has conscious experience when there is “something that it is like to be that organism”. Bats find their way by echolocation. We could learn everything about a bat’s brain and still not know what it is like for the bat to perceive this way. Experience has a subjective point of view; physical science describes things from no point of view.", "note": "Common confusion: Nagel did not conclude that physicalism is false. He said we do not yet understand how it could be true."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE HARD PROBLEM", "title": "Why does it feel like anything?", "text": "David Chalmers (1995) separated the easy problems of consciousness, explaining how the brain detects, reports and attends to things, from the hard problem: why all that processing is accompanied by experience at all. The easy problems are very hard in practice, but at least we know what an answer would look like.", "eg": "A thermostat detects heat and responds. You detect heat and also feel warm. Why the feeling?"}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Mary’s room", "items": [{"title": "The setup", "text": "Mary is a brilliant scientist raised in a black-and-white room. Through books and screens she learns every physical fact about colour and colour vision."}, {"title": "The release", "text": "One day she walks out and sees a ripe red tomato for the first time."}, {"title": "The intuition", "text": "She seems to learn something new: what it is like to see red."}], "conclusion": "If she knew all the physical facts and still learned a new fact, then not all facts are physical, and physicalism is false. (Frank Jackson, “Epiphenomenal Qualia”, 1982.)"}, {"t": "predict", "q": "When Mary sees red for the first time, does she learn something new?", "options": ["Yes, something new about the world", "She learns something, but it isn’t a new fact", "No, she already knew it all"], "reveal": "Most people say yes at first. Jackson himself later changed his mind and became a physicalist. The middle answer is the most popular physicalist reply, coming next."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "How physicalists answer Mary", "items": [{"tag": "ABILITY", "title": "New know-how", "text": "David Lewis, Laurence Nemirow: Mary gains abilities, to recognise, imagine and remember red, not new facts. Like learning to ride a bike."}, {"tag": "NEW CONCEPT", "title": "Old fact, new angle", "text": "Brian Loar and others: Mary gains a new way of thinking about a physical fact she already knew, as when you learn that the Morning Star is the Evening Star."}, {"tag": "DENIAL", "title": "She wouldn’t be surprised", "text": "Daniel Dennett: we cannot really imagine knowing all the physical facts. If Mary did, she might well know exactly what red would be like."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "According to the ability reply, what does Mary gain when she leaves the room?", "options": ["New skills, such as recognising and imagining red, but no new facts", "A new non-physical fact about red", "Nothing at all", "Proof that dualism is true"], "answer": 0, "why": "The ability reply says knowing what red is like is know-how, not knowledge of a fact, so Mary’s learning doesn’t show there are non-physical facts."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "ZOMBIES", "title": "All the lights, nobody home", "text": "Chalmers (1996) asks you to imagine a zombie twin: physically identical to you, atom for atom, behaving exactly as you do, but with no experience at all. If such a being is even possible, then experience is something over and above the physical, and physicalism is false.", "term": "philosophical zombie: nothing to do with horror films; it acts normally, but there is nothing it is like to be it", "note": "Remind the room of Descartes’s second step: the zombie argument also moves from what is conceivable to what is possible."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which step of the zombie argument do most physicalists reject?", "options": ["That what is conceivable is therefore possible", "That zombies would behave like us", "That you are conscious", "That physics describes the brain"], "answer": 0, "why": "Before chemistry, water without H₂O might have seemed conceivable, yet it isn’t possible. Physicalists say zombies may be like that: imaginable, but not really possible."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Could a machine think?", "sub": "Turing’s test, Searle’s room, and the machines that talk back", "min": 12}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v2", "label": "VOTE · 1 MIN", "q": "Could a machine ever have a real mind: actually understand and feel, not just act as if it does?", "options": ["Yes, if it does the right processing", "Only if it is built from the right kind of stuff", "Never", "We could never know"], "reveal": "The first option is the functionalist view, the second is Searle’s, the third is the dualist’s, and the fourth is a worry about evidence that troubles everyone. Keep your answer: we return to it at the end."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE IMITATION GAME", "title": "Turing’s test", "text": "“I propose to consider the question, ‘Can machines think?’” So begins Alan Turing’s 1950 paper in Mind. Finding the question too vague, he replaced it with a game: a judge types questions to a hidden human and a hidden machine. If the judge can’t reliably tell which is which, the machine passes. Turing predicted that by about 2000, an average judge would have at most a 70% chance of picking correctly after five minutes.", "note": "Turing himself discussed objections, including Lady Lovelace’s: that a machine only does what we tell it to. Participants who raise it are in good company."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Searle’s Chinese room", "items": [{"title": "The room", "text": "John Searle, who reads no Chinese, sits in a room with a huge rulebook. Chinese questions come in; he matches symbols by their shapes and passes back Chinese answers."}, {"title": "The result", "text": "To people outside, the answers are perfect. The room passes the test in Chinese."}, {"title": "The point", "text": "But Searle understands nothing. He only shuffles symbols by their shapes. A computer running a program does the same."}], "conclusion": "Running a program, which is pure syntax, is not enough for understanding, which needs meaning. (John Searle, “Minds, Brains, and Programs”, 1980.)"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which claim does Searle NOT make?", "options": ["No machine of any kind could ever think", "Running a program is not enough for understanding", "Passing the Turing test does not prove understanding", "Brains produce minds through their causal powers"], "answer": 0, "why": "Searle says brains are machines that think. His target is the idea that the right program alone is enough, whatever it runs on."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Replies to the Chinese room", "items": [{"tag": "SYSTEMS", "title": "The whole room understands", "text": "Searle is like one neuron. The understanding belongs to the system: man, rulebook and all. Searle: then let me memorise the rulebook; I still understand nothing."}, {"tag": "ROBOT", "title": "Connect it to the world", "text": "Give the program cameras and arms, so its symbols link to real things. Searle: that only adds more symbols coming in."}, {"tag": "INTUITION", "title": "The room misleads us", "text": "Dennett and the Churchlands: a real version would be vast and fast. Our gut feeling about one slow man may not scale."}]}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "TALKING MACHINES", "title": "When the machine says it feels", "text": "Today’s chatbots are trained on huge amounts of human writing, so they can talk fluently about feelings. That is exactly what we would expect whether or not anything is felt, so their words alone are weak evidence. Some researchers instead check how a system is built against scientific theories of consciousness. Others think only the right biology could ever be enough.", "note": "Keep the discussion on principles, not on any particular company or product. The point is that behaviour, the Turing test’s evidence, is exactly what such systems are trained to produce."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "Your neurons are replaced one by one with chips that do exactly the same job.", "items": [{"title": "Half and half", "text": "Halfway through, half your brain is silicon. Is your experience of red half as vivid? If it were, why do you still say it’s vivid?"}, {"title": "All silicon", "text": "Every neuron is now a chip. Is anyone home?"}, {"title": "A simulation", "text": "Now no chips at all: a laptop calculates what every neuron would do. Same outputs, no physical copy of a brain. Is that a mind?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change?", "note": "Chalmers argues that if experience faded while behaviour stayed the same, you would be badly wrong about your own experience, which seems absurd. Searle would say the simulation step is where the mind disappears."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Suppose experts can’t tell whether a future AI feels anything. How should we treat it?", "follow": "What is worse: treating a feeling being as a tool, or treating a tool as a feeling being?", "solo": "Write one sentence on how we should treat a machine if we can’t tell whether it feels."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "No theory of mind gets out of here unhurt", "min": 9}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "The interaction problem (against dualism)", "text": "If the mind is non-physical, how does it move neurons? And why does every mental change track a brain change, even sleep, drugs and damage?"}, {"title": "The explanatory gap (against physicalism)", "text": "Joseph Levine (1983): even a complete account of the brain leaves it mysterious why those processes feel like anything. Correlation is not explanation."}, {"title": "My zombie twin talks too (against the zombie argument)", "text": "Your zombie twin would write the same essays about its inner light. If your beliefs about consciousness are caused just as its are, what makes yours knowledge?"}, {"title": "Illusionism (against the hard problem)", "text": "Keith Frankish and Dennett: experience only seems to have special, non-physical qualities. Explain why it seems so, and nothing is left over."}, {"title": "Blockhead (against the Turing test)", "text": "Ned Block (1981): a giant lookup table holding a sensible reply to every possible conversation would pass, yet it plainly does not think."}, {"title": "The systems reply (against Searle)", "text": "No single neuron understands English either. Why should it matter that the man in the room doesn’t understand Chinese, if the system does?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "If short of time, use the interaction problem, the explanatory gap and Blockhead: one against each side and one about machines."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Is your mind just your brain at work?", "compare": "v1", "options": ["Yes: the mind is what the brain does", "No: there is a non-physical mind or soul", "The brain is physical, but experience has non-physical features", "Not sure yet"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Which argument tonight pulled hardest on you: the bat, Mary, the zombies or the interaction problem?"}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Could a machine ever have a real mind: actually understand and feel, not just act as if it does?", "compare": "v2", "options": ["Yes, if it does the right processing", "Only if it is built from the right kind of stuff", "Never", "We could never know"], "reveal": "Notice how your two answers fit together. Physicalists can still disagree about machines, and the deepest disagreement is about what kind of evidence could ever settle it."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one to try this week.", "options": ["Spend five minutes describing a single colour or taste in words to someone. What can’t you get across?", "Read Nagel’s “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?”: it is short and still argued about", "Have a conversation with a chatbot and note what, if anything, would convince you it understood", "Ask three people whether the silicon-replaced you would still be conscious"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Dualists say the mind is non-physical; physicalists say it is what the brain does; the interaction problem and the hard problem trouble each side.", "Nagel’s bat, Mary’s room and the zombie argument suggest physical facts might leave experience out; physicalists have serious replies.", "The Turing test checks behaviour; Searle argues behaviour and programs are not enough for understanding."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?", "by": "Thomas Nagel (1974)", "note": "The classic short paper on subjective experience."}, {"title": "Computing Machinery and Intelligence", "by": "Alan Turing (1950)", "note": "Readable, witty and surprisingly modern."}, {"title": "Minds, Brains, and Programs", "by": "John Searle (1980)", "note": "The Chinese room, with replies from critics."}, {"title": "Consciousness: A Very Short Introduction", "by": "Susan Blackmore", "note": "A fast, lively overview of the science and philosophy."}, {"title": "Meditations on First Philosophy, II and VI", "by": "René Descartes (1641)", "note": "Where the modern mind-body problem begins."}, {"title": "The Conscious Mind", "by": "David Chalmers (1996)", "note": "The hard problem, zombies and fading qualia in full."}], "also": ["The Last Certainty (Descartes)", "The Bat and the Room (Nagel, Jackson, Chalmers, Dennett, Searle)", "The House of Wisdom (Avicenna’s flying man)"]}]}, {"id": "knowing", "title": "Can You Know Anything?", "sub": "Dreams, demons, vats and the hands in front of your face", "question": "Can you know anything at all?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can explain the dream, demon and brain-in-a-vat arguments, state the closure argument for skepticism, compare Moore’s, Putnam’s and the Pyrrhonists’ responses, and explain what a Gettier case shows about knowledge.", "cast": ["descartes", "putnam", "bostrom", "moore", "sextus", "pyrrho", "zhuangzi"], "related": {"agora": null, "islands": ["gettier", "descartes", "sceptics", "montaigne", "agora", "dao"], "compass": ["k4", "k11"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The butterfly", "text": "Some 2,300 years ago, the Chinese thinker Zhuangzi dreamt he was a butterfly, fluttering happily about, with no idea he was Zhuangzi. Then he woke up, and there he was, solidly himself. But he could not tell, he says, whether he was Zhuangzi who had dreamt of being a butterfly, or a butterfly now dreaming it was Zhuangzi. Last night you probably dreamt too, and inside the dream it all seemed real. So here is tonight’s question. Right now, reading this, how do you know you are awake? And if you don’t know that, do you know anything at all?", "note": "The story is from Zhuangzi chapter 2. Pause after the last line and let people actually try to answer it silently before voting."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The dream and the demon: Descartes", "min": 10}, {"title": "Vats and simulations", "min": 11}, {"title": "Here is one hand: Moore and his heirs", "min": 9}, {"title": "What is knowledge anyway? Gettier", "min": 9}, {"title": "Living without belief: the Pyrrhonists", "min": 7}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, and your verdict", "min": 9}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Do you know that you have hands?", "options": ["Yes, obviously", "Yes, though I can’t prove it", "No, not strictly: I can’t rule out a dream or a vat", "The question is confused"], "reveal": "Keep your answer in mind. In the 2020 PhilPapers survey of professional philosophers, about four in five accepted “non-skeptical realism” about the external world, and only about one in twenty accepted skepticism. But a majority vote is not an argument. We vote again at the end.", "note": "Ask one person from each option to say, in a single sentence, why. Don’t debate yet."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The dream and the demon", "sub": "Descartes tries to doubt everything, on purpose", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "MEDITATIONS I, 1641", "title": "The method of doubt", "text": "René Descartes wanted knowledge built on rock, not sand. So he set out to doubt every belief that could possibly be false, not because he thought they were false, but to see what would survive. He later compared it (Seventh Replies) to tipping out a basket of apples to throw away the rotten ones before any can spoil the rest.", "eg": "A detective who suspects every witness, even the honest ones, until the evidence clears them."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The dream argument", "items": [{"title": "Premise 1", "text": "When I dream, I often believe I am awake, sitting by the fire, holding this paper."}, {"title": "Premise 2", "text": "There are no certain signs by which I can tell waking from dreaming."}, {"title": "So", "text": "For any moment, I cannot be certain I am not dreaming now."}, {"title": "So", "text": "I cannot be certain my senses tell me how the world really is."}], "conclusion": "But even in dreams, 2 + 3 = 5 and a square has four sides. So Descartes needs a stronger doubt."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE EVIL DEMON", "title": "A deceiver of the utmost power", "text": "Descartes first asks whether an all-powerful God could make him go wrong even when adding two and three. Then he swaps in a malicious spirit, supremely powerful and cunning, who uses all its energy to deceive him: about the sky, colours, sounds, even his own body. Nothing about such a deceiver can be ruled out from the inside. Every experience would feel exactly the same.", "term": "genius malignus (Latin): the evil demon or malicious spirit", "eg": "A video game so perfect that no in-game test could reveal it is a game."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Descartes move on from the dream to the evil demon?", "options": ["Because dreams are too pleasant to doubt", "Because even a dreamer can be sure of simple truths like 2 + 3 = 5, and he wants to doubt those too", "Because he believed demons really exist", "Because the dream argument proves the external world exists"], "answer": 1, "why": "Dreams cast doubt on the senses, but not on arithmetic. The demon is a tool to push doubt as far as it can go."}, {"t": "source", "text": "But there is some deceiver or other, supremely powerful and supremely cunning, who is deliberately deceiving me all the time. Then without doubt I too exist, if he deceives me; let him deceive me as much as he can, he will never bring it about that I am nothing so long as I think I am something. So after weighing everything carefully, I must conclude that this statement, “I am, I exist”, is necessarily true whenever I say it or conceive it in my mind.", "src": "René Descartes, Meditations on First Philosophy II (1641), AT VII 25", "trans": "our translation", "note": "The famous “I think, therefore I am” does not appear in this form in the Meditations; it is in the Discourse on Method (1637) and the Principles (1644). Here it is “I am, I exist”."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why can’t the demon deceive Descartes about “I am, I exist”?", "options": ["Because the demon is not powerful enough", "Because being deceived requires someone who exists to be deceived", "Because Descartes can see his own body", "Because God forbids it"], "answer": 1, "why": "Every act of deceiving needs a victim. The more the demon deceives him, the more certain it is that Descartes, as a thinking thing, exists."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 2 MIN", "q": "Hands up: can you think of any test that would prove, right now, that you are not dreaming?", "follow": "Pinching yourself, reading text twice, checking a clock: could a dream fake each of these?", "solo": "Write down your best test for being awake, then one sentence on how a dream could fake it.", "note": "Collect three tests on the board. For each, ask the room how a dream or demon could fake it. Descartes himself, in Meditation VI, appeals to coherence: waking life hangs together with memory in a way dreams do not."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Vats and simulations", "sub": "Descartes’ demon, rebuilt with wires and code", "min": 11}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE MODERN DEMON", "title": "A brain in a vat", "text": "Imagine scientists remove your brain, keep it alive in a vat of nutrients, and wire it to a computer that feeds it signals exactly like the ones your senses send now. You would see this page, feel your chair, hear voices. Nothing in your experience would differ. So how could you know you are not such a brain?", "eg": "If someone says “but I can feel my hands”, the envatted brain would say exactly the same thing, with the same confidence."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The skeptic’s closure argument", "items": [{"title": "Premise 1", "text": "If I know I have hands, then I know I am not a handless brain in a vat."}, {"title": "Premise 2", "text": "I do not know I am not a brain in a vat: my experience would be the same either way."}, {"title": "So", "text": "I do not know I have hands."}], "conclusion": "Premise 1 relies on closure: if you know P, and know that P rules out Q, you know Q is false. The same pattern works for almost any belief about the world.", "note": "Write this argument on the board. Every response tonight attacks a different line of it: Moore the conclusion, Nozick and Dretske premise 1, Putnam premise 2."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "PUTNAM, REASON, TRUTH AND HISTORY (1981)", "title": "A brain in a vat could not even say it", "text": "Hilary Putnam argued that our words get their meaning from what we are causally connected to. A brain that had always lived in a vat would never have touched a real vat. So its word “vat” would refer to vat-images in its program, not real vats. When it says “I am a brain in a vat”, it says something false. So, Putnam concludes, we cannot truly think we are brains in vats.", "term": "semantic externalism: meaning depends partly on the world outside your head"}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Bostrom’s simulation argument (2003)", "items": [{"tag": "OPTION 1", "title": "Almost no one gets there", "text": "Nearly all civilisations like ours go extinct before they gain the computing power to simulate minds."}, {"tag": "OPTION 2", "title": "Almost no one bothers", "text": "Nearly all civilisations that gain that power choose not to run detailed simulations of their ancestors."}, {"tag": "OPTION 3", "title": "We are almost surely simulated", "text": "If neither is true, simulated minds vastly outnumber real ones, so you are very probably one of them."}], "note": "State it carefully: Bostrom argues that at least one of the three is true. He does not claim we are in a simulation. David Chalmers (Reality+, 2022) adds that even if we were, tables and hands would still be real, just made of digital stuff."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What does Bostrom’s simulation argument actually conclude?", "options": ["That we are living in a computer simulation", "That at least one of three claims is true, one of which is that we are probably simulated", "That simulations cannot contain conscious minds", "That we can prove we are not simulated"], "answer": 1, "why": "It is a trilemma: it narrows the options to three without saying which one is true."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Suppose you learned for certain tomorrow that you live in a simulation. What, if anything, would change in how you live?", "follow": "Would your friends be any less real? Would your promises still bind?", "solo": "Write one sentence on one thing that would change and one that would not.", "note": "This question separates skepticism (we can’t know) from metaphysics (what is real). Push pairs to say which they are talking about."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Here is one hand", "sub": "G. E. Moore turns the argument on its head", "min": 9}, {"t": "versus", "title": "One argument, two directions", "a": {"label": "THE SKEPTIC", "title": "From vats to no hands", "text": "If I knew I had hands, I would know I’m not envatted. I don’t know that. So I don’t know I have hands."}, "b": {"label": "G. E. MOORE", "title": "From hands to no vat", "text": "Raising his hands, Moore said “Here is one hand” and here is another, so external things exist (“Proof of an External World”, 1939). I am more certain I have hands than of any premise in a skeptical argument."}, "verdict": "Both use the same closure principle. The question is which end you are more sure of: “I have hands” or “I can’t rule out the vat”.", "note": "Many students find Moore’s proof a cheat. Ask: is it more reasonable to doubt your hands, or to doubt a clever premise in a philosopher’s argument?"}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "At the zoo, you see a black-and-white striped animal in a pen marked “Zebra”. Do you know it is a zebra?", "items": [{"title": "A friend asks", "text": "A friend says: “Can you rule out that it’s a mule cleverly painted to look like a zebra?” You can’t, from where you stand. Do you still know it’s a zebra?"}, {"title": "Prank zoo", "text": "You learn this zoo once did exactly that, as a prank, last year. Do you know now?"}, {"title": "High stakes", "text": "A stranger offers you a bet: your house against a coffee that it’s a real zebra. Do you take it?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer to “do you know?” change? What changed it?", "note": "The zebra case is Fred Dretske’s (1970). Watch for the pattern: the animal never changes; only the possibilities raised and the stakes do. That is the opening for contextualism, next slide."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "CONTEXTUALISM", "title": "Does “know” shift with the conversation?", "text": "Contextualists, such as David Lewis (“Elusive Knowledge”, 1996) and Keith DeRose, say the standard for “know” rises and falls with context. At the zoo, “I know it’s a zebra” is true. In a philosophy seminar, once vats are raised, the bar soars and the same words become false. Both the skeptic and common sense are right, in their own contexts.", "eg": "“Is the table flat?” Yes for a picnic; no for a machinist measuring to a thousandth of a millimetre."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "How does a contextualist explain the pull of skepticism?", "options": ["Skeptics are simply confused about the meaning of “know”", "Raising skeptical possibilities raises the standard for “know”, so in that context we really don’t know", "We never know anything in any context", "Knowledge depends on whether you feel certain"], "answer": 1, "why": "The skeptic wins inside the seminar room by changing the standards, but loses nothing outside it, where ordinary standards apply."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "What is knowledge anyway?", "sub": "Three pages that broke a definition", "min": 9}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE CLASSIC DEFINITION", "title": "Justified true belief", "text": "Plato’s Meno (97e–98a) suggests that knowledge is true belief tied down by reasoning; his Theaetetus (201c–d) tests “true belief plus an account” but ends without accepting it. Later philosophers summed up the idea as three conditions. You believe it, it is true, and you are justified in believing it.", "term": "JTB: justified true belief", "eg": "A lucky guess that the train is late is true belief. Checking the live timetable makes it knowledge, or so it seems."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Lucky truths", "items": [{"tag": "GETTIER, 1963", "title": "Ten coins", "text": "Smith has strong evidence that Jones will get the job and that Jones has ten coins in his pocket. So he believes: “The man who gets the job has ten coins in his pocket.” In fact Smith gets the job and, unknown to him, Smith has ten coins too."}, {"tag": "RUSSELL, 1948", "title": "The stopped clock", "text": "You glance at a clock that has always been reliable. It says two o’clock, and it is two o’clock. But the clock stopped exactly twelve hours ago."}], "note": "Edmund Gettier’s paper “Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?” (Analysis, 1963) is three pages long. The clock case is from Bertrand Russell’s Human Knowledge (1948), long before Gettier."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "In the stopped clock case, do you know it is two o’clock?", "options": ["Yes", "No", "Kind of"], "reveal": "Most people say no. Early surveys suggested this intuition might vary across cultures, but larger later studies (e.g. Machery and colleagues, 2017) found it widely shared. Yet the belief is true and justified. So justified true belief is not enough for knowledge."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What do Gettier cases show?", "options": ["That justified beliefs are usually false", "That a belief can be justified and true and still not be knowledge", "That we know nothing at all", "That clocks are unreliable"], "answer": 1, "why": "In each case, all three conditions are met, but the truth is a matter of luck. Something more, or something different, is needed."}, {"t": "flow", "title": "Ways to repair the definition", "items": [{"title": "No false steps", "text": "Knowledge cannot rest on a false belief (Smith’s belief rested on “Jones will get the job”)."}, {"title": "Tracking", "text": "If it weren’t true, you wouldn’t believe it (Nozick, 1981). The stopped clock fails: it would say two even at three."}, {"title": "Safety", "text": "Your belief could not easily have been false."}, {"title": "Knowledge first", "text": "Stop defining knowledge; treat it as basic, and explain belief and evidence by it (Timothy Williamson, 2000)."}], "note": "Sixty years on, there is still no agreed repair. That itself is a lesson worth naming."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Living without belief", "sub": "The ancient skeptics who suspended judgement and found peace", "min": 7}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SEXTUS EMPIRICUS, c. 200 CE", "title": "Suspend, and be calm", "text": "The Pyrrhonists, named after Pyrrho of Elis, did not claim “nothing can be known”: that is itself a claim. Instead, for any question, they set equally strong arguments against each other, suspended judgement, and found that calm followed. Sextus says it happened like the painter Apelles, who flung his sponge at a painting in frustration and by chance created the horse’s foam he had failed to paint (Outlines of Pyrrhonism I.28).", "term": "ἰσοσθένεια (isostheneia): equal force; ἐποχή (epochē): suspension of judgement; ἀταραξία (ataraxia): tranquillity"}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Agrippa’s trap: every reason needs a reason", "items": [{"tag": "REGRESS", "title": "Reasons forever", "text": "Each reason needs another reason behind it, without end."}, {"tag": "CIRCLE", "title": "Reasons in a loop", "text": "Eventually a reason depends on the very thing it was meant to support."}, {"tag": "ASSUMPTION", "title": "Reasons that just stop", "text": "We halt at a claim we simply assume. But an opponent can assume the opposite."}], "note": "These are three of the Five Modes of Agrippa (Sextus, Outlines of Pyrrhonism I.164–177); the other two are disagreement and relativity. Today this is often called Agrippa’s trilemma."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A Pyrrhonist is asked: “Is it true that nothing can be known?” What do they say?", "options": ["“Yes, that is certain.”", "“No, some things can be known.”", "“I suspend judgement on that too.”", "“Only philosophers can know things.”"], "answer": 2, "why": "Asserting that nothing can be known would be a dogma. Pyrrhonists suspend judgement even about that, and live by how things appear, custom and need (Outlines I.23–24)."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Could you actually live without beliefs, only going by how things seem? Would you be calmer, or stuck?", "follow": "When you cross a road, do you believe the car is coming, or does it just seem to be?", "solo": "Write one sentence on whether you could act without beliefs, with one example."}, {"t": "section", "num": 6, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Skeptics and anti-skeptics both have soft spots", "min": 9}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Self-refuting?", "text": "Against the skeptic: if you claim to know that we can’t know anything, you contradict yourself. If you don’t claim to know it, why should anyone accept it?"}, {"title": "Moore’s hands", "text": "Against the skeptic: any premise in a skeptical argument is less certain than “I have hands”. So rationally we should reject a premise, not our hands."}, {"title": "Closure fails", "text": "Against the closure argument: knowing about hands does not require ruling out every wild possibility. But giving up closure means saying “I know I have hands but not that I’m not a handless brain”. Is that worse?"}, {"title": "Putnam proves too little", "text": "Against Putnam: his argument only works for a brain that was always in a vat. A brain envatted last night still means real vats by “vat”, and could be deceived now."}, {"title": "Who needs knowledge?", "text": "Against the whole debate: science runs on probabilities and evidence, not certainty. If we can believe reasonably, maybe it doesn’t matter whether we “know”."}, {"title": "The simulation undercuts itself", "text": "Against Bostrom: if we are simulated, our evidence about computers and physics might be fake, so the reasoning that led us to suspect a simulation is undermined."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Give 4 minutes, then 1 minute per group. Ask each group: did the objection hit the skeptic, or the anti-skeptic?"}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Do you know that you have hands?", "options": ["Yes, obviously", "Yes, though I can’t prove it", "No, not strictly: I can’t rule out a dream or a vat", "The question is confused"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Compare with your first vote. Many philosophers land near the second answer: knowledge without proof. That is roughly where Moore, the contextualists and many fallibilists stand. If you moved to the third, you side with the skeptic (though a strict Pyrrhonist would suspend judgement rather than answer “no”), and you will need an answer to “how do you live, then?”", "note": "Ask anyone who changed: which slide or card did it?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Read Descartes’ First Meditation: about ten pages, free online", "Next time you are sure of something, ask: what would I see if I were wrong?", "Try a Pyrrhonist hour: for one disagreement, build the best case for both sides and suspend judgement", "Ask a friend the stopped-clock question and see what they say"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Skeptical arguments work by finding a possibility you cannot rule out from the inside: a dream, a demon, a vat.", "Responses attack different steps: Moore trusts his hands, Dretske and Nozick reject closure, Putnam questions whether the vat thought makes sense, contextualists shift the standard.", "Gettier showed that knowledge is not just justified true belief; the Pyrrhonists asked whether we need knowledge to live well at all."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Meditations on First Philosophy, I–II (1641)", "by": "René Descartes", "note": "The dream, the demon and “I am, I exist”, in about twenty pages."}, {"title": "Outlines of Pyrrhonism, Book I", "by": "Sextus Empiricus", "note": "How the ancient skeptics actually lived."}, {"title": "“Proof of an External World” (1939)", "by": "G. E. Moore", "note": "The famous hands; shorter and odder than you expect."}, {"title": "“Is Justified True Belief Knowledge?” (1963)", "by": "Edmund Gettier", "note": "Three pages that changed epistemology."}, {"title": "“Are You Living in a Computer Simulation?” (2003)", "by": "Nick Bostrom", "note": "Read the argument itself, not the headlines about it."}, {"title": "What Does It All Mean? (1987), chapter 2", "by": "Thomas Nagel", "note": "A gentle, short introduction to the whole problem."}], "also": ["The Last Certainty", "The Painter’s Sponge", "The Tower of Doubt", "The Uncarved Block"]}]}, {"id": "meaning", "title": "Does Life Need a Meaning?", "sub": "Camus’ Sisyphus, Nagel’s absurd, Wolf’s fitting fulfilment and Nozick’s machine", "question": "Does life need a meaning to be worth living?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can distinguish meaning from happiness and morality, explain the main answers (religious, existentialist, subjectivist, Wolf’s hybrid view, Camus’ revolt) and say whether a life without meaning could still be worth living.", "cast": ["camus", "nagel", "tolstoy", "augustine", "sartre", "richard_taylor", "wolf", "nozick", "metz", "kierkegaard"], "related": {"agora": "meaning", "islands": ["existentialists", "schopenhauer", "kierkegaard", "garden"], "compass": ["l1", "l3", "l8"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The stone and the hill", "text": "In Greek myth, King Sisyphus cheated the gods, even death itself. His punishment in the underworld was simple. He must push a huge stone up a hill. Each time it nears the top, it rolls back down, and he must walk down after it and begin again. Forever. Homer already describes him straining at the stone in the Odyssey. The gods knew what they were doing: no punishment is worse than useless, hopeless work. Now look at your own week. Alarm, school or work, food, sleep, repeat. Is your stone so different?", "note": "Source for the image: Homer, Odyssey 11.593–600. Keep the last question light; some participants may be in a hard place. Say once that if the topic stirs something heavy, talking to someone they trust is a good idea."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The absurd: Camus and Nagel", "min": 12}, {"title": "Meaning from above: God and the infinite", "min": 8}, {"title": "Make your own: existentialists and subjectivists", "min": 9}, {"title": "Fitting fulfilment and the experience machine", "min": 11}, {"title": "Attack, then your verdict", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Does a life need a meaning to be worth living?", "options": ["Yes: without meaning, a life isn’t worth living", "Meaning makes life better, but happiness or love can be enough", "No: “the meaning of life” is a confused idea", "Life is worth living, but its meaning is something we make"], "reveal": "Keep this answer. Philosophers split along similar lines: some think meaning must come from God, some that we create it, some that it needs objective value, and a few that life is absurd and we should live on anyway."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The absurd", "sub": "Why does the question even arise?", "min": 12}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three ways a life can go well", "items": [{"tag": "HAPPINESS", "title": "It feels good from inside", "text": "Pleasure, contentment, satisfaction. A cat in the sun can have plenty."}, {"tag": "MORALITY", "title": "It treats others rightly", "text": "Keeping promises, avoiding harm. A dull but decent life can score well."}, {"tag": "MEANING", "title": "It is about something", "text": "Engagement with something worth caring about: a craft, a cause, people, a faith. Susan Wolf argues this is a third value, not reducible to the other two."}], "note": "Test the distinction: could someone be happy but live a meaningless life? Meaningful but unhappy? Most rooms quickly name examples of both, such as a devoted carer worn out by caring."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "CAMUS", "title": "The absurd is a collision", "text": "Albert Camus, in The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), says the absurd is not in us or in the world, but in their meeting. We are creatures who demand reasons, and the universe answers with silence. One day the routine breaks, someone asks “why?”, and the stage set collapses. His essay opens by saying the one truly serious philosophical problem is whether life is worth living at all.", "term": "l’absurde (French): the clash between our need for meaning and the world’s indifference", "eg": "Like shouting a question into a canyon and hearing only your own echo.", "note": "Camus’ famous opening line in the 1955 English version is copyrighted, so we paraphrase. He means: before any other question, decide whether life is worth living at all."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Camus’ three exits, and the one he takes", "items": [{"tag": "ESCAPE 1", "title": "Ending life", "text": "Camus rejects it: it does not answer the absurd, it just abolishes one side of it."}, {"tag": "ESCAPE 2", "title": "Philosophical suicide", "text": "Leaping into a faith or system that promises meaning beyond reason. Camus names Kierkegaard among others and calls this giving up lucidity."}, {"tag": "HIS ANSWER", "term": "la révolte", "title": "Revolt", "text": "Live with your eyes open, without appeal to anything beyond, with freedom and passion. Keep the tension; don’t resolve it."}]}, {"t": "quote", "text": "One must imagine Sisyphus happy.", "src": "Albert Camus, The Myth of Sisyphus (1942), the essay’s final sentence; our translation of “Il faut imaginer Sisyphe heureux”", "note": "Ask: why “must”? Is it a description, a hope, or a command? Camus thinks Sisyphus’ scorn and lucidity on the walk down make him master of his fate."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "According to Camus, what is the right response to the absurd?", "options": ["To live fully while refusing false comfort, keeping the absurd in view", "To find a religion that gives life meaning", "To conclude that life is not worth living", "To prove the universe has a purpose after all"], "answer": 0, "why": "Camus rejects both ending life and the leap of faith. Revolt means living lucidly and passionately without pretending the question has been answered."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "NAGEL", "title": "Not too small, not too short", "text": "Thomas Nagel, in “The Absurd” (1971), rejects the usual reasons. We are tiny and brief, but if a life is absurd now, being huge or living a million years would not help. The real source is a clash inside us: we take our lives seriously, yet we can always step back and see that seriousness as arbitrary. He recommends neither heroism nor despair, but irony.", "eg": "You agonise over a grade. Then, for a second, you see yourself from the stars: one ape worrying about marks on paper. Then you go back to worrying.", "note": "Nagel’s point surprises people: ask the room first why life might seem absurd, collect “we’re tiny” and “we die”, then show that Nagel rejects both."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Nagel think living forever would not cure absurdity?", "options": ["Because an endless life could be viewed from outside as arbitrary too, just at greater length", "Because immortality is impossible", "Because only short lives can be happy", "Because God would be needed to make life eternal"], "answer": 0, "why": "Nagel locates absurdity in our capacity to step back from any commitment, not in time or size. A million years of arbitrary seriousness is still arbitrary seriousness."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Hands up: if you knew everything you did would be forgotten in a thousand years, would it matter less to you today?", "follow": "What about a hundred years? Ten?", "solo": "Write one sentence: does how long something lasts change whether it matters?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Meaning from above", "sub": "Does it take God, or something infinite, to make a life count?", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "TOLSTOY’S CRISIS", "title": "Rich, famous, and stopped cold", "text": "In A Confession (1882), Leo Tolstoy describes how, around fifty, with a loving family, great wealth and world fame, he was struck by questions he could not answer: why? and then what? Everything he did would end in death and forgetting. He tells an old Eastern fable of a traveller clinging to a branch over a pit, licking honey while mice gnaw the branch. He found his way out in faith.", "eg": "Achieving everything you hoped for, and finding the trophy empty.", "note": "Tolstoy found faith by looking at the lives of ordinary believing peasants, not by an argument. Ask whether that counts as an answer or a way of living past the question."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "Our heart is restless until it rests in you.", "src": "Augustine, Confessions I.1 (c. 397–400 CE); our translation of “inquietum est cor nostrum donec requiescat in te”"}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Where can meaning come from?", "a": {"label": "SUPERNATURALISM", "title": "Only through God or a soul", "text": "A life has meaning by fulfilling God’s purpose or by relating to an infinite, lasting good. Without this, everything ends in nothing."}, "b": {"label": "NATURALISM", "title": "Possible in a purely physical world", "text": "Love, knowledge, art and helping others can make a life meaningful even if there is no God. Some naturalists make this depend on our attitudes, some on objective value."}, "verdict": "This is how Thaddeus Metz organises the field in Meaning in Life (2013). Many religious thinkers hold that God adds meaning, not that nothing else has any.", "note": "Point out a middle position: some theists think human projects already have meaning, and God deepens or secures it."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A critic says: “If God created me for a purpose, I’m like a tool made for a job. That doesn’t sound meaningful; it sounds degrading.” What is the best reply on behalf of the religious view?", "options": ["The purpose might be one that is genuinely good for us and freely embraced, like a loving relationship rather than a tool’s function", "Tools have very meaningful lives", "Purpose has nothing to do with meaning", "There is no reply; the view collapses"], "answer": 0, "why": "Most theists say God’s purpose for humans is communion and flourishing, offered to free beings. Whether that answers the worry is debated, but it is the standard reply."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Does anything need to last forever to matter? Think of a meal shared with friends, a sandcastle, a life saved.", "follow": "If not, why did Tolstoy feel death drained everything of meaning?", "solo": "Write one sentence on something that mattered to you even though it didn’t last.", "note": "Both believers and non-believers usually find things here to say. Avoid letting this turn into a debate on whether God exists; that is a different seminar."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Make your own", "sub": "If no meaning is given, can we simply choose one?", "min": 9}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SARTRE", "title": "Existence precedes essence", "text": "In Existentialism Is a Humanism (a 1945 lecture), Jean-Paul Sartre compares people to a paper-knife. A paper-knife’s maker had its purpose in mind before it existed. If there is no God, no one designed you: you exist first and define yourself afterwards by what you do. That is a burden and a freedom. You can’t blame your nature, and no one can hand you a purpose.", "term": "l’existence précède l’essence (French)", "eg": "A hammer is for hammering before anyone buys it. A newborn is not yet for anything."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Sartre say a person, unlike a paper-knife, has no essence to begin with?", "options": ["Because, with no God as designer, there was no plan for a person before they existed", "Because humans are made of matter and knives are not", "Because people have souls that come first", "Because essence only applies to natural objects"], "answer": 0, "why": "A paper-knife’s purpose existed in its maker’s mind first. Without a divine maker, Sartre says, we exist first and define ourselves by our choices."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "TAYLOR’S TWIST", "title": "A Sisyphus who loves rolling stones", "text": "Richard Taylor, in Good and Evil (1970), imagines the gods being merciful in a strange way: they implant in Sisyphus a burning desire to roll stones. Now he gets exactly what he wants, endlessly. Taylor concludes that the meaning of life comes from within us: it is the doing of what we long to do, not some lasting result.", "eg": "Like a child happily building sandcastles all afternoon, not caring that the tide will take them.", "note": "Taylor thought Sisyphus is a picture of us: our projects also come to nothing in the long run, yet, like the stone-loving Sisyphus, we are driven to want them."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Is Taylor’s stone-loving Sisyphus living a meaningful life?", "options": ["Yes: he gets what he truly wants", "No: his work is still pointless, however he feels", "It’s happy, but not meaningful"], "reveal": "Taylor says yes. Most people hesitate, which is the opening for objective and hybrid views like Susan Wolf’s, coming next. Your answer is a test of whether you are a subjectivist about meaning."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "Someone devotes their whole life, with complete joy, to an activity.", "items": [{"title": "Counting blades of grass", "text": "John Rawls imagines a brilliant person whose only pleasure is counting blades of grass in parks and lawns (A Theory of Justice, 1971, §65). Meaningful?"}, {"title": "Collecting rubber bands", "text": "They build the world’s biggest rubber-band ball. Does a world record change your answer?"}, {"title": "Caring for a dying friend", "text": "Same joy and devotion, but the activity is caring for someone. Did the verdict jump?"}, {"title": "A novel no one reads", "text": "They write a masterpiece, then it is lost unread in a fire. Did the meaning burn with it?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? If it did, then you think meaning depends on more than how the person feels."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "If you judged the grass-counter’s life less meaningful than the carer’s, even though both were equally joyful, which view are you leaning towards?", "options": ["That meaning depends partly on the worth of what you do, not only on your feelings", "Pure subjectivism: whatever you love gives meaning", "Nihilism: no life has meaning", "Hedonism: only pleasure matters"], "answer": 0, "why": "Subjectivism says equal passion gives equal meaning. If the object of the passion matters to your verdict, you think meaning has an objective side too."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Fitting fulfilment", "sub": "Loving what is worth loving, and the machine that fakes it", "min": 11}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SUSAN WOLF", "title": "When attraction meets attractiveness", "text": "In Meaning in Life and Why It Matters (2010), Susan Wolf offers a hybrid view: “meaning arises when subjective attraction meets objective attractiveness.” You must be actively engaged with, even gripped by, what you do, and it must be genuinely worthwhile. Feeling alone is not enough; worth alone is not enough.", "eg": "A violinist who loves playing has both. A violinist forced to practise, who hates it, has the worth without the engagement.", "note": "Wolf leaves “objective worth” open-ended on purpose; she does not give a list. Expect this to be the first objection; it is on a critique card later."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three ways to miss meaning, on Wolf’s view", "items": [{"tag": "EMPTY", "title": "Gripped by nothing", "text": "Drifting through days of TV and snacks, caring about nothing in particular. Wolf’s example is a figure she calls “the Blob”."}, {"tag": "MISDIRECTED", "title": "Gripped by something worthless", "text": "Pouring your heart into something with no value beyond your liking for it: the grass-counter, Taylor’s Sisyphus."}, {"tag": "ALIENATED", "title": "Worthwhile, but heartless", "text": "Doing valuable work you feel nothing for, going through the motions of a good job or a good cause."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Wolf’s view, which life is most clearly meaningful?", "options": ["A nurse who finds her work absorbing and helps patients recover", "A man who feels deeply fulfilled spending every day memorising phone books", "A lawyer who does valuable work but is bored and detached throughout", "A person who feels nothing and does nothing in particular"], "answer": 0, "why": "Only the nurse has both: real engagement and an activity of genuine worth. The others fail on worth, on engagement, or on both."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "NOZICK", "title": "The experience machine", "text": "Robert Nozick, in Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), imagines a machine that can give you any experiences you like: writing a great novel, making friends, being loved. You float in a tank, wired up, believing it is all real. Would you plug in for life? Nozick thought most of us would not, because we want to actually do things and be someone, not merely to have the feelings.", "eg": "Getting a medal by actually winning the race versus a dream of winning that feels identical."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 1 MIN", "q": "Would you plug into the experience machine for the rest of your life?", "options": ["Yes, plug me in", "No, never", "Only if my loved ones came too", "Only if I could choose to unplug later"], "reveal": "In the 2020 PhilPapers survey, about three quarters of professional philosophers said no. But Felipe De Brigard (2010) found that when people are told they are already in such a machine, many choose to stay. Some of our refusal may be attachment to what we know, not love of reality."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "If you refused to plug in, what exactly would you lose inside the machine, given that it would feel the same?", "follow": "Does your answer show that meaning needs contact with something real outside your head?", "solo": "Write one sentence naming what the machine cannot give you, if anything.", "note": "Link back to Wolf: the machine supplies subjective attraction, but nothing objective to be attracted to. Link to Taylor too: on his view, why not plug in?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack, then your verdict", "sub": "Every answer to the meaning question has a soft spot", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the answers", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Against Camus: revolt is a value too", "text": "If nothing has meaning, why is lucidity better than comforting illusion, or revolt better than giving up? Camus seems to smuggle values into a world he says has none."}, {"title": "Against Nagel: why trust the view from nowhere?", "text": "Stepping back makes everything look arbitrary, but why should that detached view outrank the engaged one? Maybe it just loses sight of what matters."}, {"title": "Against the religious view: a Euthyphro problem", "text": "Is God’s purpose meaningful because it is good, or good because it is God’s? If the first, goodness gives meaning without God; if the second, the purpose looks arbitrary."}, {"title": "Against subjectivism: the grass-counter", "text": "If whatever you love gives meaning, a life of counting grass is as meaningful as one spent curing diseases. Few believe that."}, {"title": "Against Wolf: who decides what is worthwhile?", "text": "“Objective attractiveness” risks ranking lives by the tastes of educated elites. Whose list of worthy pursuits counts, and why?"}, {"title": "Against the question: meaning is optional", "text": "Plenty of people live happy, kind lives without asking about meaning. Maybe meaning is a luxury concern, and a good life needs only joy and decency."}, {"title": "Against life itself: the pessimist", "text": "Schopenhauer held that life is mostly striving and suffering. Even a meaningful life might not be worth its pain. Does meaning answer that, or only distract from it?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "The pessimist card can be heavy. Give it to a group that seems confident, and frame it as a philosophical position with strong replies, not a mood."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "Does a life need a meaning to be worth living?", "options": ["Yes: without meaning, a life isn’t worth living", "Meaning makes life better, but happiness or love can be enough", "No: “the meaning of life” is a confused idea", "Life is worth living, but its meaning is something we make"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Notice the gap between two questions you may have merged at the start: does the universe give life a meaning, and can a life have meaning in it? Many people answer “no” to the first and “yes” to the second."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week.", "options": ["List three things you do that grip you, and ask of each: is it worth it, by Wolf’s test?", "Read Camus’ short essay “The Myth of Sisyphus” (the final chapter, a few pages) and decide if Sisyphus is happy", "Spend one hour on something that matters to you but leaves no trace: a talk, a walk, a meal with someone", "Ask an older person you trust what made their life feel worthwhile, and just listen"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Meaning is distinct from happiness and morality; the absurd (Camus, Nagel) comes from our need for reasons meeting an indifferent world, or our own detached view.", "Answers range from God-given purpose, through self-made meaning (Sartre, Taylor), to Wolf’s fitting fulfilment: engagement with what is truly worthwhile.", "The experience machine suggests most of us want contact with reality, not just good feelings, though the evidence is mixed."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "The Myth of Sisyphus", "by": "Albert Camus (1942)", "note": "The essay that made the absurd famous; read at least the final chapter."}, {"title": "“The Absurd”", "by": "Thomas Nagel (1971), reprinted in Mortal Questions (1979)", "note": "A short, calm analysis of why life can seem absurd."}, {"title": "Meaning in Life and Why It Matters", "by": "Susan Wolf (2010)", "note": "Two clear lectures setting out fitting fulfilment, with replies from critics."}, {"title": "A Confession", "by": "Leo Tolstoy (1882)", "note": "A great novelist’s crisis of meaning, told with brutal honesty."}, {"title": "Anarchy, State, and Utopia, pp. 42–45", "by": "Robert Nozick (1974)", "note": "Where the experience machine first appears."}, {"title": "Man’s Search for Meaning", "by": "Viktor Frankl (1946)", "note": "A psychiatrist’s account of finding meaning in a concentration camp."}], "also": ["Condemned to Be Free (Sartre, Beauvoir, Camus)", "Fear and Trembling (Kierkegaard)", "The Blind Will (Schopenhauer)", "The Quiet Garden (Epicurus)"]}]}, {"id": "death", "title": "Is Death Bad for You?", "sub": "Epicurus, Nagel and the tedium of living forever", "question": "Is death bad for the one who dies?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can state Epicurus’ argument, Lucretius’ symmetry argument and Nagel’s deprivation reply, explain Williams’ case against immortality, and defend their own verdict against the strongest objection to it.", "cast": ["epicurus", "lucretius", "nagel", "williams", "seneca", "epictetus", "marcus_aurelius", "buddha"], "related": {"agora": "death", "islands": ["garden", "stoa", "buddha", "hemlock", "parfit"], "compass": ["l2", "l7", "l6"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "Who is harmed?", "text": "Imagine a sudden, painless death tonight, in your sleep. You feel nothing. You never find out. Tomorrow your friends grieve, your unread messages pile up, and your plans for next summer simply stop. Now the strange question. Your friends are harmed, clearly. But are you? There is no “you” left to feel the loss, to miss anything, to regret. A 2,300-year-old argument says: no one is harmed at all. Death is nothing to the one who dies. Most people find that answer obviously wrong. Very few can say exactly what is wrong with it. Tonight we try.", "note": "Read the story aloud slowly, then go straight to the vote. Don’t let anyone argue yet: we want first instincts on record."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "Nothing to us: Epicurus and Lucretius", "min": 12}, {"title": "What you lose: Nagel’s reply", "min": 12}, {"title": "Would you want forever? Williams", "min": 10}, {"title": "Living with it: Stoics and Buddhists", "min": 8}, {"title": "Attack the arguments", "min": 10}, {"title": "Your verdict and one step", "min": 3}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Is death bad for the one who dies?", "options": ["Yes: it takes away everything life would have given", "Sometimes: it depends on what the rest of life would have held", "No: once you are gone, nothing can be bad for you", "I’m not sure the question even makes sense"], "reveal": "Hold on to your answer. Most people pick the first or second. Epicurus, Lucretius and the Stoics pick the third; Thomas Nagel and many philosophers today pick the first or the second, depending on what more life would have held. We vote again at the end.", "note": "Count hands for each option and write the numbers on the board. You will compare them in the closing vote."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "Nothing to us", "sub": "The ancient argument that fearing death is a mistake", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "EPICURUS, 341–270 BCE", "title": "A cure for fear", "text": "Epicurus ran a school in a garden outside Athens. He thought philosophy was medicine for the mind, and that the worst sickness was fear: of the gods and of death. His goal was calm, untroubled living. His key assumption: the only things that can be good or bad for you are things you can experience, pleasure and pain.", "term": "ἀταραξία (ataraxia): freedom from disturbance, peace of mind", "eg": "A sleepless night spent dreading an exam you will pass anyway: the dread is the real harm, not the exam."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The argument in four lines", "items": [{"title": "Premise 1", "text": "Something can be bad for you only if you can experience it."}, {"title": "Premise 2", "text": "Once you are dead, you experience nothing."}, {"title": "So", "text": "Being dead cannot be bad for you."}, {"title": "And so", "text": "Fearing death is fearing nothing. “While we exist, death is not present; when death is present, we no longer exist.”"}], "conclusion": "Death is nothing to us. Epicurus, Letter to Menoeceus 124–125 and Principal Doctrines 2 (our summary).", "note": "Notice the argument is about being dead, not dying. Dying can hurt; Epicurus grants that. Keep the room focused on the state of being dead."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Most critics grant that the dead feel nothing. Which of Epicurus’ claims do they usually attack instead?", "options": ["That there is no afterlife in which you could suffer", "That nothing can be bad for you unless you experience it", "That dying is always painless", "That a long life is no better than a short one"], "answer": 1, "why": "Epicurus does deny an afterlife, but the real engine is the experience requirement. Reject it, and his conclusion no longer follows."}, {"t": "source", "text": "Death, then, is nothing to us and does not concern us at all. … Look back, too, at how the endless ages of time before we were born meant nothing to us. Nature holds this up to us as a mirror of the time that will come after our death. Is there anything frightening in it? Does anything seem sad? Is it not more peaceful than any sleep?", "src": "Lucretius, On the Nature of Things (De Rerum Natura) III.830 and III.972–977", "trans": "our translation", "note": "This is the symmetry argument. Lucretius, a Roman follower of Epicurus (c. 99–55 BCE), asks you to compare two infinite stretches of non-existence: before you were born, and after you die."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Does the eternity before you were born bother you as much as the eternity after you die?", "options": ["No: only the time after death bothers me", "Both bother me equally", "Neither bothers me"], "reveal": "Almost everyone picks the first. That is exactly Lucretius’ point: if the two stretches of non-existence are alike, your different feelings need a reason. If you can’t find one, he says, you should feel about death as you feel about the time before your birth."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What does Lucretius’ symmetry argument ask you to accept?", "options": ["That death is painless, because birth was painless", "That if pre-birth non-existence is not bad, post-death non-existence is not bad either", "That we existed before birth and will exist after death", "That we should feel equally sad about birth and death"], "answer": 1, "why": "The argument rests on treating the two periods alike. Any reply has to find a real difference between them."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 2 MIN", "q": "Hands up if Epicurus has convinced you, at least for a moment. Now hands up if you think he has made a mistake but can’t yet say what it is.", "follow": "Those who think he is wrong: which premise would you reject, 1 or 2?", "solo": "Write one sentence: which premise of Epicurus’ argument you would reject, and why.", "note": "Most people want to reject premise 1. Write “harm without experience?” on the board: it is the bridge to Nagel."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "What you lose", "sub": "Thomas Nagel: death is bad for what it takes away", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "NAGEL, “DEATH” (1970)", "title": "The deprivation account", "text": "The American philosopher Thomas Nagel replied that death is bad not because being dead feels bad, but because it deprives you of the good things more life would have held. A loss can be real even if you never notice it. Badness here is comparative: your life with this death against your life without it.", "term": "deprivation account: death harms by what it takes away", "eg": "Your lottery ticket would have won, but it blew away before the draw. You never find out. Haven’t you still lost something?"}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Nagel’s two test cases", "items": [{"tag": "BETRAYAL", "title": "Behind your back", "text": "Your friends mock you behind your back and are polite to your face. You never learn of it and never feel a thing. Nagel says you are still wronged and harmed: your life went worse than you think."}, {"tag": "INJURY", "title": "The contented infant", "text": "A brilliant adult suffers a brain injury and is left with the mind of a happy baby. He is content and feels no loss. Yet we rightly call this a terrible misfortune for him, measured against the life he would have had."}], "note": "Ask: does the betrayal case convince you? If you think the mocked person is harmed, you have already rejected Epicurus’ premise 1."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On the deprivation account, what makes one person’s death worse for them than another’s?", "options": ["How painful the dying was", "How much good the rest of their life would have contained", "How many people grieve for them", "How much they feared death beforehand"], "answer": 1, "why": "The account compares the life the person got with the life they would have had. Pain in dying is a separate harm, and grief is a harm to others."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A healthy 30-year-old dies painlessly in their sleep.", "items": [{"title": "Ninety and frail", "text": "The person is 90, and their next years would have held some good days but also pain and confusion. Is their death as bad for them? Less bad? Not bad at all?"}, {"title": "Agony ahead", "text": "The 30-year-old was about to develop an illness that would bring five years of severe, untreatable pain. On Nagel’s view, could their death have been good for them?"}, {"title": "A one-day-old baby", "text": "A newborn dies. It loses the most future life, but it has no plans, hopes or sense of itself yet. Is this the worst death of all, or not?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Which detail did the work?", "note": "The baby case is where people split. Jeff McMahan’s “time-relative interests” view says the baby’s loss counts for less because it is barely connected psychologically to its future self. Mention it only if the room gets there."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE TIMING PROBLEM", "title": "When is death bad for you?", "text": "Epicureans hit back: a harm should happen at some time. Not before you die, since you are alive and well. Not after, since you no longer exist. So when? Some answer: before death, since your life is already one that will be cut short. Others, like Fred Feldman, say the harm is timeless: it is simply true that your whole life went worse.", "eg": "When were you harmed by the lottery ticket that blew away? On the day it blew away, the day of the draw, or never at any particular moment?"}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Is the symmetry real?", "a": {"label": "LUCRETIUS", "title": "Two mirrors", "text": "Before birth and after death are the same nothing. You don’t grieve the first, so you shouldn’t grieve the second."}, "b": {"label": "NAGEL", "title": "Two different gaps", "text": "You could die later than you will, so death takes time you could have had. But you could not have been born much earlier and still be you: a different sperm and egg make a different person. Nagel himself admits this reply is not quite airtight."}, "verdict": "Derek Parfit added a twist: we are biased towards the future in general. Most people would rather have had ten hours of pain yesterday than one hour tomorrow (Reasons and Persons, 1984, §64). Is that bias rational, or just how we are built?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Nagel think the time before birth differs from the time after death?", "options": ["Because we can remember the time before birth", "Because you could not have been born much earlier and still be the same person, but you could have died later", "Because non-existence before birth is shorter", "Because the dead have souls and the unborn do not"], "answer": 1, "why": "Death takes away time that could have been yours; a later birth takes nothing, since an earlier birth would have produced someone else."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Is it irrational to care more about the time after your death than the time before your birth?", "follow": "If the bias is just evolution’s doing, does that make it wrong, or simply ours?", "solo": "Write one sentence explaining whether your future-directed fear of death is reasonable or just an instinct.", "note": "One minute alone, two minutes in pairs, one minute of reports. Collect two answers that disagree."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Would you want forever?", "sub": "Bernard Williams and the tedium of immortality", "min": 10}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE MAKROPULOS CASE", "title": "Three hundred years at forty-two", "text": "In Karel Čapek’s play The Makropulos Affair (1922), Elina Makropulos takes an elixir of life. She stays 42 for some three hundred years. By the end she is cold, bored and empty, and she refuses to take the elixir again. The British philosopher Bernard Williams used her story in a famous 1973 essay to argue that living forever would not be a gift.", "eg": "Think of your favourite song played on repeat. Now think of it on repeat for three centuries."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Two kinds of desire", "items": [{"tag": "CONDITIONAL", "title": "If I’m alive, then…", "text": "Wants that assume you go on living: if I am alive tomorrow, I want breakfast. They give you no reason to stay alive."}, {"tag": "CATEGORICAL", "title": "Reasons to go on", "text": "Projects and loves that give you a reason to live: finishing your novel, seeing your child grow up. Williams says death is bad because it frustrates these."}], "note": "Common confusion: “categorical” here has nothing to do with Kant. It just means unconditional on staying alive."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which is a categorical desire in Williams’ sense?", "options": ["Wanting a hot shower if you wake up tomorrow", "Wanting to finish building the boat you have worked on for years", "Wanting your pain to stop", "Wanting to sleep when you are tired"], "answer": 1, "why": "A long project you care about gives you reason to keep living. The others are wants you have only on condition that you are alive."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Williams’ dilemma for immortality", "items": [{"title": "Either", "text": "You keep the same character and interests forever. Then sooner or later every project is done, every pleasure stale: endless boredom."}, {"title": "Or", "text": "You change so much, over centuries, that new interests keep appearing. But then the far-future person is not really you; your desires are not being served."}, {"title": "So", "text": "An endless life would be either tedious or not yours."}], "conclusion": "Death is bad when it comes too early, yet immortality would be bad too. Williams, “The Makropulos Case: Reflections on the Tedium of Immortality” (1973)."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "A treatment would let you live forever, never ageing or falling ill. The people you love could take it too. You could never undo it. Would you take it?", "options": ["Yes, at once", "Yes, if I could stop later", "No", "I honestly don’t know"], "reveal": "Williams says no. His critics, such as John Martin Fischer (“Why Immortality Is Not So Bad”, 1994), point to “repeatable” pleasures: good food, music, friendship, which we enjoy again and again without tiring. Notice: if you said “yes, if I could stop later”, you share at least part of Williams’ worry about forever."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Can death be bad and immortality be bad at the same time? Or must one of these give way?", "follow": "If both are bad, what is the right length of a human life, and who gets to say?", "solo": "Write one sentence: is there a life length you would choose, and why that number?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Living with it", "sub": "Stoic and Buddhist ways to face death", "min": 8}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three Stoic moves", "items": [{"tag": "EPICTETUS", "title": "It’s the judgement", "text": "What disturbs us is not things but our judgements about things. Death is nothing terrible, or it would have seemed so to Socrates (Enchiridion 5)."}, {"tag": "MARCUS AURELIUS", "title": "Only the present", "text": "However long you live, you lose only the life you are living now. The present is all anyone can lose (Meditations 2.14)."}, {"tag": "SENECA", "title": "Not length, quality", "text": "A life is good like a play is good: by how well it is acted, not how long it runs (Letters 77.20)."}], "note": "Contrast with Epicurus: the Stoics also say death is not an evil, but for a different reason. For them only vice is truly bad, and death is natural, part of the order of the world."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "As with a play, so with life: what matters is not how long it is, but how well it is acted.", "src": "Seneca, Letters to Lucilius 77.20 (our translation)"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "BUDDHIST VIEWS", "title": "Letting go of the one who fears", "text": "Buddhist teaching places death within impermanence: everything conditioned arises and passes away. Suffering comes from clinging, including clinging to a self that does not truly exist as a fixed thing. Traditional Buddhism also teaches rebirth, so death is not simply an end; the goal is release from the cycle. Practitioners cultivate maraṇasati, mindful recollection of death, to loosen fear.", "term": "anicca (Pali): impermanence; anattā: not-self; maraṇasati: mindfulness of death", "eg": "A later commentary tells of Kisā Gotamī, sent to fetch a mustard seed from a house no one had died in. She found none, and her grief changed shape."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "According to Epictetus, what makes death seem terrible?", "options": ["The pain of dying", "The loss of all future goods", "Our judgement that death is terrible", "The punishments of the afterlife"], "answer": 2, "why": "Enchiridion 5: it is our opinion about death, not death itself, that disturbs us. Change the judgement and the fear goes."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 3 MIN", "q": "Would you want to lose your fear of death completely? What might you lose with it?", "solo": "Write one sentence on whether a person with no fear of death would value life more or less.", "note": "If it comes up, be careful and kind: some participants may be grieving. Keep it about ideas, and let people pass."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every view tonight has a weak point. Find it.", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Harm without experience", "text": "Against Epicurus: if betrayal behind your back or a ruined reputation after death can harm you, then experience is not needed for harm, and premise 1 collapses."}, {"title": "Harm to whom, and when?", "text": "Against Nagel: a harm needs a subject who has it. After death there is no subject, and before death nothing bad has happened yet. So where is the harm located?"}, {"title": "Compared with what?", "text": "Against Nagel: if death is bad for what it takes, every death deprives you of something, since you could always have lived longer. Is dying at 100 then a tragedy too?"}, {"title": "The unborn", "text": "Against Nagel: if missing goods is a harm, are the billions of people who could have been conceived but never were harmed by never existing? If not, why is the dead person different?"}, {"title": "Repeatable pleasures", "text": "Against Williams: boredom is not inevitable. Music, friendship, walks and meals renew themselves, and people change gradually without ceasing to be themselves."}, {"title": "Calm or numbness?", "text": "Against the Stoics and Buddhists: if you train yourself not to mind losing your life, you may also train yourself not to care about what makes it worth living."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Give 4 minutes, then 1 minute per group to report. Push each group: what is the best reply a defender could give?"}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Is death bad for the one who dies?", "options": ["Yes: it takes away everything life would have given", "Sometimes: it depends on what the rest of life would have held", "No: once you are gone, nothing can be bad for you", "I’m not sure the question even makes sense"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Compare with your first vote. If you moved, which argument moved you? If you stayed, which objection did you have to answer? Notice that “death is bad for you” and “you should fear death” are separate questions: you can say yes to the first and no to the second.", "note": "Write the new numbers beside the old ones and ask one person who changed their vote to explain why."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Read Epicurus’ Letter to Menoeceus: it is short and free online", "Try a Stoic evening review: one minute asking what you would regret leaving undone", "Write down one categorical desire: a project that gives you reason to go on", "Ask an older relative what they think about death, and listen"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Epicurus: nothing is bad for you unless you experience it, so being dead cannot harm you.", "Nagel: death harms by deprivation, taking the goods more life would have held, even if you never feel the loss.", "Williams: death is bad when it comes too soon, but endless life might be tedious or no longer yours."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Letter to Menoeceus", "by": "Epicurus", "note": "Five pages that started the whole debate."}, {"title": "On the Nature of Things, Book III", "by": "Lucretius", "note": "The symmetry argument in Latin verse; any good translation."}, {"title": "“Death”, in Mortal Questions (1979)", "by": "Thomas Nagel", "note": "The classic deprivation reply; short and clear."}, {"title": "“The Makropulos Case”, in Problems of the Self (1973)", "by": "Bernard Williams", "note": "Why living forever might not be a gift."}, {"title": "Death (2012)", "by": "Shelly Kagan", "note": "A friendly book-length tour from a famous Yale course."}, {"title": "Letters to Lucilius", "by": "Seneca", "note": "Letters 24, 30 and 54 are on facing death calmly."}], "also": ["The Quiet Garden", "The Inner Citadel", "The Tree of Waking", "The Glass Tunnel"]}]}, {"id": "evil", "title": "Can a Good God Allow Suffering?", "sub": "Mackie, Rowe’s fawn, Plantinga and Hick on the problem of evil", "question": "Can a good God allow suffering?", "minutes": 50, "soloMinutes": 15, "aim": "Participants can tell the logical problem of evil from the evidential one, explain the free will defence, soul-making and skeptical theism, and say which reply survives the strongest objection.", "cast": ["mackie", "rowe", "plantinga", "hick", "augustine", "hume", "adams_mm", "leibniz"], "related": {"agora": "evil", "islands": ["design", "augustine", "leibniz", "hume"], "compass": ["k9k", "k9"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "All Saints’ Day, Lisbon, 1755", "text": "On the morning of 1 November 1755 the churches of Lisbon were full for a holy day. Then the ground shook. Walls and roofs came down on the worshippers. A tsunami swept the harbour, and fires burned for days. Tens of thousands died; estimates still vary widely. Across Europe, people asked a question as old as religion itself. If God is all-powerful and perfectly good, why would he let this happen, and on a day of prayer? Voltaire wrote a furious poem. Others defended God. Tonight you get to decide who had the better argument.", "note": "Some people in the room may have lost someone, or hold faith deeply. Say at the start: we test arguments, not people. Believers and non-believers both get the strongest case."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The oldest puzzle", "min": 8}, {"title": "Is it a contradiction? Mackie and Plantinga", "min": 11}, {"title": "Is it evidence? Rowe’s fawn", "min": 9}, {"title": "Two replies: soul-making and humility", "min": 11}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then your verdict", "min": 11}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Does the suffering in the world make it unlikely that an all-powerful, perfectly good God exists?", "options": ["Yes, decisively", "It counts against God, but not decisively", "No: God could have good reasons we can grasp", "No: God’s reasons may be beyond us"], "reveal": "Hold on to your answer. In the 2020 PhilPapers survey of professional philosophers, roughly one in five accepted or leaned towards theism, and most of the rest towards atheism. But many on both sides agree on something surprising, which you will meet in section 2."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The oldest puzzle", "sub": "Three things believers say about God, and one fact everyone sees", "min": 8}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Four claims that seem hard to hold together", "items": [{"tag": "POWER", "term": "omnipotent", "title": "God can do anything", "text": "Anything logically possible, at least. Stopping an earthquake is easy for such a being."}, {"tag": "KNOWLEDGE", "term": "omniscient", "title": "God knows everything", "text": "God cannot fail to notice a child in pain or a fire in a forest."}, {"tag": "GOODNESS", "term": "omnibenevolent", "title": "God is perfectly good", "text": "A good being wants to prevent suffering where it can."}, {"tag": "FACT", "title": "There is terrible suffering", "text": "Disease, cruelty, famine, animals dying slowly. No one denies this."}]}, {"t": "source", "text": "Epicurus’s old questions are yet unanswered. Is he willing to prevent evil, but not able? then is he impotent. Is he able, but not willing? then is he malevolent. Is he both able and willing? whence then is evil? … Why is there any misery at all in the world? Not by chance surely. From some cause then. Is it from the intention of the Deity? But he is perfectly benevolent. Is it contrary to his intention? But he is almighty.", "src": "David Hume, Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion (1779), Part X, spoken by Philo", "trans": "original English, public domain", "note": "Philo credits Epicurus, but the trilemma reaches us through the Christian writer Lactantius (On the Anger of God 13), and many scholars doubt Epicurus framed it this way. Notice that Hume puts it in a character’s mouth, not his own."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two versions of the problem", "a": {"label": "LOGICAL", "title": "God and evil contradict each other", "text": "Like a square circle: if evil exists, a perfect God cannot. One case of suffering would be enough to refute theism."}, "b": {"label": "EVIDENTIAL", "title": "Evil makes God unlikely", "text": "God and evil might be compatible in principle, but the amount and kind of suffering we see is strong evidence that there is no such God."}, "verdict": "The first claims proof. The second claims probability. They need different replies."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A friend says: “Maybe God has reasons, but honestly, a million years of animals dying in agony before any human existed makes God very hard to believe in.” Which problem is she raising?", "options": ["The logical problem: God and evil are contradictory", "The evidential problem: the scale of suffering lowers the probability of God", "Neither: she is talking about free will", "Both at once, since they are the same argument"], "answer": 1, "why": "She grants that God might have reasons, so she is not claiming a contradiction. She is saying the evidence makes God improbable."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE · ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "A murder and an earthquake both kill an innocent person. As a problem for belief in God, what is the difference between them?", "follow": "Philosophers call these moral evil (caused by free agents) and natural evil (caused by nature). Which seems harder for a believer to explain?", "solo": "Write one sentence on why an earthquake might be harder (or easier) to square with a good God than a murder.", "note": "Write MORAL EVIL and NATURAL EVIL on the board. Keep them up: every reply tonight has to deal with both."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Is it a contradiction?", "sub": "Mackie says belief in God is not just false but inconsistent", "min": 11}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Mackie’s logical problem (1955)", "items": [{"title": "God is omnipotent", "text": "There are no limits to what an omnipotent being can do, apart from logical impossibilities."}, {"title": "God is wholly good", "text": "A good being eliminates evil as far as it can."}, {"title": "So God would eliminate all evil", "text": "Nothing could stop him, and he would want to."}, {"title": "But evil exists", "text": "Every believer admits this too."}], "conclusion": "So the believer’s claims, taken together, contradict each other. That is the charge J. L. Mackie pressed in “Evil and Omnipotence” (Mind, 1955).", "note": "Point out that premise 2 is the hidden hinge. Does a good being always eliminate evil as far as it can? A dentist causes pain. A parent lets a child fall off a bike."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "A KEY DISTINCTION", "title": "Defence, not theodicy", "text": "A theodicy tries to give God’s actual reasons for allowing evil. A defence aims lower: it only shows that God and evil are not contradictory, by describing a possible reason. To answer a claim of contradiction, you need not know the truth. You need only show a consistent story.", "term": "theodicy, from Greek theos (God) + dikē (justice): “justifying God”", "eg": "If someone says “a bachelor can’t own a ring”, you refute them just by describing a possible bachelor who owns one.", "note": "Write DEFENCE (possible reason) and THEODICY (actual reason) on the board. Many later confusions come from mixing them."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Plantinga’s free will defence", "items": [{"title": "Freedom is very valuable", "text": "A world with creatures who freely choose good is better than a world of puppets."}, {"title": "Real freedom includes the power to do wrong", "text": "If God causes you to choose good, you did not choose freely."}, {"title": "So God cannot guarantee free creatures never sin", "text": "Even omnipotence cannot make a free choice turn out a certain way and leave it free."}, {"title": "Possibly, every world with free creatures contains some evil", "text": "This only has to be possible, not proven true."}], "conclusion": "So an all-powerful, good God and evil are logically compatible. Alvin Plantinga, God, Freedom, and Evil (1974)."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Could God have created people who are genuinely free but who, as it happens, always choose the good?", "options": ["Yes: freely choosing good every time is possible, so God could pick those people", "No: if they’re free, nothing can guarantee it", "Not sure"], "reveal": "Mackie said yes: if one free choice can be good, why not all of them? Plantinga replied with transworld depravity: it is possible that every free creature God could make would go wrong at least once, whatever world God placed it in. God can’t control which way free creatures would freely go."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Plantinga says it is only possible that every free creature would sin at least once. Why is “possible” enough for him?", "options": ["Because he is defending against a claim of contradiction, which a consistent possibility defeats", "Because he thinks possibilities are as good as facts", "Because the evidential problem is about possibilities", "It isn’t enough; he admits the argument fails"], "answer": 0, "why": "Mackie claimed God and evil cannot both exist. Showing one consistent scenario where they do is enough to refute a claim of impossibility."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE HARD CASE", "title": "What about earthquakes?", "text": "Free will explains murder, not tsunamis. Plantinga noted it is at least possible that natural evils come from free non-human spirits, such as fallen angels. Many readers find that strained as a real explanation, but remember it only has to be possible. Today many philosophers, atheists included, judge the logical problem answered. The fight moved to the evidence.", "eg": "A defence is like an alibi that is merely consistent. It gets you acquitted of “impossible”, not of “unlikely”.", "note": "If someone says “that’s just made up”, agree: it is a defence, not a theodicy. The question is whether it is consistent. Then ask whether consistency is all a believer should want."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why is natural evil, like the Lisbon earthquake, harder for the free will defence than murder is?", "options": ["Because no human free choice seems to cause it", "Because earthquakes cause less suffering than murders", "Because natural evil is not really evil", "Because free will only applies to animals"], "answer": 0, "why": "The free will defence explains evil as the price of creatures’ free choices. An earthquake has no obvious chooser, so the defence must stretch, for example to non-human free agents."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 4 MIN", "q": "Is human freedom worth the price of Auschwitz, child abuse and war? Would you, as creator, have made free beings at that cost?", "follow": "Does it matter that the victims never agreed to pay the price for other people’s freedom?", "solo": "Write one sentence saying whether freedom is worth that price, and one reason."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Is it evidence?", "sub": "A single fawn in a forest fire", "min": 9}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "ROWE’S CASE", "title": "The fawn", "text": "William Rowe (1979) asked us to picture a fawn caught in a forest fire started by lightning. It is horribly burned and lies in agony for several days before it dies. No human sees it. No one learns from it, no one is tested by it, and no free choice caused it. What good could this possibly serve?", "eg": "Rowe chose an animal on purpose: free will and character-building seem to have nothing to say about it."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Rowe’s evidential argument, in plain words", "items": [{"title": "Pointless suffering exists", "text": "Probably, there is intense suffering an all-powerful, all-knowing being could have prevented without losing a greater good or allowing something equally bad or worse."}, {"title": "A good God would prevent pointless suffering", "text": "A perfectly good, all-knowing being stops intense suffering unless allowing it serves such a greater good."}, {"title": "So probably there is no such God", "text": "The conclusion follows validly from the two premises."}], "conclusion": "Rowe, “The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism”, American Philosophical Quarterly (1979). Most of the debate is about premise 1."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Rowe’s argument is valid. If you are a theist, where is the most promising place to push back?", "options": ["Premise 1: we can’t be confident the fawn’s suffering really serves no greater good", "Premise 2: a good God would not care about animals", "The logic: the conclusion does not follow", "The fawn never existed, so the case is irrelevant"], "answer": 0, "why": "Most theists accept premise 2 and the logic. They argue that our failing to see a reason does not show that there is none. Rowe himself agreed premise 1 was the battleground."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "TURNING IT AROUND", "title": "The G. E. Moore shift", "text": "Rowe pointed out that a theist can run his argument backwards. If a good God exists and would prevent pointless suffering, then the fawn’s suffering is not pointless. Which do you have more reason to believe: that the fawn suffered for nothing, or that God exists? Your answer depends on what else you already know.", "term": "named after G. E. Moore, who argued he was more sure he had hands than of any sceptical premise", "eg": "If a reliable friend says “I was home all night” and your camera seems to show her outside, you may doubt the camera before her."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A fawn suffers for days in a forest fire, unseen and for no apparent reason.", "items": [{"title": "A child, not a fawn", "text": "The victim is a five-year-old with a painful illness. Does the suffering of a being who can understand make it worse, or easier to explain?"}, {"title": "One hour, not five days", "text": "The fawn dies quickly. Is a little pointless suffering easier to accept than a lot?"}, {"title": "Millions of years", "text": "Animals hunted, starved and burned for hundreds of millions of years before any human existed. Does the scale change things?"}, {"title": "Someone learns", "text": "A hiker finds the fawn and becomes a lifelong firefighter. Does that justify it?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Does the problem depend on how much suffering there is, or just that there is any?", "note": "The scale point is Rowe’s real force: theists may explain some suffering, but explaining all of it, in this amount, is harder. Give 1 minute per variation."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "It’s not God that I don’t accept, Alyosha, only I most respectfully return Him the ticket.", "src": "Fyodor Dostoevsky, The Brothers Karamazov (1880), Book V, ch. 4 “Rebellion”, trans. Constance Garnett (1912)", "note": "Ivan is not arguing God doesn’t exist. He refuses any future harmony bought with a tortured child’s tears. Ask: is that a philosophical argument, or a moral protest? Can it be both?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Two replies", "sub": "A school for souls, and the limits of what we can see", "min": 11}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SOUL-MAKING", "title": "Hick: a world for growing up in", "text": "John Hick, in Evil and the God of Love (1966), followed the early bishop Irenaeus. Humans were not made perfect and then fell; they were made immature, to grow. A world with no danger, no pain and no need would make no one brave, kind or patient. Hick borrowed a phrase from the poet Keats: the world as “the vale of Soul-making”.", "eg": "A parent who wants a child to become a good adult does not raise her in a padded room where nothing can ever go wrong.", "note": "Keats used the phrase in a letter of April 1819. Hick admits soul-making does not happen in everyone’s life; he adds an afterlife in which the process can be completed."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two classic stories about evil", "a": {"label": "AUGUSTINE", "title": "Perfect creation, then a fall", "text": "God made everything good. Evil is not a thing but an absence of good (privatio boni), brought in by the misuse of free will by angels and humans."}, "b": {"label": "IRENAEUS AND HICK", "title": "Unfinished creation, then growth", "text": "God made us at a distance, immature and free, in a tough world where character can be formed. Evil is the cost of a process, not the ruin of a finished work."}, "verdict": "Augustine looks back to a lost paradise; Hick looks forward to a goal. Which fits what science says about the long history of life before humans?"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Hick think God stays hidden, at an “epistemic distance” from us?", "options": ["So that we can freely come to goodness and faith instead of being overwhelmed by God’s obvious presence", "Because God does not know about us in detail", "Because suffering is an illusion", "Because God prefers some people to others"], "answer": 0, "why": "If God were as obvious as the sun, Hick argues, we could not freely grow towards him. Distance leaves room for real choice."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Picture a world with no pain, no danger and no hardship at all. Would people in it be better or worse people than us?", "options": ["Better: suffering mostly damages people", "Worse: they’d never need courage or compassion", "About the same"], "reveal": "Hick bets on “worse”. Critics reply that much suffering does not build character but crushes it, and that a world with far less suffering could still train virtue. Both sides agree the answer matters for his theodicy."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SKEPTICAL THEISM", "title": "Would we see God’s reasons?", "text": "Stephen Wykstra (1984) asked: if God had a reason for allowing the fawn’s suffering, should we expect to see it? Looking into a tent and seeing no dog is good evidence there is no dog. Seeing no flea is not good evidence there is no flea. An infinite mind’s reasons may be more like fleas than dogs.", "eg": "A baby getting a vaccine cannot see why her loving parent lets the nurse hurt her. The reason is real, just beyond her grasp.", "note": "Wykstra’s principle is often called CORNEA. If asked: the analogy cuts both ways, since a parent eventually explains things to a child, and critics ask why God does not."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What exactly does skeptical theism deny?", "options": ["That we can move from “I can’t see a reason for this suffering” to “there probably is no reason”", "That suffering exists", "That God is all-powerful", "That anyone can know anything at all"], "answer": 0, "why": "It is scepticism about one inference only: from our not seeing God’s reasons to there being none. It targets Rowe’s premise 1."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "If God might have hidden reasons for allowing any suffering, should we still try to stop suffering when we can?", "follow": "If yes, why? If we can’t know which suffering serves a purpose, how do we know ours isn’t interfering?", "solo": "Write one sentence: does skeptical theism leave believers free to fight suffering, or does it tie their hands?", "note": "This sets up the strongest critique card (moral paralysis). Let the room feel the pull before you name it."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 1 MIN", "q": "Which reply on behalf of God is strongest so far?", "options": ["Free will defence", "Soul-making", "Skeptical theism", "None of them works"], "reveal": "Many theist philosophers combine them: free will for moral evil, soul-making for the shape of the world, and skeptical theism for cases like the fawn that nothing else explains. Notice that the combination is only as strong as its weakest part."}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Objections to both sides, and your verdict", "min": 11}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the arguments", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Horrors that crush", "text": "Marilyn McCord Adams pressed “horrendous evils” that can destroy a person’s ability to see their life as good at all. A theodicy that only shows the world is good overall ignores the individual victim. Is God good to her?"}, {"title": "Animals before humans", "text": "Hundreds of millions of years of animal suffering preceded any human free will or character to build. Neither free will nor soul-making seems to touch it."}, {"title": "Moral paralysis", "text": "If we can never tell whether suffering serves a hidden good, we can’t know whether preventing it is right either. Skeptical theism seems to undermine ordinary moral reasoning."}, {"title": "The heaven problem", "text": "Many believers think heaven holds free beings who never sin. If that is possible there, why not here? This presses Plantinga’s defence and Hick’s need for a tough world."}, {"title": "Seeing no reason is not no reason", "text": "Against Rowe: humans routinely miss the reasons of people far wiser than them. Why should our failure to see a purpose for the fawn count as evidence there is none?"}, {"title": "One-sided evidence", "text": "Against atheists: the problem of evil weighs only the bad. Beauty, love, consciousness and a universe orderly enough for science may count for God. Evidence must be weighed as a whole."}, {"title": "A better explanation?", "text": "Paul Draper (1989) argued the mix of pleasure and pain we see fits a universe indifferent to us far better than one designed by a loving God. Can theism explain the pattern, not just its existence?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Make sure at least two groups take cards aimed at the atheist side (5 and 6). Ask each group to say who the objection hurts most."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "Does the suffering in the world make it unlikely that an all-powerful, perfectly good God exists?", "options": ["Yes, decisively", "It counts against God, but not decisively", "No: God could have good reasons we can grasp", "No: God’s reasons may be beyond us"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Few people change sides in an hour, but many change why they hold their side. If you moved between “decisively” and “not decisively”, that is a real philosophical shift."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week.", "options": ["Read Ivan’s chapter “Rebellion” in The Brothers Karamazov (about 20 pages) and decide if he is right", "Ask someone of a different view from yours how they think about suffering, and only listen", "Write down the single case of suffering that troubles you most, and test each reply from tonight against it", "Read Hume’s Dialogues, Parts X and XI, where Philo and Cleanthes argue it out"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["The logical problem claims God and evil contradict; Plantinga’s free will defence persuaded many that they need not.", "The evidential problem (Rowe’s fawn) claims pointless suffering makes God improbable; the fight is over whether we would see God’s reasons.", "Soul-making and skeptical theism are the main replies; horrendous evils and animal suffering are their hardest tests."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion, Parts X–XI", "by": "David Hume (1779)", "note": "The classic statement, in a lively dialogue where no side gets an easy win."}, {"title": "“Evil and Omnipotence”", "by": "J. L. Mackie (1955)", "note": "The short paper that launched the modern logical problem."}, {"title": "God, Freedom, and Evil", "by": "Alvin Plantinga (1974)", "note": "The free will defence, written for non-specialists."}, {"title": "“The Problem of Evil and Some Varieties of Atheism”", "by": "William Rowe (1979)", "note": "Where the fawn comes from; clear and fair to believers."}, {"title": "Evil and the God of Love", "by": "John Hick (1966)", "note": "The soul-making theodicy and its history from Irenaeus."}, {"title": "The Brothers Karamazov, Book V", "by": "Fyodor Dostoevsky (1880)", "note": "“Rebellion” and “The Grand Inquisitor”: the problem as a human cry."}], "also": ["The Restless Heart (Augustine)", "The Best of All Possible Worlds (Leibniz and Voltaire)", "The Harbour of Habit (Hume)"]}]}, {"id": "justice", "title": "What Would a Fair Society Look Like?", "sub": "Rawls, Nozick, the utilitarians, luck and capabilities", "question": "What would a fair society look like?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 18, "aim": "Participants can explain the veil of ignorance and the difference principle, Nozick’s Wilt Chamberlain argument, the utilitarian, luck-egalitarian and capabilities views, and state the strongest objection to each, including the one they find most persuasive.", "cast": ["rawls", "nozick", "bentham", "mill", "dworkin", "ga_cohen", "anderson_e", "arneson", "nussbaum"], "related": {"agora": null, "islands": ["justice", "polis", "republic", "marx", "mill", "hobbes"], "compass": ["s8", "s10"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The charter of New Harbour", "text": "A thousand settlers are travelling to a new world. Before they land, they must write the rules: who owns the land, how work is paid, who gets healthcare and schooling, what happens to people who cannot work. There is one strange condition. During the voyage, everyone has lost their memory of who they are. You don’t know whether you will wake up strong or ill, gifted or ordinary, a hard worker or a dreamer, born to rich parents or poor ones. Your memories return the moment you step ashore. You have one hour to write the charter. What do you put in it?", "note": "Let people sit with the charter question for 30 seconds of silence. Tell them no one will be asked to defend their real-world politics tonight; we are testing arguments."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "Behind the veil: Rawls", "min": 12}, {"title": "Wilt Chamberlain: Nozick", "min": 11}, {"title": "The greatest total: utilitarians", "min": 7}, {"title": "Choice and luck: luck egalitarians", "min": 8}, {"title": "What people can do and be: capabilities", "min": 7}, {"title": "Attack the theories, and your verdict", "min": 10}]}, {"t": "vote", "id": "v1", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Everyone got what they have honestly: by working, trading, saving and receiving gifts, with no theft or fraud. Some are very rich, some very poor. Is that society fair?", "options": ["Yes: honest holdings are just holdings", "Only if the poorest are as well off as they could be", "Only if everyone has enough for a decent life", "No: large gaps are unfair in themselves"], "reveal": "Each answer roughly matches a view we meet tonight: Nozick, Rawls, sufficiency and capabilities views, and stronger egalitarians. In the 2009 PhilPapers survey, about a third of philosophers leaned towards egalitarianism and about one in ten towards libertarianism. Philosophers are not a random sample, so treat that as a fact about philosophers, not about who is right.", "note": "Count and record. If the room leans heavily one way, say so, and tell them your job tonight is to make the minority view as strong as possible."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "Behind the veil", "sub": "John Rawls: choose the rules before you know who you are", "min": 12}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "A THEORY OF JUSTICE, 1971", "title": "The veil of ignorance", "text": "John Rawls asked what principles free and equal people would agree to if they chose from an “original position” behind a veil of ignorance. You don’t know your class, talents, sex, race, religion or idea of the good life. The point is fairness: no one can rig the rules for their own side, because no one knows which side they are on.", "term": "original position: the imagined choosing situation behind the veil", "eg": "One child cuts the cake, the other chooses first. The cutter has every reason to cut fairly."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "What Rawls thinks you would choose", "items": [{"tag": "FIRST PRINCIPLE", "title": "Equal basic liberties", "text": "Everyone gets the same extensive set of basic liberties: speech, conscience, vote, the rule of law. These come first and may not be traded for wealth."}, {"tag": "SECOND PRINCIPLE: OPPORTUNITY", "title": "Fair equality of opportunity", "text": "Positions are open to all, and people with similar talent and effort have similar chances, whatever family they are born into."}, {"tag": "SECOND PRINCIPLE: DIFFERENCE", "title": "The difference principle", "text": "Inequalities in wealth and income are allowed only if they work to the greatest benefit of the least advantaged."}]}, {"t": "steps", "title": "Why the difference principle?", "items": [{"title": "Premise 1", "text": "Behind the veil, you might turn out to be among the worst off."}, {"title": "Premise 2", "text": "The stakes are your whole life, and you cannot know the odds of each position."}, {"title": "Premise 3", "text": "In such a choice, it is rational to make the worst outcome as good as possible (“maximin”)."}, {"title": "So", "text": "You would allow inequality only when it raises the floor."}], "conclusion": "Rawls adds a second reason: talents and family background are “morally arbitrary”. No one deserves the genes or parents they started with.", "note": "Premise 3 is the most attacked line. Flag it now; it comes back on a critique card."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which inequality could the difference principle allow?", "options": ["Surgeons earn more because they work harder, even if no one else benefits", "Higher pay draws more people into medicine, so the poorest get better healthcare than under equal pay", "The rich pay less tax because they earned their money", "Any inequality, as long as it came about by voluntary trade"], "answer": 1, "why": "The test is whether the inequality makes the least advantaged better off than they would otherwise be. Desert and voluntariness alone don’t pass it."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "Justice is the first virtue of social institutions.", "src": "John Rawls, A Theory of Justice (1971), §1"}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 2 MIN", "q": "Back to New Harbour. Hands up if you would choose a society with a guaranteed decent minimum, even if that meant the average was a bit lower.", "follow": "Now: who would gamble on a higher average with no floor? Why?", "solo": "Write one sentence: would you play safe or gamble behind the veil, and why?", "note": "If some gamble, celebrate it: they are reasoning like John Harsanyi, Rawls’ main critic on this point."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why does Rawls hide even your talents and character behind the veil?", "options": ["Because talents don’t matter to how well your life goes", "Because no one deserves their natural talents, so they shouldn’t tilt the rules in their owner’s favour", "Because he thinks everyone has equal talents", "Because people would lie about their talents"], "answer": 1, "why": "Rawls treats natural endowments, like the family you were born into, as a matter of luck, not desert. Critics reply that what you do with your talents is your own."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Wilt Chamberlain", "sub": "Robert Nozick: liberty upsets patterns", "min": 11}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "ANARCHY, STATE, AND UTOPIA, 1974", "title": "Justice is about history, not shape", "text": "Robert Nozick, Rawls’ Harvard colleague, said justice does not depend on the pattern of who has what, but on how they got it. If you acquired something justly, and it was passed on by free exchange or gift, you are entitled to it, whatever the final picture looks like. If something was stolen, it must be put right.", "term": "entitlement theory: just acquisition, just transfer, rectification of injustice", "eg": "A football league table is not fair or unfair in its shape. It is fair if every match was played by the rules."}, {"t": "steps", "title": "The Wilt Chamberlain argument", "items": [{"title": "Start", "text": "Pick any distribution you think is perfectly just. Call it D1. Maybe everyone has equal shares."}, {"title": "Then", "text": "Wilt Chamberlain, a star basketball player, signs a contract: 25 cents from every ticket goes into a box for him. A million fans happily pay."}, {"title": "Result", "text": "Now Wilt has $250,000 more than anyone else. Call this D2."}, {"title": "Question", "text": "Everyone started with a just share and chose freely. Who has been wronged?"}], "conclusion": "Nozick’s answer: no one. So keeping any pattern in place would mean constantly interfering with people’s free choices."}, {"t": "quote", "text": "Taxation of earnings from labor is on a par with forced labor.", "src": "Robert Nozick, Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), chapter 7"}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is the difference between a “historical” and a “patterned” principle of justice?", "options": ["Historical principles apply only to the past", "Historical principles ask how holdings came about; patterned ones ask whether the distribution matches some formula", "Patterned principles ignore what people need", "There is no real difference"], "answer": 1, "why": "“To each according to need” or “equal shares” are patterns. Nozick’s view looks only at the steps by which holdings were acquired and transferred."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A million fans freely pay Wilt 25 cents each. He ends up far richer than anyone else.", "items": [{"title": "The children", "text": "Wilt’s grandchildren inherit the fortune; the fans’ grandchildren start with nothing. No one alive chose this. Still fair?"}, {"title": "The wells", "text": "Wilt uses his money to buy every water well in town and charges what he likes. Each sale was voluntary. Still fair?"}, {"title": "Stolen land", "text": "It turns out D1 itself was built on land seized by force generations ago. Does that change the verdict on D2?"}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Did Nozick’s theory already have a way to handle it?", "note": "Nozick does have resources: a Lockean proviso (you may not appropriate in a way that leaves others worse off, as with the wells) and a principle of rectification (for stolen land). Let the room find these before you mention them."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 3 MIN", "q": "Is taxing someone’s wages really like forcing them to work for others? What is the best case for yes, and the best case for no?", "solo": "Write one sentence giving the strongest point on the side you disagree with.", "note": "Insist that each pair state the other side’s best case first. This is the slide where politics can take over: keep it on the argument."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "The greatest total", "sub": "Utilitarians: distribute to do the most good", "min": 7}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "BENTHAM, MILL AND AFTER", "title": "A dollar is worth more to the poor", "text": "Utilitarians judge a distribution by the total well-being it produces. They don’t value equality for its own sake. But one fact pushes them towards it: diminishing marginal utility. Each extra pound or dollar adds less happiness to someone who already has plenty.", "term": "diminishing marginal utility: each extra unit of a good adds less benefit than the one before", "eg": "£100 barely registers for a billionaire. For someone choosing between heating and food, it changes the week."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Two pulls on the utilitarian", "a": {"label": "TOWARDS EQUALITY", "title": "Spread it out", "text": "Moving resources from rich to poor usually raises total well-being, because the poor gain more than the rich lose."}, "b": {"label": "AWAY FROM EQUALITY", "title": "Grow the pie", "text": "If equal pay kills the incentive to work and invent, the total shrinks. Some inequality may produce more for everyone."}, "verdict": "So the utilitarian answer depends on facts about incentives and growth. It is flexible, but it never cares about who gets what except through the total."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Society A has a higher average well-being but a small group living in misery. Society B has a lower average, and no one is badly off. Which is fairer?", "options": ["A", "B", "It depends on how big the gap is"], "reveal": "A simple utilitarian picks A if its total is larger. Rawls said this fails to take seriously the distinction between persons: one person’s gain doesn’t cancel another’s loss. Prioritarians, following Derek Parfit (“Equality or Priority?”, 1991), split the difference: benefits matter more the worse off the person is."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why might a utilitarian who doesn’t value equality still favour redistributing from rich to poor?", "options": ["Because the rich don’t deserve their wealth", "Because each extra unit of money usually adds less well-being to someone who already has a lot", "Because the law requires it", "Because utilitarians count only the poor"], "answer": 1, "why": "Diminishing marginal utility means the same money does more good for the poor. Equality is a by-product, not a goal."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Hands up if you think a fair society must care about the shape of the distribution, not just the total.", "solo": "Write one sentence on whether you would accept a bigger total if it came with a worse bottom."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Choice and luck", "sub": "Luck egalitarians: fix bad luck, not bad choices", "min": 8}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "DWORKIN, COHEN, ARNESON", "title": "The line between luck and choice", "text": "Luck egalitarians, such as Ronald Dworkin (1981), G. A. Cohen and Richard Arneson (both 1989), say inequality is unfair when it comes from brute luck you could not control: your genes, your parents, an accident. It is fair when it comes from your own choices. A just society neutralises bad luck but lets people bear the costs of what they choose.", "term": "brute luck: risks you did not choose; option luck: the result of gambles you chose to take", "eg": "Twins start equal. One is born with a disability; the other spends her savings on a risky business that fails. Only the first, on this view, has a claim on others."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which is brute luck rather than option luck?", "options": ["Losing money on a bet you placed", "Being born with a serious illness", "Choosing a low-paid job you love over a high-paid one", "Your investments doing badly"], "answer": 1, "why": "You did not choose your birth or your genes. The other three are results of choices or gambles you took on."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "The harshness objection", "a": {"label": "ELIZABETH ANDERSON, 1999", "title": "Too harsh", "text": "A reckless driver without insurance crashes and needs surgery. On a strict luck-egalitarian view, society owes him nothing: he chose the risk. Anderson says a just society would never abandon him, and that judging people’s choices before helping them is demeaning."}, "b": {"label": "LUCK EGALITARIAN REPLY", "title": "Fixable", "text": "We can add a floor of basic needs, or make insurance compulsory, while keeping the core idea: people should not be penalised for luck, nor subsidised for choices others had to pay for."}, "verdict": "Anderson proposes instead “democratic equality”: the point of equality is that people can stand as equals, free from domination, not that luck is cancelled.", "note": "This is one of the liveliest debates in recent political philosophy. Ask: is it insulting to have to prove your bad luck to get help?"}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Where does choice end and luck begin? Is ambition a choice? Is laziness? Is the character you were raised to have?", "follow": "If almost nothing turns out to be fully chosen, what is left of the theory?", "solo": "Write one sentence about a trait of yours: is it your choice, your luck, or both?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "What people can do and be", "sub": "Sen and Nussbaum: measure freedoms, not money", "min": 7}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SEN, “EQUALITY OF WHAT?”, 1979", "title": "Capabilities", "text": "The economist and philosopher Amartya Sen argued that equal money or goods can leave people very unequal in what they can actually do. What matters is capability: your real freedom to achieve valuable “functionings”, such as being nourished, moving about, reading, and taking part in community life. His work helped shape the UN’s Human Development Index (1990), which counts health and education, not just income.", "term": "functionings: beings and doings; capabilities: the real freedom to achieve them", "eg": "Two people get the same bicycle. One can ride to work. The other uses a wheelchair; for her the same resource gives no mobility at all."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Nussbaum’s list (a sample of ten)", "items": [{"tag": "LIFE · BODILY HEALTH", "title": "Living a normal span", "text": "Being able to live to the end of a human life of normal length, in good health and adequately nourished."}, {"tag": "THOUGHT", "title": "Senses, imagination, thought", "text": "Being able to use your mind, informed by an adequate education."}, {"tag": "AFFILIATION", "title": "Living with others", "text": "Being able to live with and towards others, and to be treated with dignity."}, {"tag": "CONTROL", "title": "Over your environment", "text": "Being able to take part in political choices that govern your life, and to hold property and seek work on an equal basis."}], "note": "Martha Nussbaum (Women and Human Development, 2000; Creating Capabilities, 2011) lists ten central capabilities and says justice requires a threshold of each for everyone. Sen deliberately refuses to fix a list."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why do capability theorists think equal resources are not enough?", "options": ["Because resources are never fairly earned", "Because people differ in how well they can turn resources into real freedoms", "Because money has no value", "Because the state should decide how everyone lives"], "answer": 1, "why": "Disability, illness, pregnancy, climate or discrimination can mean the same resources give very different real opportunities."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 2 MIN", "q": "Add one capability to Nussbaum’s list, or remove one. Who should get to decide what goes on the list?", "solo": "Write one capability you think every person should have, and one sentence on why."}, {"t": "section", "num": 6, "title": "Attack the theories", "sub": "Every theory of justice has a case that embarrasses it", "min": 10}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Maximin is too cautious", "text": "Against Rawls: John Harsanyi (1975) argued that rational choosers behind the veil would maximise average well-being, accepting a small risk of being worst off for a much better life on average."}, {"title": "Whose effort?", "text": "Against Rawls: if talent and effort are “arbitrary”, nothing anyone does is creditable to them. Doesn’t that erase the person the theory is meant to protect?"}, {"title": "History is not clean", "text": "Against Nozick: almost every holding on Earth traces back to conquest or theft. If rectification is required but impossible to calculate, the theory gives no guidance for the real world."}, {"title": "Free choices, unchosen effects", "text": "Against Nozick: G. A. Cohen (1977) noted that the fans consented for themselves, not for their descendants or neighbours, who may face Wilt’s power without ever agreeing to it."}, {"title": "Persons are not totals", "text": "Against utilitarianism: adding up well-being allows a few to be sacrificed if enough others gain a little. Fairness seems to be about each person, not the sum."}, {"title": "Too harsh, or too nosy", "text": "Against luck egalitarianism: either it abandons people who chose badly, or the state must investigate everyone’s choices to decide who deserves help."}, {"title": "Whose list?", "text": "Against capabilities: any list of central capabilities embeds a view of the good life. Is it fair to impose it on people who value different things?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Seven cards: with fewer groups, choose so every theory is attacked at least once. Each group reports its verdict and the best reply."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "q": "Everyone got what they have honestly: by working, trading, saving and receiving gifts, with no theft or fraud. Some are very rich, some very poor. Is that society fair?", "options": ["Yes: honest holdings are just holdings", "Only if the poorest are as well off as they could be", "Only if everyone has enough for a decent life", "No: large gaps are unfair in themselves"], "compare": "v1", "reveal": "Compare with your first vote. Whatever you chose, you now know the strongest objection to it. A good test of your view: can you state the best opposing argument in a way its defenders would accept?", "note": "Ask one person who moved and one who stayed to say why in a sentence each."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Run the veil test on one rule at your school or club: would you accept it not knowing who you’d be?", "Read chapter 7 of Nozick’s Anarchy, State, and Utopia, then the first chapter of Rawls", "Look up the Human Development Index and see what it measures besides income", "Ask three people the opening question, and note which theory each answer fits"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Rawls: choose principles behind a veil of ignorance; inequalities are just only if they benefit the least advantaged.", "Nozick: justice is about how holdings arose; free transfers from a just start can produce any pattern.", "Utilitarians, luck egalitarians and capability theorists ask different questions: the total, the role of luck, and what people can actually do and be."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "A Theory of Justice (1971), chapters 1–3", "by": "John Rawls", "note": "The veil and the two principles, from the source."}, {"title": "Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), chapter 7", "by": "Robert Nozick", "note": "Wilt Chamberlain and the entitlement theory."}, {"title": "“What Is the Point of Equality?” (1999)", "by": "Elizabeth Anderson", "note": "The sharpest critique of luck egalitarianism."}, {"title": "Creating Capabilities (2011)", "by": "Martha Nussbaum", "note": "A short, readable case for the capabilities approach."}, {"title": "Justice: What’s the Right Thing to Do? (2009)", "by": "Michael Sandel", "note": "A lively tour of all these views, with cases."}, {"title": "The Mirage of Social Justice (1976)", "by": "Friedrich Hayek", "note": "A classic market-liberal argument that “social justice” asks the impossible; read it against Rawls."}], "also": ["The Veil and the Ledger", "The Political Animal", "The Liberty Harbour", "The Engine of History"]}]}, {"id": "speech", "title": "Which Speech Should Be Free?", "sub": "Mill, Feinberg, Waldron and Popper on harm, offence, dignity and tolerance", "question": "Which speech should be free?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 16, "aim": "Participants can explain the harm principle, the offence principle, the dignity argument against hate speech and the paradox of tolerance, and can say where they would draw the line, for the law and for platforms, and why.", "cast": ["mill", "feinberg", "waldron", "popper"], "related": {"agora": null, "islands": ["mill", "vienna"], "compass": ["s4", "s10"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The Invitation", "text": "Your school’s debate club invites a guest speaker. He is polite, articulate and famous for one claim: that followers of a certain religion can never be loyal citizens and should be barred from public office. Within an hour a petition appears: “Cancel him. Words like his make our classmates feel hunted.” A second petition follows: “Let him speak, then take him apart in the Q&A.” A third group asks something else: even if he may speak, must the school lend him its hall and its name? Tonight’s question is not only what may be said, but who may stop it: the law, the school, the platform, or the rest of us.", "note": "Read it aloud, then ask for a show of hands on the three petitions before the formal vote. Don’t discuss yet; the vote comes next."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "Mill: harm and the market of ideas", "min": 12}, {"title": "Offence and dignity", "min": 13}, {"title": "Tolerating the intolerant", "min": 7}, {"title": "Law, platforms and you", "min": 8}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then vote again", "min": 15}]}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "id": "v1", "q": "Which comes closest to your view of what the law should allow?", "options": ["Almost all speech should be legally free: answer bad speech with more speech", "Free unless it directly causes harm, like threats or incitement to violence", "Speech that attacks a group’s equal standing should also be banned", "It depends so much on the country that there’s no general answer"], "reveal": "Democracies split here too. In the United States, even hateful advocacy is protected unless it is meant and likely to incite imminent lawless action (Brandenburg v. Ohio, 1969). Germany, the United Kingdom, Canada and many others punish some forms of hate speech. Keep your answer: we vote again at the end."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "Mill: harm and the market of ideas", "sub": "The most famous defence of free speech ever written, and the one line where it stops", "min": 12}, {"t": "quote", "text": "“The only purpose for which power can be rightfully exercised over any member of a civilised community, against his will, is to prevent harm to others.”", "src": "John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. 1", "note": "Stress “harm to others”. Mill’s point is that offending you, or harming yourself, isn’t enough to justify force."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE HARM PRINCIPLE", "title": "Your freedom ends where harm to others begins", "text": "Mill’s harm principle says society may restrain you, by law or by heavy social pressure, only to stop you harming other people. Not to make you better. Not to stop you shocking anyone. In chapter 2 he applies it to opinion with special force: silencing an opinion, he argues, is almost never justified, even if only one person holds it.", "eg": "You may write a blog saying a popular law is stupid. You may not post a neighbour’s home address with a call to “pay them a visit”."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Mill’s four reasons to hear even the opinion you hate", "items": [{"tag": "1 · IT MAY BE TRUE", "title": "You are not infallible", "text": "To silence an opinion is to assume you cannot be wrong. History is full of “certain” beliefs that turned out false."}, {"tag": "2 · PART OF IT MAY BE TRUE", "title": "Truth comes in pieces", "text": "Popular opinions are rarely the whole truth; the dissenter often holds the missing piece."}, {"tag": "3 · DEAD DOGMA", "title": "Unchallenged truths go stale", "text": "Even a true belief, never contested, is held “as a dead dogma, not a living truth”: people repeat it without knowing why it’s true (ch. 2)."}, {"tag": "4 · LOST MEANING", "title": "The meaning itself fades", "text": "Without challenge, a doctrine stops shaping how people feel and act. It becomes words."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "According to Mill, why should we let people defend an opinion we know to be false?", "options": ["Because every opinion is equally true for the person who holds it", "Because without challenge, even true beliefs become dead dogma we can’t defend", "Because the state has no right to decide anything about truth", "Because false opinions always lose in the end"], "answer": 1, "why": "Mill is no relativist: he thinks some opinions are false. His point is that meeting false opinions keeps our grasp of true ones alive and reasoned."}, {"t": "source", "text": "“No one pretends that actions should be as free as opinions. On the contrary, even opinions lose their immunity, when the circumstances in which they are expressed are such as to constitute their expression a positive instigation to some mischievous act. An opinion that corn-dealers are starvers of the poor, or that private property is robbery, ought to be unmolested when simply circulated through the press, but may justly incur punishment when delivered orally to an excited mob assembled before the house of a corn-dealer, or when handed about among the same mob in the form of a placard.”", "src": "John Stuart Mill, On Liberty (1859), ch. 3", "trans": "original English, 1859", "note": "Same words, two settings. The newspaper is protected; the speech to the mob at the door is not. What changed is not the content but the link to imminent harm."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "In Mill’s corn-dealer example, what makes the speech to the mob punishable?", "options": ["The opinion is false and insulting to corn-dealers", "The setting turns the words into a direct trigger for harm", "Speaking aloud is always less protected than printing", "The mob is offended by the speaker’s views"], "answer": 1, "why": "Mill protects the very same opinion in print. Only the circumstances, an angry crowd at the dealer’s door, make it a “positive instigation” to a harmful act."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "A speaker tells an angry crowd outside a corn-dealer’s house that he is starving the poor. Mill says this may be punished.", "items": [{"title": "In print", "text": "The same words appear in a newspaper column read across the country."}, {"title": "Online", "text": "The same words go out on social media to two million followers, and the post includes the dealer’s street."}, {"title": "Tomorrow", "text": "The crowd won’t gather until next week, after people have had time to cool down and argue back."}, {"title": "No intent", "text": "The speaker sincerely only meant to criticise prices and was shocked when the crowd turned violent."}], "ask": "After each step: did your answer change? Which detail did the work: audience size, timing, or intent?", "note": "The US test in Brandenburg (1969) focuses on intent, imminence and likelihood. Let the room discover those three ingredients themselves before you name them."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE MARKETPLACE OF IDEAS", "title": "Let ideas compete", "text": "The famous phrase is not Mill’s. It grew from US Justice Oliver Wendell Holmes’s dissent in Abrams v. United States (1919): “the best test of truth is the power of the thought to get itself accepted in the competition of the market.” The idea is Millian: the cure for bad speech is more speech, not enforced silence.", "eg": "A conspiracy theory spreads in your group chat. The marketplace answer: post the evidence, don’t ask the admin to delete it."}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Offence and dignity", "sub": "If speech doesn’t hit anyone, can it still wound?", "min": 13}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "FEINBERG’S BUS RIDE", "title": "Trapped on a bus", "text": "Joel Feinberg, in Offense to Others (1985), asks you to imagine a long bus ride you cannot leave. Other passengers do a series of things that harm no one but are disgusting, shocking or deeply insulting, some aimed at your religion or family. You’re not harmed in Mill’s sense. Yet it seems absurd to say the law could never step in.", "note": "Keep examples tame in the room; Feinberg’s own list is deliberately graphic. The point is “offended but not harmed”."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Hands up: should the law ever be allowed to stop conduct that offends but harms no one?", "follow": "If yes: what makes offence serious enough? If no: does your answer survive the bus?", "solo": "Write one sentence: is being offended ever enough to justify a law? Why or why not?"}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Feinberg’s offence principle: a balancing test", "a": {"label": "HOW SERIOUS IS THE OFFENCE?", "title": "Weigh against", "text": "How intense and long-lasting it is; how many people it reaches; whether they could easily avoid it; whether they consented to it."}, "b": {"label": "HOW REASONABLE IS THE SPEECH?", "title": "Weigh for", "text": "How important it is to the speaker; its social value; whether there were less offensive ways or places to say it; whether it was done out of spite."}, "verdict": "Feinberg makes offence a possible reason to restrict, but a weak one. Views that are merely unpopular almost always win the balance."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Under Feinberg’s balancing test, which case most clearly favours leaving the speech alone?", "options": ["A loudspeaker blasting insults outside a hospital all night", "A deeply offensive argument printed in a book nobody has to open", "Graphic images pasted on the only route children take to school", "A spiteful slur shouted repeatedly at one stranger on a train"], "answer": 1, "why": "The book is easy to avoid and its readers choose it, so the offence weighs little. The other cases are hard to escape, and the slur adds spite."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "WALDRON · DIGNITY", "title": "Not hurt feelings: public standing", "text": "Jeremy Waldron, in The Harm in Hate Speech (2012), argues that hate speech harms, and doesn’t just offend. A society owes every member a public assurance that they are an equal citizen. Posters, leaflets and signs calling a group vermin or traitors chip away at that assurance, like pollution in a shared environment. The target is your social standing, your dignity, not your feelings.", "eg": "Imagine walking to school past posters on every lamppost saying people like your family should be expelled. No one touched you. Something was still taken."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE · ASK THE ROOM · 3 MIN", "q": "Case A: a cartoon that mocks a religion’s founder. Case B: a poster saying followers of that religion are traitors who should be deported. Waldron would treat them very differently. Why?", "follow": "Is there a clear line between attacking a belief and attacking the people who hold it?", "solo": "Write one sentence explaining the difference between offending a belief and attacking people’s standing.", "note": "Waldron himself distinguishes attacks on beliefs (which free debate must allow) from attacks on the standing of believers. Steer the room toward that distinction, then test whether it holds."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Mill’s heir against Waldron", "a": {"label": "MORE SPEECH", "title": "Answer hate in the open", "text": "Bans drive hateful views underground, make martyrs of their authors and hand governments a dangerous tool. Counter-speech persuades; censorship only silences."}, "b": {"label": "DIGNITY FIRST", "title": "Some damage happens before any reply", "text": "The targeted group must live in the environment the speech creates. Asking them to keep winning the argument about whether they are human is itself a burden others don’t bear."}}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is Waldron’s main reason for restricting hate speech?", "options": ["It makes its targets feel upset and offended", "It undermines the public assurance that members of a group are equal citizens", "It is always false, and false speech has no value", "It usually leads directly to violence"], "answer": 1, "why": "Waldron explicitly separates his case from offence. The harm is to dignity as social standing, the public assurance of equality, not to feelings, and it doesn’t depend on proving violence follows."}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Tolerating the intolerant", "sub": "Must a tolerant society tolerate those who would destroy tolerance?", "min": 7}, {"t": "quote", "text": "“Unlimited tolerance must lead to the disappearance of tolerance.”", "src": "Karl Popper, The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), vol. 1, ch. 7, note 4"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "WHAT POPPER ACTUALLY SAID", "title": "A footnote, often misread", "text": "Popper’s paradox is usually quoted as a licence to ban intolerant views. His footnote is more careful. As long as intolerant ideas can be countered by rational argument and kept in check by public opinion, he says, suppressing them would be most unwise. But we should claim the right to suppress them if necessary, since they may refuse argument and answer it with “fists or pistols”. He adds that incitement to intolerance and persecution should be treated as criminal.", "eg": "A group that argues for an authoritarian state: debate it. A group that forbids its members to listen to opponents and plans to break up rival meetings by force: different case.", "note": "This is the most common misreading in the session. Many people have only seen the meme version."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Which best summarises Popper’s own position in the footnote?", "options": ["Intolerant opinions should be banned as soon as they appear", "Tolerance should be unlimited, or it isn’t tolerance", "Tolerate intolerant views while argument works; reserve the right to suppress movements that reject argument for violence", "Only the courts may decide which opinions are intolerant"], "answer": 2, "why": "Popper says suppression would be unwise while reason and public opinion can keep such ideas in check. The right to suppress is a backstop against movements that abandon argument for force."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Imagine the politician you most distrust gets to decide which views count as “intolerant” and may be banned. Would you still support the power to ban?", "options": ["Yes: some views are dangerous whoever is in charge", "No: that’s exactly why the power is too dangerous", "Only if independent courts apply a narrow, clear rule"], "reveal": "Call this the opponent test: design a rule you’d accept even if your rivals enforce it. It’s one of the strongest arguments for narrow, precise speech laws, or none, and many defenders of hate speech laws answer it by insisting on tight definitions and judicial oversight."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 3 MIN", "q": "Back to the guest speaker. Does he count as “intolerant” in Popper’s sense? Is he refusing argument, or offering one?", "follow": "Would your answer change if he urged his audience to stop people of that religion from voting?", "solo": "Write one sentence applying Popper’s test to the guest speaker."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Law, platforms and you", "sub": "Freedom from prison is not the same as freedom from deletion", "min": 8}, {"t": "table", "title": "Who can stop you speaking?", "cols": ["Who", "What they can do", "Limits on them"], "rows": [["The state", "Fines, prison", "Constitutions, courts, due process"], ["A school or employer", "Discipline, dismissal", "Its own rules, contracts, some laws"], ["A platform", "Remove posts, ban accounts, limit reach", "Its terms of service, some regulation"], ["Everyone else", "Criticise, boycott, shun", "Almost none"]], "note": "Ask which row people worry about most. Then reveal that Mill worried about the last row too."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "MILL ON SOCIAL TYRANNY", "title": "The law is not the only censor", "text": "The US First Amendment restrains the government, not private companies. But Mill warned in chapter 1 of On Liberty about “a social tyranny more formidable than many kinds of political oppression”: the pressure of majority opinion, which reaches into private life where the law can’t. For Mill, free speech is a culture, not just a legal rule.", "eg": "No law stops you posting an unpopular opinion. Fear of losing every friend might."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What does Mill’s worry about “social tyranny” imply for platforms and campuses?", "options": ["Free speech questions only arise when the government acts", "Free speech concerns can arise even when no law is involved", "Private bodies must allow everything the law allows", "Social pressure is always worse than legal punishment"], "answer": 1, "why": "Mill thought majority pressure could silence as effectively as law. That doesn’t settle what platforms should do, but it means “it’s a private company” doesn’t end the debate."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "A huge platform must decide about posts that are legal but hateful toward a group. What should it do?", "options": ["Remove them", "Leave them up but stop recommending them", "Leave them up and answer them with labels or counter-speech", "Let each user choose their own filters"], "reveal": "Real systems differ. US law (Section 230, 1996) largely shields platforms from liability for users’ posts and leaves moderation to them. Germany’s NetzDG (2017) requires large platforms to remove clearly unlawful content quickly, usually within 24 hours. Critics on one side fear over-removal; critics on the other fear a flood of abuse."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 3 MIN", "q": "Is a ban by a company that hosts much of a country’s public conversation different from a ban by a small club? Should it be held to the standards we hold governments to?", "solo": "Write one sentence: should the biggest platforms follow free speech rules like a government, or their own rules like a club?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every position tonight has a weak spot. Find it.", "min": 15}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: build the strongest version of the objection, then the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "The market fails (against Mill)", "text": "Markets reward what spreads, not what’s true. A large 2018 study in Science (Vosoughi, Roy and Aral) found false news spread farther and faster on Twitter than true news. Can “more speech” still be trusted to win?"}, {"title": "Speech as a deed (against the marketplace)", "text": "Rae Langton (1993), writing about pornography, argues some speech doesn’t just state views but subordinates and silences, making it harder for its targets to be heard; others extend the idea to hate speech. If so, hate speech shrinks the marketplace it claims to join."}, {"title": "“Harm” is too elastic (against Mill)", "text": "If harm includes distress, the harm principle bans almost everything. If it excludes distress, it ignores real damage like a campaign of humiliation. Mill never draws the line precisely."}, {"title": "Who guards the guardians? (against Waldron)", "text": "Laws written to protect minorities can be turned against them and against dissidents. Nadine Strossen (2018) argues such laws are vague, chill lawful speech and have often been used against the very groups they meant to protect."}, {"title": "Offence can be manufactured (against Feinberg)", "text": "If offence counts, people learn to be offended strategically. The angriest audience gains a “heckler’s veto” over what others may say."}, {"title": "Someone else pays (against Mill)", "text": "Mill says hearing false views keeps our true beliefs lively. But the benefit goes to the majority, while the targeted group pays the cost. Is it fair to make them the sparring partner?"}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "Give each group 4 minutes, then 1 minute each to report. Push groups to state the best reply before giving a verdict. Cards 1 and 4 tend to produce the liveliest clash; try to make sure both are taken."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "Which comes closest to your view of what the law should allow?", "options": ["Almost all speech should be legally free: answer bad speech with more speech", "Free unless it directly causes harm, like threats or incitement to violence", "Speech that attacks a group’s equal standing should also be banned", "It depends so much on the country that there’s no general answer"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Did anything move you, or did you hold your ground with better reasons? Both count as progress: Mill would say a view that has survived attack is now a living truth, not a dead dogma.", "note": "Ask two people who changed their answer to explain why, and one who didn’t."}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Read chapter 2 of On Liberty (free online) and find the argument you think is weakest", "Find a view you strongly reject and write its best defence in three sentences", "Next time you want something deleted or reported, first write the reply you’d post instead", "Look up the speech laws of one country you know little about and compare them with your own"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Mill defends almost all speech, but not speech that, in its setting, directly triggers harm.", "Feinberg counts serious, unavoidable offence; Waldron counts attacks on equal standing; Popper reserves suppression for movements that abandon argument for force.", "Law, platforms and social pressure are different censors, and each needs its own answer."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "On Liberty (1859), chs. 1–2", "by": "John Stuart Mill", "note": "The classic case for free speech; short, free online, still argued over."}, {"title": "Offense to Others (1985)", "by": "Joel Feinberg", "note": "The bus ride and the careful balancing test for offence."}, {"title": "The Harm in Hate Speech (2012)", "by": "Jeremy Waldron", "note": "The strongest dignity-based case for hate speech laws."}, {"title": "HATE: Why We Should Resist It with Free Speech, Not Censorship (2018)", "by": "Nadine Strossen", "note": "A former ACLU president’s reply, rich in real cases."}, {"title": "The Open Society and Its Enemies (1945), vol. 1, ch. 7, note 4", "by": "Karl Popper", "note": "Read the paradox of tolerance in full, not the meme."}, {"title": "Free Speech: Ten Principles for a Connected World (2016)", "by": "Timothy Garton Ash", "note": "How old arguments apply to the internet age."}], "also": ["The Liberty Harbour", "Rebuilding the Ship at Sea"]}]}, {"id": "art", "title": "What Makes Something Art?", "sub": "Duchamp’s urinal, Hume’s true judges, Kant’s beauty, a famous forger and the AI image", "question": "What makes something art, and can taste be wrong?", "minutes": 55, "soloMinutes": 16, "aim": "Participants can explain the institutional theory of art, Hume’s standard of taste and Kant’s account of judgements of beauty, apply them to forgery, AI images and pop music, and defend a view on whether taste can be wrong.", "cast": ["hume", "kant", "dickie", "danto", "goodman"], "related": {"agora": null, "islands": ["beauty", "hume", "kant"], "compass": ["a4", "a1", "a7", "a9"]}, "slides": [{"t": "story", "title": "The Urinal", "text": "New York, April 1917. The Society of Independent Artists promises a show with no jury: pay the fee and your work goes up. Among the entries arrives a factory-made porcelain urinal, laid on its back, signed “R. Mutt 1917” and titled Fountain. The organisers refuse to display it. The original soon vanishes; a photograph by Alfred Stieglitz survives. The entry is usually credited to Marcel Duchamp, who sat on the society’s board. In 2004 a poll of 500 art-world experts named it the most influential modern artwork. So: is a shop-bought urinal art? And if critics revere it and your grandmother calls it a joke, is one of them wrong?", "note": "Mention that a few scholars argue the idea came from the artist Elsa von Freytag-Loringhoven; most still credit Duchamp. Don’t let this take more than 30 seconds."}, {"t": "route", "title": "Tonight’s route", "items": [{"title": "The urinal in the gallery", "min": 12}, {"title": "Hume: can taste be wrong?", "min": 11}, {"title": "Kant: beauty that speaks for everyone", "min": 8}, {"title": "Fakes and machines", "min": 11}, {"title": "Attack the arguments, then vote again", "min": 13}]}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "id": "v1", "q": "Can someone’s taste in art be wrong?", "options": ["No: beauty is in the eye of the beholder", "Yes: some judgements are simply more accurate than others", "Partly: there are better and worse critics, but no single right answer", "“Good taste” is mostly social class in disguise"], "reveal": "Each answer has serious defenders: the subjectivist, Hume, many modern critics, and the sociologist Pierre Bourdieu. Keep your answer: we vote again at the end."}, {"t": "section", "num": 1, "title": "The urinal in the gallery", "sub": "If a urinal can be art, what can’t?", "min": 12}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three old answers to “What is art?”", "items": [{"tag": "IMITATION", "term": "mimēsis (μίμησις)", "title": "Art copies the world", "text": "From Plato and Aristotle: art represents things, as a painting of a horse imitates a horse."}, {"tag": "FORM", "title": "Art has significant form", "text": "Clive Bell, in Art (1914), held that art is whatever has lines, colours and shapes that stir a special aesthetic emotion."}, {"tag": "EXPRESSION", "title": "Art passes on feeling", "text": "Leo Tolstoy, in What Is Art? (1897), argued that art infects its audience with the feelings the artist lived through."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "Why is Fountain such a problem for all three old theories?", "options": ["It is too ugly to be art", "It wasn’t made by the artist, imitates nothing, and wasn’t chosen for beauty or feeling", "It was refused by the exhibition, so it never officially counted", "It was made in a factory, and art must be handmade"], "answer": 1, "why": "Fountain represents nothing, has no special form compared with any urinal, and expresses no lived feeling in the usual sense. Its art-status seems to come from something else entirely."}, {"t": "versus", "title": "Danto: two identical boxes", "a": {"label": "IN THE SUPERMARKET", "title": "A Brillo box", "text": "A cardboard carton printed to sell soap pads. Nobody thinks it is art."}, "b": {"label": "IN THE GALLERY, 1964", "title": "Warhol’s Brillo Box", "text": "A wooden box Andy Warhol made to look just like it. Critics treat it as an important artwork."}, "verdict": "Arthur Danto, in “The Artworld” (1964), asked what makes the difference when the eye can’t tell them apart. His answer: a context of art theory and art history, which he called the artworld. Seeing something as art takes knowledge the eye alone doesn’t give."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "DICKIE · THE INSTITUTIONAL THEORY", "title": "Art by status", "text": "George Dickie, in Art and the Aesthetic (1974), turned Danto’s hint into a definition. A work of art is an artefact that has had the status of a candidate for appreciation conferred on it by someone acting on behalf of the artworld: artists, curators, critics, galleries. On this view, art is a social status, like being married or being a citizen, not a visible property.", "note": "Common confusion: Dickie doesn’t say it must be good art, or even appreciated. Only that it has been put forward for appreciation within the artworld."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Dickie’s institutional theory, what makes Fountain a work of art?", "options": ["Its elegant, curving form", "The feelings it expressed for Duchamp", "A person acting on behalf of the artworld put it forward for appreciation", "Most people who see it agree it is beautiful"], "answer": 2, "why": "The theory is about status, not looks or feelings. Duchamp, acting within the artworld, offered it as a candidate for appreciation."}, {"t": "variations", "title": "Now change one detail at a time", "case": "Fountain, a urinal signed by Duchamp, is displayed in a major museum as a work of art.", "items": [{"title": "In the shop", "text": "The very same urinal stays in a plumber’s showroom, unsigned."}, {"title": "In the school show", "text": "A twelve-year-old signs a urinal and puts it in the school art show, inspired by Duchamp."}, {"title": "Signed, never shown", "text": "A famous artist signs a urinal in a hardware store, then walks out. No one ever displays it."}, {"title": "An elephant’s brush", "text": "A museum hangs a painting made by an elephant holding a brush."}], "ask": "After each step: art or not? Which theory explains your answer best?", "note": "The school show is the hardest for Dickie: is a school art show part of the artworld? Let the room fight about it."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "hands", "label": "HANDS UP · 1 MIN", "q": "Hands up if you think “art” can’t be defined at all.", "follow": "Morris Weitz (1956) argued just that: art is an “open concept”, whose members share family resemblances rather than one defining feature. Is that a deep insight, or giving up?", "solo": "Write one sentence: can “art” be defined, and if not, how do we still use the word?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 2, "title": "Hume: can taste be wrong?", "sub": "Taste is a feeling. Hume still thinks some people are better at it.", "min": 11}, {"t": "quote", "text": "“Beauty is no quality in things themselves: It exists merely in the mind which contemplates them; and each mind perceives a different beauty.”", "src": "David Hume, “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757)", "note": "Warning: Hume is stating the sceptical view he’s about to qualify, not his final answer. Ask the room to guess where he goes next."}, {"t": "source", "text": "“Whoever would assert an equality of genius and elegance between Ogilby and Milton, or Bunyan and Addison, would be thought to defend no less an extravagance, than if he had maintained a mole-hill to be as high as Teneriffe, or a pond as extensive as the ocean.”", "src": "David Hume, “Of the Standard of Taste” (1757)", "trans": "original English, 1757", "note": "Ogilby was a forgettable translator; Milton wrote Paradise Lost; Teneriffe is a mountain on Tenerife. Hume’s point: in some comparisons, “it’s all subjective” is plainly absurd. (Many today would defend Bunyan, which is itself a nice test of Hume’s claim.)"}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "SANCHO’S KINSMEN", "title": "The key in the barrel", "text": "Hume borrows a story from Don Quixote. Two men taste wine from a barrel. One says it’s good, but with a slight taste of leather. The other agrees, but tastes iron. Everyone laughs at them. When the barrel is emptied, at the bottom lies an old key on a leather strap. Delicate taste detects real qualities that others miss, and there can be evidence of who was right.", "eg": "A trained musician hears that a violin is slightly out of tune. You don’t. They aren’t imagining it."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Hume’s true judge", "items": [{"tag": "DELICACY", "title": "Fine discrimination", "text": "Can detect small qualities, like the leather and the iron."}, {"tag": "PRACTICE", "title": "Long experience", "text": "Has looked and listened a great deal in that art."}, {"tag": "COMPARISON", "title": "Wide range", "text": "Has compared many works across different kinds and levels of quality."}, {"tag": "NO PREJUDICE + GOOD SENSE", "title": "Fair and clear-headed", "text": "Sets aside personal and national bias, and understands what a work is trying to do."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What does the story of the key in the barrel show, for Hume?", "options": ["That taste in wine is more reliable than taste in art", "That delicate taste can detect real features of things, so disputes about taste can have better and worse answers", "That experts always agree with each other", "That beauty is a property we can measure with instruments"], "answer": 1, "why": "Taste rests on feeling, but feelings respond to real qualities. Some people perceive those qualities more finely, and finding the key confirms they were right."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE TEST OF TIME", "title": "Homer is still read", "text": "Hume adds a second test: works that please across ages and cultures. The same Homer who pleased at Athens and Rome two thousand years ago, he notes, is still admired at Paris and London. Fashions fade; works that keep pleasing different people for different reasons must suit something deep in human nature.", "eg": "Plenty of best-selling novels from 1900 are forgotten. A few are still read. Hume thinks that difference isn’t luck."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "VOTE · 2 MIN", "q": "Can a great pop song be as great a work of art as a great symphony?", "options": ["Yes, fully", "Yes, but it’s rare", "No: they aren’t the same kind of achievement", "The question itself is snobbish"], "reveal": "The lines are moving. Bob Dylan won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 2016, and Kendrick Lamar’s DAMN. won the 2018 Pulitzer Prize for Music, the first winner outside classical music and jazz. Theodor Adorno (1941) argued popular music is standardised and passive; Noël Carroll’s A Philosophy of Mass Art (1998) defends mass art as real art built for wide access.", "note": "Ask a pop defender to apply Hume’s five qualities. Do they favour the symphony or not?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 3, "title": "Kant: beauty that speaks for everyone", "sub": "Taste is a feeling, yet it claims everyone’s agreement", "min": 8}, {"t": "versus", "title": "The agreeable and the beautiful", "a": {"label": "THE AGREEABLE", "title": "“I like it”", "text": "Kant’s example is Canary wine (§7). If I say it’s agreeable, I speak only for myself. If you dislike it, fine: neither of us is wrong."}, "b": {"label": "THE BEAUTIFUL", "title": "“It is beautiful”", "text": "When I call something beautiful, I speak as if for everyone. I don’t just report my pleasure; I expect you to agree, and think you ought to."}, "verdict": "Kant: judgements of beauty are subjective in origin but universal in their claim."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "KANT · CRITIQUE OF JUDGMENT (1790)", "title": "Disinterested, universal, without a rule", "text": "For Kant, a judgement of beauty rests on a feeling of pleasure, but a special one. It is disinterested: you don’t want to own, eat or use the thing (§2). It speaks “with a universal voice” (§8). Yet no rule or concept can prove it: you can’t argue someone into finding a sunset beautiful. The pleasure comes from the free play of imagination and understanding.", "term": "Zweckmäßigkeit ohne Zweck: purposiveness without purpose", "eg": "A flower seems perfectly designed, yet you enjoy it without asking what it is for. That is what Kant calls purposiveness without purpose."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "What is distinctive about a judgement of beauty, according to Kant?", "options": ["It can be proved by applying the correct rules of art", "It rests on a feeling, yet claims that everyone ought to agree", "It reports only personal preference, like a taste for a wine", "It depends on how useful the object is"], "answer": 1, "why": "Kant’s key move is subjective universality: the judgement rests on pleasure, not proof, yet it demands everyone’s agreement."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "pair", "label": "SPOT THE DIFFERENCE · THINK, PAIR, SHARE · 3 MIN", "q": "“I like this song” versus “This song is beautiful.” Do you mean something different by them? When a friend disagrees with the second, what are you actually disagreeing about?", "solo": "Write one sentence describing the difference between “I like it” and “It’s beautiful”, in your own use."}, {"t": "section", "num": 4, "title": "Fakes and machines", "sub": "If no one can tell the difference, is there one?", "min": 11}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE FORGER", "title": "Han van Meegeren", "text": "In 1937 the leading Vermeer expert Abraham Bredius hailed The Supper at Emmaus as a masterpiece by Vermeer, and a Rotterdam museum bought it. It was painted by Han van Meegeren. After the war he was arrested for selling another “Vermeer” to the Nazi leader Hermann Göring, a treason charge. To prove it was his own fake, he painted a new one in front of witnesses. In 1947 he was convicted of forgery and fraud instead, and died that same year.", "note": "The irony delights rooms: he became a folk hero for having fooled Göring."}, {"t": "predict", "q": "Once The Supper at Emmaus was exposed as a fake, did it become a worse painting?", "options": ["Yes: it’s worth much less as art", "No: the paint on the canvas didn’t change", "It’s now a different kind of thing, neither better nor worse"], "reveal": "Its market value collapsed, and critics soon saw obvious flaws they had missed. Alfred Lessing (1965) argued a perfect forgery is no worse aesthetically; what it lacks is originality. Nelson Goodman (1968) replied that knowing it’s a fake changes how we look, and trains us to see differences. Denis Dutton (1979) argued art is a performance, an achievement, and the forger’s achievement is a different and lesser one."}, {"t": "cards", "title": "Three answers to the forgery puzzle", "items": [{"tag": "LESSING", "title": "No aesthetic difference", "text": "If two canvases look identical, they are aesthetically equal. What a forgery lacks is originality, not looks."}, {"tag": "GOODMAN", "title": "Knowing changes seeing", "text": "In Languages of Art (1968), Goodman argues that knowing one is a fake gives you reason to look for differences, which you may learn to see."}, {"tag": "DUTTON", "title": "Art is an achievement", "text": "We admire what an artist did: solved problems, invented a style. Copying Vermeer’s style is a lesser achievement than inventing it."}]}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "On Dutton’s view, why is a perfect forgery less valuable than the original?", "options": ["Because it always contains visible flaws", "Because art is an achievement, and the forger achieved something different and lesser", "Because the artworld has refused to grant it status", "Because it is illegal to sell"], "answer": 1, "why": "Dutton treats art as a kind of performance. The original solved artistic problems for the first time; the forger only imitated the solution."}, {"t": "idea", "kicker": "THE MACHINE", "title": "The AI image that won a prize", "text": "In 2022 Jason Allen won the digital art category at the Colorado State Fair with Théâtre D’opéra Spatial, made with the AI image generator Midjourney, followed by his own editing. Some artists were furious; he said he had worked hard on the prompts and edits. In 2023 the US Copyright Office refused to register the image unless the AI-generated parts were excluded, holding that copyright needs human authorship.", "note": "Stay on the philosophy, not later legal developments. The question is whether it’s art, not whether it’s legal."}, {"t": "quiz", "q": "A museum curator exhibits an AI-generated image as an artwork. Which theory most directly says that is enough to make it art?", "options": ["Tolstoy’s expression theory", "Dickie’s institutional theory", "Dutton’s achievement view", "Hume’s standard of taste"], "answer": 1, "why": "For Dickie, art status is conferred by someone acting for the artworld. Tolstoy needs a feeling the artist lived through, and Dutton needs an achievement, so both have harder questions to answer."}, {"t": "room", "mode": "ask", "label": "ASK THE ROOM · 4 MIN", "q": "Run the AI image through tonight’s theories: Dickie, Hume, Kant, Tolstoy, Dutton. Which ones say it’s art? Which say it could be good art?", "follow": "Does the prompt-writer count as the artist? Does it matter who, or what, made it, if it moves you?", "solo": "Write one sentence: is an AI image from a one-line prompt art, and which theory supports your answer?"}, {"t": "section", "num": 5, "title": "Attack the arguments", "sub": "Every theory tonight has a crack. Find it.", "min": 13}, {"t": "critique", "title": "Your turn: attack the argument", "how": ["Form groups of 4–6", "Take one card", "4 minutes: strongest version of the objection, and the best reply", "Deliver a verdict"], "items": [{"title": "Circular (against Dickie)", "text": "Art is what the artworld says; the artworld is the people who deal in art. Dickie himself admitted the circle and argued it isn’t vicious. Is it?"}, {"title": "Who picks the judges? (against Hume)", "text": "True judges are identified by their good taste, which is what we wanted to define. And Hume’s judges in practice were educated European men. Is the standard just their taste?"}, {"title": "Taste is class (against Hume and Kant)", "text": "Pierre Bourdieu’s Distinction (1979) found that preferences in art track education and class. “Good taste” may be a way of marking social rank while pretending it’s nature."}, {"title": "No universal voice (against Kant)", "text": "Ideals of beauty vary across cultures and centuries. Is Kant’s claim to speak for everyone just a European assumption dressed up as a principle?"}, {"title": "The forgery dilemma (against everyone)", "text": "If only what you see matters, the perfect fake is as good as the original. If history matters too, then art isn’t really about looking. Each horn has a cost."}, {"title": "Anything goes (against the Duchamp tradition)", "text": "If a urinal can be art just by being presented as art, the word marks nothing. Has “art” become an empty honour?"}, {"title": "No one home (against AI art sceptics)", "text": "If meaning lies in the viewer’s experience, the maker’s mind may not matter. Then rejecting AI images as art looks like prejudice about where art comes from."}], "verdicts": ["Fatal", "Fixable", "Misguided"], "note": "4 minutes per group, then 1 minute each to report. If time is short, prioritise the Hume, Bourdieu and forgery cards."}, {"t": "vote", "label": "YOUR VERDICT NOW · 1 MIN", "compare": "v1", "q": "Can someone’s taste in art be wrong?", "options": ["No: beauty is in the eye of the beholder", "Yes: some judgements are simply more accurate than others", "Partly: there are better and worse critics, but no single right answer", "“Good taste” is mostly social class in disguise"], "reveal": "Compare with your first answer. Notice that you’ve been arguing all evening as if some answers were better than others. Hume would say that itself is evidence for a standard of taste. Bourdieu would ask who taught you how to argue about art.", "note": "Ask whether anyone moved from the first option. If so, what moved them: the key in the barrel, the test of time, or Kant’s universal voice?"}, {"t": "action", "title": "One step", "q": "Pick one thing to try this week:", "options": ["Spend ten full minutes with one artwork you think you dislike, then write what you noticed", "Read Hume’s “Of the Standard of Taste” (short and free online) and find his weakest step", "Pick a pop song you love and argue its case using Hume’s five qualities of a true judge", "Ask three people of different ages whether Fountain is art, and note their reasons"]}, {"t": "summary", "items": ["Fountain broke the old theories; Danto and Dickie answer that art is a status within the artworld, not a look.", "Hume and Kant both root taste in feeling, yet both think judgements of beauty can be better or worse, or claim everyone’s agreement.", "Forgeries and AI images test whether art lies in what we see, in what someone achieved, or in who stands behind it."]}, {"t": "explore", "read": [{"title": "“Of the Standard of Taste” (1757)", "by": "David Hume", "note": "Short, witty, and the best place to start; free online."}, {"title": "Critique of Judgment (1790), §§1–22", "by": "Immanuel Kant", "note": "The analysis of the beautiful: hard but rewarding, best in a good introduction."}, {"title": "“The Artworld” (1964)", "by": "Arthur Danto", "note": "The essay that started the debate over Warhol’s boxes."}, {"title": "Art and the Aesthetic (1974)", "by": "George Dickie", "note": "The institutional theory set out in full."}, {"title": "Languages of Art (1968), ch. 3", "by": "Nelson Goodman", "note": "The classic chapter on forgery."}, {"title": "The Art Question (2003)", "by": "Nigel Warburton", "note": "A clear, short beginner’s guide to the theories of art."}], "also": ["The Harbour of Habit", "The Lawgiver of Königsberg"]}]}];
